window.MKT_DATA = {"generated":"2026-10-15","chapters":[{"id":1,"title":"Consumer Behavior and Technology","exam":"midterm","slides":[{"img":"slides/ch1/01.jpg","title":"Consumer Behavior","text":"Consumer Behavior Twelfth Edition, Global Edition Chapter 1 Consumer Behavior and Technology Copyright © 2019 Pearson Education, Ltd. All Rights Reserved."},{"img":"slides/ch1/02.jpg","title":"Learning Objectives","text":"Learning Objectives 1.1 To understand the evolution of the marketing concept, what consumer behavior is, and the components of strategic marketing. 1.2 To understand how technology has benefited both marketers and consumers. 1.3 To understand providing value and satisfaction and how technology has enhanced customer loyalty and retention. 1.4 To understand marketers’ social and ethical responsibilities. 1.5 To understand consumer decision-making as the foundation of this book. 1.6 To explain how the knowledge of consumer behavior advances seeking employment after graduation."},{"img":"slides/ch1/03.jpg","title":"Marketing","text":"Marketing Defined Marketing is the activity, set of institutions, and processes for creating, communicating, and delivering offerings that have value for customers, clients, partners, and society."},{"img":"slides/ch1/04.jpg","title":"Consumer Behavior","text":"Consumer Behavior Defined Consumer behavior is the study of consumers’ choices during searching, evaluating, purchasing, and using products and services that they believe would satisfy their needs."},{"img":"slides/ch1/05.jpg","title":"What Can a Car Help Express About its Owner?","text":"What Can a Car Help Express About its Owner?"},{"img":"slides/ch1/06.jpg","title":"Vices and Virtues","text":"Vices and Virtues Presence of healthy food option “licensed” consumers to eat unhealthy food Estimated burger calories decreased over 100 calories when accompanied by three celery sticks. How and why do consumers make decisions based on other factors than facts and rationality?"},{"img":"slides/ch1/07.jpg","title":"Learning Objective 1.1","text":"Learning Objective 1.1 1.1 To understand the evolution of the marketing concept, what consumer behavior is, and the components of strategic marketing."},{"img":"slides/ch1/08.jpg","title":"Marketing Concept","text":"Marketing Concept Defined The premise that marketing consists of satisfying consumers’ needs, creating value, and retaining customers, and that companies must produce only those goods that they have already determined would satisfy consumer needs and meet organizational goals."},{"img":"slides/ch1/09.jpg","title":"Marketing Concept Application","text":"Marketing Concept Application How does the Vans ad relate to the marketing concept?"},{"img":"slides/ch1/10.jpg","title":"Development of the Marketing Concept","text":"Development of the Marketing Concept Production Concept Product Concept Selling Concept Marketing Concept"},{"img":"slides/ch1/11.jpg","title":"Marketing Myopia","text":"Marketing Myopia Short-sighted approach where companies “look in the mirror instead of out the window” In other words, managers focus on the product, not the needs it is designed to fulfill"},{"img":"slides/ch1/12.jpg","title":"Marketing Concept Requirements","text":"Marketing Concept Requirements Market Segmentation Targeting Positioning The Marketing Mix (4 Ps) Product or service Price Place Promotion"},{"img":"slides/ch1/13.jpg","title":"Learning Objective 1.2","text":"Learning Objective 1.2 1.2 To understand how technology has benefited both marketers and consumers."},{"img":"slides/ch1/14.jpg","title":"Value Exchange","text":"Value Exchange Technologies create a value exchange Technology makes it easier to shop and access information, entertainment, and customized products Consumers pay for content with information about themselves"},{"img":"slides/ch1/15.jpg","title":"Consumers Have Embraced Technology","text":"Consumers Have Embraced Technology"},{"img":"slides/ch1/16.jpg","title":"Most Prominent Online Activities","text":"Most Prominent Online Activities"},{"img":"slides/ch1/17.jpg","title":"More Precise Targeting","text":"More Precise Targeting Cookies Global Positioning Systems (G P S) Selfies Interactive communications"},{"img":"slides/ch1/18.jpg","title":"Cross-Screen Marketing","text":"Cross-Screen Marketing Defined A promotional strategy that consists of tracking and targeting users across their computers, mobile phones, and tablets, and sending them personalized ads based on their interests, as observed by marketers."},{"img":"slides/ch1/19.jpg","title":"Discussion Question","text":"Discussion Question How does technology affect the Marketing Mix? Provide examples Interactive and novel communication channels Customizing products and promotional messages Better prices and distribution"},{"img":"slides/ch1/20.jpg","title":"Learning Objective 1.3","text":"Learning Objective 1.3 1.3 To understand providing value and satisfaction and how technology has enhanced customer loyalty and retention."},{"img":"slides/ch1/21.jpg","title":"Successful Relationships (1 of 3)","text":"Successful Relationships (1 of 3) Value, Satisfaction, and Retention Customer Value Customer Satisfaction Customer Retention Defined as the ratio between the customer’s perceived benefits and the resources used to obtain those benefits Perceived value is relative and subjective Developing a value proposition is critical"},{"img":"slides/ch1/22.jpg","title":"Discussion Questions","text":"Discussion Questions How does McDonald’s create value for the consumer? How do they communicate this value?"},{"img":"slides/ch1/23.jpg","title":"Successful Relationships (2 of 3)","text":"Successful Relationships (2 of 3) Value, Satisfaction, and Retention Customer Value Customer Satisfaction Customer Retention The individual's perception of the performance of the product or service in relation to his or her expectations. Customer groups based on loyalty include loyalists, apostles, defectors, terrorists, hostages, and mercenaries"},{"img":"slides/ch1/24.jpg","title":"Successful Relationships (3 of 3)","text":"Successful Relationships (3 of 3) Value, Satisfaction, and Retention Customer Value Customer Satisfaction Customer Retention The objective of providing value is to retain highly satisfied customers. Loyal customers are key They buy more products They are less price sensitive Servicing them is cheaper They spread positive word of mouth"},{"img":"slides/ch1/25.jpg","title":"Customer Relationships","text":"Customer Relationships For Discussion: Provide two examples where brands used technology to engage consumers/enhance customer relationships. Provide two examples where technology was used to add value to the consumer."},{"img":"slides/ch1/26.jpg","title":"Forms of Engagement","text":"Forms of Engagement Emotional Bonds Personal commitment and attachment Social media attempts to get consumers to engage emotionally with products and brands Transactional Bonds Mechanics and structures that facilitate exchanges between consumers and sellers Factors like assortment and transaction ease could shape the relationship"},{"img":"slides/ch1/27.jpg","title":"Determinants of Site Satisfaction","text":"Determinants of Site Satisfaction Adaptation Interactivity Nurturing Commitment Network Assortment Transaction ease Engagement Loyalty Inertia Trust"},{"img":"slides/ch1/28.jpg","title":"Profitability-Focused Segmentation","text":"Profitability-Focused Segmentation Miles Travelled Annually and Matching Revenues Airplane’s Class and Matching Revenues per Mile At least 250,000 Miles At least 150,000 miles At least 100,000 miles First Class ($ 4 per mile) $ 1,000,000 DIAMOND $ 600,000 EMERALD $ 400,000 EMERALD Business Class ($ 2 per mile) $ 500,000 EMERALD $ 300,000 SAPPHIRE $ 200,000 SAPPHIRE Premium Economy ($ 0.6 per mile) $ 150,000 ELITE $ 90,000 SELECT $ 60,000 SELECT Economy ($ 0.3 per mile) $ 75,000 SELECT $ 45,000 $ 30,000"},{"img":"slides/ch1/29.jpg","title":"Measures of Customer Retention","text":"Measures of Customer Retention Customer valuation Retention rates Analyzing defections"},{"img":"slides/ch1/30.jpg","title":"Review Question","text":"Review Question What is the difference between emotional and transactional bonds? Identify and describe four of the eleven determinants of customer satisfaction with online merchants. Characterize each selected determinant as primarily driven by emotion or stemming from the mechanics of the transaction."},{"img":"slides/ch1/31.jpg","title":"Learning Objective 1.4","text":"Learning Objective 1.4 1.4 To understand marketers social and ethical responsibilities."},{"img":"slides/ch1/32.jpg","title":"Social Responsibility","text":"Social Responsibility Companies incorporate social goals into their mission statements Marketing ethics and social responsibility can shape organizational effectiveness Socially responsible activities improve image among stakeholders"},{"img":"slides/ch1/33.jpg","title":"Learning Objective 1.5","text":"Learning Objective 1.5 1.5 To understand consumer decision-making as the foundation for this book."},{"img":"slides/ch1/34.jpg","title":"Consumer Decision Making (1 of 2)","text":"Consumer Decision Making (1 of 2) Inputs Firm marketing efforts Sociocultural influences Process Psychological factors Need Recognition, Decision Type, Prepurchase Search, Evaluation of Alternatives Learning Outputs Purchase Post-purchase evaluation"},{"img":"slides/ch1/35.jpg","title":"Consumer Decision Making (2 of 2)","text":"Consumer Decision Making (2 of 2)"},{"img":"slides/ch1/36.jpg","title":"Learning Objective 1.6","text":"Learning Objective 1.6 1.6 To explain how knowledge of consumer behavior advances seeking employment after graduation."},{"img":"slides/ch1/37.jpg","title":"Employment Opportunities","text":"Employment Opportunities Brand management Advertising Consumer research"}],"questions":[{"id":"1-1","n":1,"type":"mcq","q":"Which of the following is the study of the processes involved when individuals or groups select, purchase, use, or dispose of products, services, ideas, or experiences to satisfy needs and desires?","answer":2,"exp":"Chapter 1 defines consumer behavior as the study of consumers' choices during searching, evaluating, purchasing and using products that they believe will satisfy their needs. That is exactly what the question describes. Lifestyle marketing, role theory and marketing research are different ideas.","options":["lifestyle marketing","role theory","consumer behavior","marketing research"]},{"id":"1-2","n":2,"type":"mcq","q":"Which of the following is considered an example of consumer behavior?","answer":4,"exp":"Consumer behavior covers every part of choosing, buying, using and disposing of a product. Preferring a brand, choosing when to buy gas, choosing where to buy produce and recycling are all consumer behaviors, so the answer is 'All of the above'.","options":["Janice prefers to buy name-brand pain relievers like Tylenol and Advil, rather than the store brand.","Javier generally gets gas on Monday mornings on his way to work.","Jessica prefers to buy her produce from the farmer's market instead of the grocery store.","Jeremy generally recycles his old newspapers and cardboard boxes.","All of the above are examples of consumer behavior."],"book":"The textbook adds that consumer behavior also includes how consumers dispose of products after use, which is why recycling counts.","depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 1, p. 3","page":3},{"id":"1-3","n":3,"type":"mcq","q":"Marketing and consumer behavior stem from the ________, which maintains that the essence of marketing is satisfying consumers' needs, creating value and retaining customers.","answer":4,"exp":"The marketing concept says marketing is about satisfying consumers' needs, creating value and retaining customers. Both marketing and consumer behavior as fields grew out of this idea.","options":["production concept","product concept","selling concept","societal concept","marketing concept"]},{"id":"1-4","n":4,"type":"mcq","q":"A(n) ________ is a person who identifies a need or desire, makes a purchase, and then disposes of a product.","answer":1,"exp":"A consumer is the person who identifies a need, buys the product and then uses and disposes of it. Chapter 1 frames consumer behavior around this person's choices. Marketers and influencers try to affect the consumer. They are not the consumer.","options":["marketer","consumer","influencer","content generator"]},{"id":"1-5","n":5,"type":"mcq","q":"Tripp runs a product development division at a software company. His division was focused on producing a word processing software that performs better and has more features than any competitive product, so they added every feature that was technically feasible. However, they found that consumers were satisfied with fewer features and unwilling to pay more for the advanced software. Tripp and his team suffered from ________.","answer":0,"exp":"Marketing myopia means looking 'in the mirror instead of out the window': focusing on the product instead of the need it serves. Tripp's team added every feature they could without checking what consumers actually wanted.","options":["marketing myopia","over-segmentation","target marketing","technology devotion","marketing vision"]},{"id":"1-6","n":6,"type":"mcq","q":"During historical periods when demand exceeded supply, businesses adopted the ________ concept.","answer":3,"exp":"The production concept was the first stage. When demand is greater than supply, companies focus on producing more and making products widely available. Persuading or satisfying buyers is not the priority yet.","options":["marketing","technology","societal","production","product"],"book":"Textbook: the production concept assumes consumers are mostly interested in product availability at low prices.","depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 1, p. 4","page":4},{"id":"1-7","n":7,"type":"mcq","q":"The ________ concept in business assumes that consumers are mostly interested in product availability at low prices.","answer":3,"exp":"Under the production concept, firms assume consumers mostly want products that are available and cheap. Their goal is to expand production and cut costs.","options":["marketing","societal","product","production","technology"],"depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 1, p. 4","page":4},{"id":"1-8","n":8,"type":"mcq","q":"The ________ is characterized by the gearing up of manufacturing skills in order to expand production.","answer":3,"exp":"The production concept is about increasing manufacturing ability to expand production: making more, faster and cheaper.","options":["marketing concept","product concept","selling concept","production concept","societal concept"],"depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 1, p. 4","page":4},{"id":"1-9","n":9,"type":"mcq","q":"The production concept makes sense for a business when ________.","answer":0,"exp":"The production concept makes sense when consumers care more about getting the product at all than about variety or special features. For example, a market where demand is greater than supply.","options":["consumers are more interested in product availability than in product variation","consumers are interested in obtaining the product that offers them the highest quality, best performance, and most features","consumers have changing needs and insist that those needs be satisfied","consumers are unlikely to buy the product unless they are persuaded to do so","consumers are not sensitive to price"],"depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 1, p. 4","page":4},{"id":"1-10","n":10,"type":"mcq","q":"The focus of the ________ was to sell more of what the manufacturing department was able to produce.","answer":1,"exp":"The selling concept focuses on pushing and selling what the factory has already made. It does not ask whether consumers need it.","options":["marketing concept","selling concept","product concept","production concept","market orientation"],"depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 1, p. 5","page":5},{"id":"1-11","n":11,"type":"mcq","q":"Kevin's company owns a factory that can produce 1,000 widgets per week. His primary focus is on getting consumers to purchase as many widgets as possible to ensure that he is maximizing his sales relative to his production capacity. Kevin's business is exhibiting the ________.","answer":3,"exp":"Kevin wants to sell as many widgets as his factory can make. Selling what has already been produced is the selling concept. The marketing concept would start from consumer needs instead.","options":["marketing concept","product concept","not-for-profit concept","selling concept","market orientation"],"depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 1, p. 5","page":5},{"id":"1-12","n":12,"type":"mcq","q":"The key assumption underlying the marketing concept is that ________.","answer":1,"exp":"Chapter 1: the marketing concept means the company first figures out what target consumers need and produces only goods that satisfy those needs, better than competitors do.","options":["if manufacturers make a product, consumers will buy it","a company must determine the needs and wants of specific target markets and satisfy consumers' needs better than the competition","the primary focus of a business should be producing as much as it can, in the cheapest way possible","consumers are most interested in obtaining generic products at low prices","consumers are unlikely to buy a product unless they are aggressively persuaded to do so"]},{"id":"1-13","n":13,"type":"mcq","q":"The selling concept focuses on the needs of the ________ and on existing products, while the marketing concept focuses on the needs of the ________.","answer":2,"exp":"The selling concept looks at the seller's needs and the seller's existing products. The marketing concept looks at the buyer's needs.","options":["manufacturer; seller","buyer; manufacturer","seller; buyer","seller; manufacturer","buyer; seller"],"depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 1, p. 5","page":5},{"id":"1-16","n":16,"type":"mcq","q":"________ refers to the development of a distinct image for the product in the mind of the consumer.","answer":1,"exp":"Positioning means creating a distinct image for the product in the consumer's mind. Segmentation divides the market, and targeting picks which segment to serve.","options":["Targeting","Positioning","Placement","Promotion","Segmenting"],"depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 1, p. 5","page":5},{"id":"1-17","n":17,"type":"mcq","q":"Tanya and her marketing team at a fast food restaurant are breaking their company's target audience into groups that have shared needs that are different from the needs shared by other groups. They are engaged in ________.","answer":4,"exp":"Dividing a market into groups with shared needs that differ from other groups' needs is market segmentation, the first requirement of the marketing concept in Chapter 1.","options":["market targeting","positioning","placement","promotion","market segmentation"],"depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 1, p. 5","page":5},{"id":"1-18","n":18,"type":"mcq","q":"Successful positioning focuses on communicating the ________.","answer":1,"exp":"Positioning works by communicating the product's distinct benefits, meaning why it is different and better for the target market.","options":["segments that the company views as prospective customers","distinct benefits that the product provides","retail shelf space the product should occupy","competing offerings that are available","product instructions for use"],"depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 1, p. 5","page":5},{"id":"1-19","n":19,"type":"mcq","q":"The four Ps of the marketing mix are ________.","answer":2,"exp":"Chapter 1 lists the 4 Ps of the marketing mix: Product or service, Price, Place and Promotion. 'People' and 'potential' are not part of the 4 Ps.","options":["product/service, price, place, people","potential, price, place, people","product/service, price, place, promotion","product/service, promotion, potential, price","price, purchase, placement, product/service"]},{"id":"1-20","n":20,"type":"mcq","q":"Matt's Mobile Services has designed a cell phone plan especially for tech-savvy teens who are heavy cell phone users and live in households earning over $150,000 per year. In tailoring the plan for a specific group of consumers based on their particular needs, Matt's Mobile Services has provided an example of ________.","answer":2,"exp":"Matt designed a plan for one specific group (tech-savvy teens in wealthy households) based on their needs. Choosing a segment to serve is targeting.","options":["societal marketing","broadcasting","target marketing","the selling orientation","the product concept"],"depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 1, p. 5","page":5},{"id":"1-21","n":21,"type":"mcq","q":"The process of dividing a market into subsets of consumers with common needs or characteristics is known as ________.","answer":3,"exp":"Market segmentation is dividing a market into subsets of consumers with common needs or characteristics.","options":["market targeting","ethical marketing","product positioning","market segmentation","market stewardship"],"depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 1, p. 5","page":5},{"id":"1-22","n":22,"type":"mcq","q":"\"Me too\" products are ________.","answer":2,"exp":"'Me too' products are products with no unique image or benefit in the consumer's mind. They are the result of weak positioning.","options":["products that need to be bought along with the original product","secondary products that customers need to be reminded to buy","products that lack a unique image or benefit in the eyes of consumers","products aimed at helping children learn to share with each other","products that are included for free with the desired product"],"book":"Textbook: positioning aims to make a product stand out. A product without a distinct benefit is a 'me-too' product that competes mostly on price.","depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 1, p. 5","page":5},{"id":"1-23","n":23,"type":"mcq","q":"When advertising to teens, Matt's Mobile Services focuses its messaging on how its expansive network enables subscribers to stay connected with friends at all times, so subscribers are always accessible and \"in the loop\" on the latest social news. This is an example of ________.","answer":2,"exp":"Matt's ad tells teens what the product does for them: it keeps them connected and 'in the loop'. Positioning communicates a product's benefits, not its features.","options":["a unique social proposition","societal marketing","communicating the benefits the product provides","product placement","broadcasting"],"depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 1, p. 5","page":5},{"id":"1-24","n":24,"type":"mcq","q":"Packaging and warranties are elements of which of the four Ps?","answer":4,"exp":"Product covers the features, design, brand name, packaging and post-purchase benefits such as warranties and return policies.","options":["price","people","promotion","place","product"],"book":"Textbook: Product = features, designs, brand name, packaging, post-purchase benefits (warranties, return policy).","depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 1, p. 5","page":5},{"id":"1-25","n":25,"type":"mcq","q":"Of the four Ps of the marketing mix, promotion includes ________.","answer":1,"exp":"Promotion covers advertising, public relations, personal selling and sales promotions, meaning everything that communicates the offer.","options":["payment methods","public relations","warranties","discounts","product size"],"book":"Textbook: Promotion = advertising, public relations, sales promotions and personal selling.","depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 1, p. 5","page":5},{"id":"1-26","n":26,"type":"mcq","q":"Of the four Ps of the marketing mix, place includes ________.","answer":3,"exp":"Place is distribution: the channels, stores, distribution centers and online outlets that get the product to consumers.","options":["discounts","public relations","warranties","distribution centers","packaging"],"depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 1, p. 5","page":5},{"id":"1-27","n":27,"type":"mcq","q":"Of the four Ps of the marketing mix, price includes ________.","answer":0,"exp":"Price covers the list price, discounts, allowances and payment methods.","options":["payment methods","public relations","warranties","distribution centers","packaging"],"book":"Textbook: Price = list price, including discounts, allowances and payment methods.","depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 1, p. 5","page":5},{"id":"1-28","n":28,"type":"mcq","q":"Those in favor of socially responsible marketing call on marketers to adopt the societal marketing concept, which requires marketers to ________.","answer":3,"exp":"Chapter 1 says companies build social goals into their missions. The societal marketing concept asks marketers to meet target consumers' needs in ways that also improve society while reaching company goals.","options":["minimize production costs in order to offer the product at the lowest price possible","aggressively market all products to all segments of society","track customer preferences via the Internet to improve customer research databases","fulfill the needs of the target audience in ways that improve society as a whole, while fulfilling the objectives of the organization","lobby for government regulation of their industries"],"book":"Textbook: the societal marketing concept requires marketers to fulfill the needs of the target audience in ways that improve, preserve and enhance society's well-being, while meeting business objectives.","depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 1, p. 18","page":18},{"id":"1-30","n":30,"type":"mcq","q":"Which of the following is consistent with the societal marketing concept?","answer":4,"exp":"All four examples show a company protecting society while still selling: healthier food, responsible ads to young people, no athletes in liquor ads and realistic body images. Each one is socially responsible marketing.","options":["Fast food restaurants develop offerings with less fat and more nutrients.","Food marketers advertise foods to young people in a way that does not encourage overeating.","Marketers do not use professional athletes in liquor or tobacco advertisements because they may serve as role models for young people.","Marketers avoid featuring unreasonably slim females in their advertising campaigns because of the potential of such images to increase eating disorders.","All of the above are consistent with the societal marketing concept."],"depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 1, p. 18","page":18},{"id":"1-31","n":31,"type":"mcq","q":"Socially responsible activities can result in ________.","answer":3,"exp":"Chapter 1: socially responsible activities improve a company's image among stakeholders, including consumers and the financial community, and that can raise sales. So 'all of the above'.","options":["improved corporate image among consumers","improved corporate image among the financial community","increased sales","all of the above","none of the above"]},{"id":"1-32","n":32,"type":"mcq","q":"Online companies create a ________: consumers gain information that turns them into sophisticated customers, opportunities to customize products easily, and entertainment content; marketers gain information about consumers that allows them to be more efficient and precise when marketing their products.","answer":4,"exp":"Chapter 1 calls this a value exchange. Consumers get information, entertainment and customized products, and in return they 'pay' with information about themselves.","options":["value vacuum","one-sided transaction","click-to-buy culture","divestment opportunity","value exchange"]},{"id":"1-33","n":33,"type":"mcq","q":"The adoption of digital technologies has introduced drastic changes into the business environment, including which of the following?","answer":3,"exp":"Technology makes it easier to shop and to reach information and customized products. Marketers can now offer more products and services than ever. The other options are the opposite of what really happened.","options":["Customers are forced to deal with distribution outlets and middlemen in order to obtain goods.","The exchange between marketers and customers is less interactive than in the past.","Consumers face more barriers to accessing information.","Marketers can offer more products and services than ever before.","Market research has become significantly more difficult."],"depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 1, p. 6","page":6},{"id":"1-34","n":34,"type":"mcq","q":"Emerging digital technologies are allowing consumers to have more power than ever before. This means that now customers ________.","answer":2,"exp":"Technology gives consumers easy access to information, so they can compare and find the best prices in seconds.","options":["are required to buy goods and services from local vendors","are limited in the time of day that they are able to buy products","can locate the best prices for products or services","are more limited in the range of products that they can purchase","are forced to acquire used items through middlemen instead of directly from the original owner"]},{"id":"1-35","n":35,"type":"mcq","q":"As consumers spend more time online and have more technological tools that enable them to avoid exposure to TV ads, marketers are ________.","answer":3,"exp":"Consumers now spend more time online and skip TV ads, so marketers move their budgets to digital platforms that offer precise targeting and large audiences.","options":["investing in flashier television advertisements to attract attention to themselves","buying more air time in an attempt to crowd out their competitors' advertisements","lobbying for legislation that will prohibit the sale of devices that allow consumers to avoid advertising on the basis that such devices are anti-competitive","shifting dollars from traditional display advertising to sites like Facebook that can deliver huge audiences","blocking out any mention of brand names during regular programming"],"depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 1, p. 6","page":6},{"id":"1-36","n":36,"type":"mcq","q":"Technology enables marketers to do which of the following?","answer":4,"exp":"Chapter 1 says technology affects the whole marketing mix: new interactive channels, customized products and promotional messages, and better prices and distribution. That supports long-term relationships too, so 'all of the above'.","options":["customize offerings and promotional messages","offer more effective pricing","utilize shorter distribution channels","build long-term relationships with customers","all of the above"]},{"id":"1-38","n":38,"type":"mcq","q":"When consumers use online sites to compare models and brands of products and click for more technical information, companies gain information about ________.","answer":1,"exp":"When consumers click to compare models and read technical details, they show which attributes matter most to them. Tracking these clicks is one of the more precise targeting tools in Chapter 1.","options":["competitors' prices","product attributes that consumers consider the most important","buying patterns","market research","self-reported data"],"depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 1, p. 7","page":7},{"id":"1-39","n":39,"type":"mcq","q":"When Samsung incorporated twitter comments about the iPhone into its advertising pitch, it was taking advantage of which facet of the interactive exchange?","answer":2,"exp":"Samsung used consumers' own Twitter comments in its ads. That is consumer-generated content, part of the interactive communications in Chapter 1.","options":["the ability to get instant reactions to marketers' messages","one-way communications","consumer-generated content","scanner panel data","technological innovations"],"depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 1, p. 10","page":10},{"id":"1-40","n":40,"type":"mcq","q":"Tracy licensed a new software product that enables her to figure out when the same person is accessing her company's website via a mobile phone, tablet, and/or computer so she can keep track of their behavior and target them accordingly. This is an example of ________.","answer":0,"exp":"Cross-screen marketing means tracking and targeting the same user across their computer, phone and tablet, then sending personalized ads.","options":["cross-screen marketing","cyberstalking","time shifting","mobile advertising","commercial blanketing"]},{"id":"1-41","n":41,"type":"mcq","q":"Based on the criteria for successful customization, which of the following products is least likely to be effectively customized?","answer":3,"exp":"Customization works best for high-involvement products where consumers care about personal fit, such as shoes, cars or sunglasses. Laundry detergent is a low-involvement product, so customizing it adds little value.","options":["running shoes","fountain pens","sunglasses","laundry detergent","an automobile"],"book":"Textbook: customization is most practical for products consumers are highly involved with and that can be tailored easily.","depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 1, p. 4","page":4},{"id":"1-42","n":42,"type":"mcq","q":"Which of the following is an example of how the Internet affects prices and distribution?","answer":4,"exp":"Chapter 1 says technology brings better prices and better distribution. Every option shows this: price matching, cheaper e-books, in-store apps and Amazon Lockers. So 'all of the above'.","options":["Consumers use smart phones to compare prices as they shop, leading to price matching guarantees from retailers.","Apple distributes iBooks via its digital bookstore, reducing prices of high school textbooks by almost 90%.","Marketers are using smartphone apps to target shoppers in stores who are looking for information about products.","Amazon installed Amazon Lockers in grocery, convenience and drugstores that accept packages for customers to pick up later.","all of the above"]},{"id":"1-43","n":43,"type":"mcq","q":"________ is defined as the ratio between the customer's perceived benefits and the resources used to obtain those benefits.","answer":1,"exp":"Chapter 1 defines customer value as the ratio between the customer's perceived benefits and the resources used to obtain those benefits.","options":["Customer satisfaction","Customer value","Customer relationship management","Consumer decision making","Consumer marketing"]},{"id":"1-44","n":44,"type":"mcq","q":"Customers have different expectations when they purchase a Lexus than when they purchase a Hyundai. These expectations affect ________.","answer":2,"exp":"Customer satisfaction is performance compared to expectations. Lexus and Hyundai buyers expect different things, so their satisfaction is judged differently.","options":["market segmentation","the value proposition","customer satisfaction","the corporate vision","the market targeting"]},{"id":"1-45","n":45,"type":"mcq","q":"An individual's perception of the performance of a product or service in relation to one's expectations is known as ________.","answer":1,"exp":"Chapter 1 defines customer satisfaction as the individual's perception of the product's performance in relation to his or her expectations.","options":["market segmentation","customer satisfaction","market targeting","product placement","product promotion"]},{"id":"1-46","n":46,"type":"mcq","q":"________ is turning individual consumer transactions into long-term customer relationships by making it in the best interests of customers to stay with the company rather than switch to another firm.","answer":3,"exp":"Customer retention means turning single transactions into long-term relationships by making it in the customer's interest to stay.","options":["Market segmentation","Customer satisfaction","Market targeting","Customer retention","Product promotion"],"depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 1, p. 4","page":4},{"id":"1-47","n":47,"type":"mcq","q":"Which of the following is NOT one of the benefits of retaining loyal customers?","answer":1,"exp":"Chapter 1 says loyal customers are LESS price sensitive. They also buy more, cost less to serve and spread positive word of mouth. So 'more price-sensitive' is NOT a benefit.","options":["Loyal customers are more likely to purchase high margin supplemental products.","Loyal customers are more price-sensitive.","It is less expensive to service existing customers.","Loyal customer refer other customers and engage in positive word-of-mouth.","Loyal customers make employees' jobs easier and more satisfying."]},{"id":"1-48","n":48,"type":"mcq","q":"Antoine is in charge of customer acquisition and retention. He allocates more of his budget to customer retention than customer acquisition. Why does Antoine's decision make sense?","answer":0,"exp":"Loyal customers buy more, are cheaper to serve and spread good word of mouth. Low customer turnover therefore goes with higher profits, which is why retention deserves more budget.","options":["Low customer turnover is correlated with higher profits.","High customer turnover is correlated with higher profits.","Low customer acquisition is correlated with higher profits.","Low customer acquisition is correlated with higher employee satisfaction.","High customer acquisition is correlated with higher employee satisfaction."]},{"id":"1-49","n":49,"type":"mcq","q":"Researchers have identified two interrelated forms of customer engagement with marketers: ________ and ________.","answer":4,"exp":"Chapter 1 lists the two forms of engagement: emotional bonds (personal commitment and attachment) and transactional bonds (the mechanics that make exchanges easy).","options":["customer satisfaction; commercial exchange","social exchange; commercial exchange","emotional bonds; customer satisfaction","customer satisfaction; transaction-based relationships","emotional bonds; transaction-based relationships"]},{"id":"1-52","n":52,"type":"mcq","q":"Which of the following determinants of customer satisfaction with online websites and merchants while shopping online is characterized by the merchant's ability to match purchase recommendations to one's needs and customize products and advertisements so the customer feels unique and valued?","answer":4,"exp":"Adaptation means the site tailors recommendations, products and ads to you, so you feel unique and valued.","options":["trust","engagement","network","interactivity","adaptation"],"depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 1, p. 14","page":14},{"id":"1-53","n":53,"type":"mcq","q":"Which of the following determinants of customer satisfaction with online websites and merchants while shopping online is characterized by the customer's willingness to count on the merchant to successfully complete the purchase transaction and feeling the merchant is reliable and honest?","answer":0,"exp":"Trust means you are willing to count on the merchant to finish the transaction, and you see the merchant as reliable and honest.","options":["trust","engagement","network","interactivity","adaptation"],"depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 1, p. 14","page":14},{"id":"1-54","n":54,"type":"mcq","q":"Which of the following determinants of customer satisfaction with online websites and merchants while shopping online is characterized by an attractive site design; enjoyable shopping at the site; and an inviting, comfortable site?","answer":1,"exp":"Engagement is about the site being attractive, enjoyable and comfortable to shop on.","options":["trust","engagement","network","interactivity","adaptation"],"depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 1, p. 13","page":13},{"id":"1-56","n":56,"type":"mcq","q":"Which is NOT one of the benefits of highly satisfied customers?","answer":3,"exp":"Highly satisfied customers stay with the brand. They keep buying, spread word of mouth and become customers for life. Switching for a slightly lower price describes a mercenary, NOT a satisfied loyal customer.","options":["They keep purchasing the same products.","They provide word-of-mouth to others.","They become customers for life.","They switch to competitors if offered a somewhat lower price.","They repeatedly buy the same brand."]},{"id":"1-57","n":57,"type":"mcq","q":"When Gary buys ice cream, he typically buys whichever brand is on sale that week. With regard to ice cream, Gary is best described as a(n) ________.","answer":4,"exp":"Mercenaries are very satisfied but have no real loyalty. They buy whatever is cheapest or on sale.","options":["brand advocate","apostle","hostage","terrorist","mercenary"],"depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 1, p. 15","page":15},{"id":"1-58","n":58,"type":"mcq","q":"Megan uses Bed Head hair products and enthusiastically recommends them to others any time she receives complements on her hair. With regard to hair products, Megan is best described as a(n) ________.","answer":1,"exp":"Apostles are so delighted with a brand that they actively recommend it to others.","options":["ambivalent consumer","apostle","hostage","terrorist","mercenary"],"depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 1, p. 15","page":15},{"id":"1-59","n":59,"type":"mcq","q":"Norman dislikes the health insurance plan provided by his employer, but is unwilling to switch to a different health insurer because obtaining private health insurance would be significantly more expensive than the employer-subsidized plan in which he is currently enrolled. With regard to health insurance, Norman is best described as a(n) ________.","answer":2,"exp":"Hostages are unhappy but stuck, because switching is too costly or there is no real alternative. That describes Norman's employer health plan.","options":["loyalist","apostle","hostage","terrorist","mercenary"],"depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 1, p. 15","page":15},{"id":"1-60","n":60,"type":"mcq","q":"The primary objective of providing value to customers continuously and more effectively than the competition is ________.","answer":4,"exp":"Chapter 1: the objective of providing value is to retain highly satisfied customers.","options":["to monopolize the market","to produce a compelling advertising message","to help recruit a dedicated workforce","to avoid government regulation of the industry","to create and to retain highly satisfied customers"]},{"id":"1-61","n":61,"type":"mcq","q":"Customer profitability-focused marketing ________.","answer":0,"exp":"Profitability-focused segmentation tracks the costs and revenues of each customer and groups customers into tiers by how profitable they are.","options":["tracks costs and revenues of individual customers and then categorizes those consumers into tiers based on consumption behaviors that are specific to the company's offerings","minimizes the cost of production in order to offer the product at the best price possible","aggressively markets products to consumers who do not necessarily think that they need the product","continually updates the product without regard to the needs of the consumer","invests money in attracting customers that are very sensitive to price and are not loyal to any given brand"],"depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 1, p. 16","page":16},{"id":"1-62","n":62,"type":"mcq","q":"Sophisticated marketers selectively build relationships with customers according to their profitability by customer retention by ________.","answer":3,"exp":"Marketers monitor how much and how each customer buys, place customers in profitability tiers, and build a different strategy for each tier. So the answer is A, B and C.","options":["monitoring customers' consumption volume and shopping patterns","creating tiers of customers according to their profitability levels","developing distinct strategies for each group of customers","A, B and C","none of the above"],"depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 1, p. 16","page":16},{"id":"1-63","n":63,"type":"mcq","q":"John is a frequent flier, who travels with American Airlines at least 250,000 miles a year in first class. According to the text, the profitability classification given him would be ________.","answer":3,"exp":"In the table in Chapter 1, flying at least 250,000 miles in First Class ($4/mile = $1,000,000) puts John in the DIAMOND tier.","options":["emerald","sapphire","titanium","diamond","platinum"]},{"id":"1-64","n":64,"type":"mcq","q":"Using the classification system used by airlines in the text, a diamond or an emerald who is some miles short of keeping his or her status at the end of a given year technically must be \"demoted\" to ________.","answer":1,"exp":"The Chapter 1 table ranks tiers as Diamond, then Emerald, then Sapphire, then Elite/Select. A Diamond or Emerald who falls short of the miles drops down to Sapphire.","options":["gold","sapphire","diamond","iron","platinum"],"book":"Textbook: such customers are often kept at their status anyway, because demoting a valuable customer can push them to a competitor.","depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 1, p. 16","page":16},{"id":"1-65","n":65,"type":"mcq","q":"Which of the following customer retention measurement methods is described: the percentage of customers at the beginning of the year that are still customers by the end of the year?","answer":1,"exp":"Retention rate is the percentage of customers at the start of the year who are still customers at the end of the year.","options":["customer valuation","retention rate","complaint analysis","defection rate","benchmark"],"depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 1, p. 17","page":17},{"id":"1-67","n":67,"type":"mcq","q":"The three distinct but interlocking steps of the consumer decision-making process are ________.","answer":2,"exp":"Chapter 1 shows the decision-making model in three steps: Input, then Process, then Output.","options":["input, analysis, and output","environment, search, and evaluation","input, process, and output","analysis, decision making, and output","analysis, process, and output"]},{"id":"1-68","n":68,"type":"mcq","q":"In the consumer decision-making process, the ________ stage influences the consumer's recognition of a product need.","answer":2,"exp":"The Input stage includes the firm's marketing efforts and sociocultural influences. These outside influences are what make the consumer recognize a need.","options":["analysis","evaluation","input","process","output"]},{"id":"1-69","n":69,"type":"mcq","q":"In the consumer decision-making process, the ________ stage focuses on how consumers make decisions.","answer":3,"exp":"The Process stage is where the consumer actually decides: need recognition, prepurchase search and evaluation of alternatives, all shaped by psychological factors.","options":["analysis","evaluation","input","process","output"]},{"id":"1-70","n":70,"type":"mcq","q":"In the consumer decision-making process, the ________ stage consists of purchase behavior and post-purchase evaluation.","answer":4,"exp":"The Output stage is the purchase itself plus post-purchase evaluation.","options":["analysis","evaluation","input","process","output"]},{"id":"1-71","n":71,"type":"mcq","q":"Ryan runs a store that caters to athletes. He divides the athletic market into different subsets of shoe consumers, such as runners, basketball players, and golfers, which is known as ________.","answer":3,"exp":"Ryan splits the athletic market into runners, basketball players and golfers. Dividing a market into subsets with shared needs is market segmentation.","options":["market targeting","product placement","product positioning","market segmentation","social marketing"]},{"id":"1-72","n":72,"type":"mcq","q":"Ryan runs a store that caters to athletes. He divides the athletic market into different subsets of shoe consumers, such as runners, basketball players, and golfers. Ryan decides to focus his attention on that segment of the market that is interested in running. This selection of a specific market segment is known as ________.","answer":1,"exp":"Ryan chooses one segment (runners) to focus on. Choosing a segment is market targeting.","options":["social marketing","market targeting","product placement","market segmentation","product positioning"]},{"id":"1-73","n":73,"type":"mcq","q":"Ryan runs a store that caters to athletes. He portrays his store as a one-stop-shop for all your running needs, so he has developed a distinct image for his store. This is an example of ________.","answer":3,"exp":"Ryan creates a distinct image for his store as the one-stop shop for runners. Creating that image in consumers' minds is positioning.","options":["segmentation","targeting","placement","positioning","social marketing"]},{"id":"1-74","n":74,"type":"mcq","q":"Transatlantic Airlines identifies the most profitable customers and offers them special incentives, a practice known as ________.","answer":1,"exp":"Ranking customers by how profitable they are and treating each tier differently is customer profitability-focused marketing.","options":["social marketing","customer profitability-focused marketing","market targeting","market segmentation","product positioning"],"depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 1, p. 16","page":16},{"id":"1-89","n":89,"type":"tf","q":"Consumer behavior includes the behavior that consumers display in searching for, purchasing, evaluating and disposing of products and services.","answer":true,"exp":"TRUE. Chapter 1 defines consumer behavior as consumers' choices during searching, evaluating, purchasing and using products. The textbook also includes disposing of them."},{"id":"1-90","n":90,"type":"tf","q":"Marketing-oriented companies try to persuade consumers to buy what the firm has already produced, regardless of whether those goods satisfy consumers' needs.","answer":false,"exp":"FALSE. Pushing what the firm has already made, whether or not it meets needs, is the SELLING concept. Marketing-oriented firms start from consumer needs."},{"id":"1-91","n":91,"type":"tf","q":"The production concept makes sense in developing countries where the main objective is to make products available to the maximum number of consumers at the lowest prices.","answer":true,"exp":"TRUE. The production concept fits markets like developing countries, where the main goal is to make products available to as many people as possible at the lowest price.","depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 1, p. 4","page":4},{"id":"1-92","n":92,"type":"tf","q":"Marketing myopia causes companies to focus on crucial changes in the marketplace as they look through the window rather than into the mirror.","answer":false,"exp":"FALSE. Chapter 1 says the opposite. Myopic companies look IN THE MIRROR (at their own product) instead of OUT THE WINDOW (at consumer needs), so they miss important changes in the market."},{"id":"1-93","n":93,"type":"tf","q":"The selling concept does not consider customer satisfaction because consumers who are aggressively induced to buy products they do not want or need will not buy them again.","answer":true,"exp":"TRUE. The selling concept ignores satisfaction. Consumers pushed into buying things they don't want won't buy again.","depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 1, p. 5","page":5},{"id":"1-94","n":94,"type":"tf","q":"Implementing the marketing concept includes consumer research, market segmentation, a combination of the product, price, place and promotion strategies, providing value, and longterm customer satisfaction and retention.","answer":true,"exp":"TRUE. Implementing the marketing concept requires research, segmentation, targeting, positioning and the 4 Ps. It also requires delivering value, satisfaction and retention."},{"id":"1-95","n":95,"type":"tf","q":"Companies that focus on understanding customers are able to continue to grow and remain leaders in their industries in spite of increased competition and changing business environments.","answer":true,"exp":"TRUE. The marketing concept puts understanding and satisfying customers at the center. Companies that do this keep growing even when competition and conditions change.","depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 1, p. 3","page":3},{"id":"1-97","n":97,"type":"tf","q":"Most products do not have substitutes or similar products in the marketplace, so having a distinct benefit is usually unimportant for marketers.","answer":false,"exp":"FALSE. Most products DO have substitutes, which is exactly why positioning and a distinct benefit matter so much.","depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 1, p. 5","page":5},{"id":"1-98","n":98,"type":"tf","q":"Me-too products lack a unique image or benefit.","answer":true,"exp":"TRUE. 'Me-too' products have no unique image or benefit. They are a failure of positioning.","depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 1, p. 5","page":5},{"id":"1-99","n":99,"type":"tf","q":"Post-purchase benefits such as warranties and return policies are part of the place element of the marketing mix.","answer":false,"exp":"FALSE. Warranties and return policies belong to the PRODUCT element of the 4 Ps, not to Place.","depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 1, p. 5","page":5},{"id":"1-100","n":100,"type":"tf","q":"The societal marketing concept requires marketers to fulfill the needs of the target audience in ways that improve, preserve and enhance society's well-being and meet business objectives.","answer":true,"exp":"TRUE. The societal marketing concept, which applies the social responsibility in Chapter 1, means meeting target consumers' needs in ways that protect and improve society's well-being while meeting business goals.","depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 1, p. 18","page":18},{"id":"1-102","n":102,"type":"tf","q":"The Internet offers marketers the ability to customize their products, services, and promotional messages to individual consumers, as seen in how Amazon.com sends emails to customers who are interested in a product category telling them about a new promotional offer.","answer":true,"exp":"TRUE. Chapter 1 lists 'customizing products and promotional messages' as one way technology affects the marketing mix. Amazon's personalized emails are an example."},{"id":"1-103","n":103,"type":"tf","q":"Much like traditional television advertising, digital technologies allow for two-way interactive exchanges between customers and marketers.","answer":false,"exp":"FALSE. Traditional TV advertising is one-way. Digital technologies are what made two-way interactive exchanges possible.","depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 1, p. 6","page":6},{"id":"1-104","n":104,"type":"tf","q":"When Amazon buyers find books instantly, read sample pages and reviews posted by other readers, and begin reading purchased books within minutes after placing their orders, Amazon collects information about what books they looked at, the sample pages and reviews they clicked on, and the time spent on each activity. This is an example of a value exchange.","answer":true,"exp":"TRUE. Amazon buyers get speed, samples and reviews, and Amazon gets data about their behavior. That is the value exchange in Chapter 1."},{"id":"1-105","n":105,"type":"tf","q":"The societal marketing concept advocates a short-term perspective in driving for increased market share and quick profits.","answer":false,"exp":"FALSE. Social responsibility takes a LONG-term view, improving society and the company's image. Chasing quick profits is short-term thinking."},{"id":"1-106","n":106,"type":"tf","q":"The downside of electronic communications is marketers have less ability to gauge the effectiveness of their promotional messages than they did via traditional media outlets.","answer":false,"exp":"FALSE. Digital communication lets marketers measure responses (clicks, views, reactions) much more precisely than traditional media.","depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 1, p. 6","page":6},{"id":"1-107","n":107,"type":"tf","q":"Cross-screen marketing consists of tracking and targeting users across their computers, mobile phones and tablets.","answer":true,"exp":"TRUE. That is the definition of cross-screen marketing in Chapter 1."},{"id":"1-108","n":108,"type":"tf","q":"Customization is more practical and effective for marketers who are selling low involvement products.","answer":false,"exp":"FALSE. Customization works best for HIGH-involvement products, where personal fit matters to the buyer.","depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 1, p. 4","page":4},{"id":"1-109","n":109,"type":"tf","q":"The three drivers of successful relationships between marketers and customers are customer value, high levels of customer satisfaction, and customer retention.","answer":true,"exp":"TRUE. Chapter 1 shows the three drivers of successful relationships: Customer Value, Customer Satisfaction and Customer Retention."},{"id":"1-110","n":110,"type":"tf","q":"Customer satisfaction is a function of customer expectations.","answer":true,"exp":"TRUE. Satisfaction is perceived performance compared to expectations."},{"id":"1-111","n":111,"type":"tf","q":"Sophisticated marketers today practice selective relationship building, which can lead to companies rejecting or firing certain types of consumers.","answer":true,"exp":"TRUE. With profitability-focused segmentation, firms build relationships selectively and may even 'fire' customers who are not profitable.","depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 1, p. 15","page":15},{"id":"1-112","n":112,"type":"tf","q":"Purchase behavior and post-purchase evaluation are elements of the input stage of the consumer decision-making model.","answer":false,"exp":"FALSE. Purchase and post-purchase evaluation belong to the OUTPUT stage. Input is the firm's marketing efforts and sociocultural influences."},{"id":"1-113","n":113,"type":"tf","q":"Courtney has been looking at laptop computers for a while and has decided to purchase a Dell. Courtney's decision to purchase a Dell signifies the output stage of decision making.","answer":true,"exp":"TRUE. The purchase decision itself is part of the Output stage."},{"id":"1-114","n":114,"type":"tf","q":"Students with expertise in consumer behavior should pursue employment in three areas: brand management, advertising, and consumer research.","answer":true,"exp":"TRUE. Chapter 1 lists the employment opportunities: brand management, advertising and consumer research."}],"book":{"first":2,"last":25,"count":24}},{"id":2,"title":"Consumer Segmentation","exam":"midterm","slides":[{"img":"slides/ch2/01.jpg","title":"Consumer Behavior","text":"Consumer Behavior Twelfth Edition, Global Edition Chapter 2 Market Segmentation and Real-Time Bidding Copyright © 2019 Pearson Education, Ltd. All Rights Reserved."},{"img":"slides/ch2/02.jpg","title":"Learning Objectives","text":"Learning Objectives 2.1 To understand how to segment markets along demographics, lifestyles, product benefits and usage, and media exposure. 2.2 To understand how to identify, select, and reach target markets. 2.3 To understand real-time bidding as compared with market segmentation."},{"img":"slides/ch2/03.jpg","title":"Usage-Occasion Segmentation","text":"Usage-Occasion Segmentation Source: Mack’s® is a registered trademark of Mckeon Products, Inc."},{"img":"slides/ch2/04.jpg","title":"Learning Objective 2.1","text":"Learning Objective 2.1 2.1 To understand how to segment markets along demographics, lifestyles, product benefits and usage, and media exposure."},{"img":"slides/ch2/05.jpg","title":"Two Types of Shared Characteristics","text":"Two Types of Shared Characteristics Quantitative factors Consumer-intrinsic Consumption-based Cognitive factors Consumer-intrinsic Consumption-specific"},{"img":"slides/ch2/06.jpg","title":"Marketers Combine Factors","text":"Marketers Combine Factors Demographics determine needs for products Psychographics explain buyers’ purchase decisions and choices"},{"img":"slides/ch2/07.jpg","title":"Demographic Segmentation","text":"Demographic Segmentation Age Gender Marital Status Household type and Size Income and Wealth; Occupation Geographical location"},{"img":"slides/ch2/08.jpg","title":"Age","text":"Age Age influences buying priorities Marketers target age groups Example: movie studios compete for teens and young adults each summer"},{"img":"slides/ch2/09.jpg","title":"Gender","text":"Gender Gender roles have blurred Gender less accurate to distinguish among consumers in some product categories than it used to be Some ads depict mean and women in roles traditionally occupied by the opposite gender Some products (e.g. skin care and snacks) are marketed differently to men and women"},{"img":"slides/ch2/10.jpg","title":"Households","text":"Households Family life cycle Important occasions change consumption patterns"},{"img":"slides/ch2/11.jpg","title":"Social Standing","text":"Social Standing Social class Fluctuations affect marketing"},{"img":"slides/ch2/12.jpg","title":"Ethnicity","text":"Ethnicity Members of the same culture often share the same values, beliefs, and customs. Marketers may target culturally distinct segments with the same product using different promotional appeals."},{"img":"slides/ch2/13.jpg","title":"Psychographics","text":"Psychographics Defined Consumers’ lifestyles, which include consumers’ activities, interests, and opinions"},{"img":"slides/ch2/14.jpg","title":"Table 2.1 Psychographic Measures (1 of 2)","text":"Table 2.1 Psychographic Measures (1 of 2) Psychographic Statements Personal Values* I have a sense of belonging. I seek fun and enjoyment in life. I am self-fulfilled. Financial security is important to me. My greatest achievements are ahead of me. I am more conventional than experimental. My social status is an important part of my life. Apparel and Fashion* I buy clothes I like regardless of current fashion. My friends often ask me for advice on fashion. Men do not notice women who do not dress well."},{"img":"slides/ch2/15.jpg","title":"Table 2.1 Psychographic Measures (2 of 2)","text":"Table 2.1 Psychographic Measures (2 of 2) Psychographic Statements Gift Giving* I generally give gifts because people expect me to. I try to give gifts that carry personal messages to recipients. Gifts always communicate love and friendship. Personal Relationships* I find it hard to speak in front of a group. When I make friends I always try to make the relationships work. I enjoy making my own decisions. Others usually know what’s best for me. Buying Online* It requires too much time to set up accounts with online stores. The look of a website is an important factor in my buying decisions. I tell others about my experiences in buying online. At Leisure** Played adult games (e.g., cards or mahjong) Visited art gallery or museum Went hunting or shooting Went to the movies Attended a sporting event * Responses on a “strongly agree” to “strongly disagree” scale ** Responses indicate the number of times respondents had engaged in the activity during the past 6 months."},{"img":"slides/ch2/16.jpg","title":"V A L S™","text":"V A L S™"},{"img":"slides/ch2/17.jpg","title":"Geodemographic Segmentation","text":"Geodemographic Segmentation P R I Z M ® Location, demographics, and consumer behavior Lifestage groups Urbanization and affluence ConneXions P $ Y C L E"},{"img":"slides/ch2/18.jpg","title":"Benefit Segmentation","text":"Benefit Segmentation Benefits sought represent consumer needs Important for positioning Source: Campbell Soup Company"},{"img":"slides/ch2/19.jpg","title":"Expectations of Service Providers","text":"Expectations of Service Providers Social Benefits Recognized/known by staff Treated in a way that they feel important/friendship Special Treatment Benefits Helping when something goes wrong Receiving priority treatment Confidence-Related Benefits Feel confident service will be provided correctly Clear descriptions of services and what to expect"},{"img":"slides/ch2/20.jpg","title":"Product Usage","text":"Product Usage Usage-rate segmentation (heavy, medium, light) Lots of competition for heavy users"},{"img":"slides/ch2/21.jpg","title":"Learning Objective 2.2","text":"Learning Objective 2.2 2.2 To understand how to identify, select, and reach target markets."},{"img":"slides/ch2/22.jpg","title":"Airlines Example","text":"Airlines Example Coach Premium Economy (50% more than coach) Business Class (2+x Premium Economy) First Class (3-4x Business)"},{"img":"slides/ch2/23.jpg","title":"Segments Should Be…","text":"Segments Should Be… Identifiable Profitable Reachable"},{"img":"slides/ch2/24.jpg","title":"Reachable Market Segments","text":"Reachable Market Segments"},{"img":"slides/ch2/25.jpg","title":"Mobile Targeting","text":"Mobile Targeting Showrooming Geofencing Websites are versatile"},{"img":"slides/ch2/26.jpg","title":"Learning Objective 2.3","text":"Learning Objective 2.3 2.3 To understand real-time bidding as compared with market segmentation."},{"img":"slides/ch2/27.jpg","title":"Real-Time Bidding (R T B)","text":"Real-Time Bidding (R T B) Defined A technique that allows advertisers to reach the right user in the right place at the right time, which also sets the price advertisers pay per impression or action"},{"img":"slides/ch2/28.jpg","title":"Web Crawlers","text":"Web Crawlers Defined Programs that capture content across the internet and transmit it to the data broker’s servers"},{"img":"slides/ch2/29.jpg","title":"Predictive Analytics","text":"Predictive Analytics Defined Measures that predict consumers’ future purchases on the bases of past buying information and other data, and also evaluate the impact of personalized promotions stemming from the predictions."},{"img":"slides/ch2/30.jpg","title":"Application: Target","text":"Application: Target"},{"img":"slides/ch2/31.jpg","title":"Ad Exchange","text":"Ad Exchange Defined A big pool of ad impressions – websites – paste their ad impressions into the pool, hoping someone will buy them"}],"questions":[{"id":"2-1","n":1,"type":"mcq","q":"The process of dividing a market into distinct subsets of consumers with common needs or characteristics is known as ________.","answer":1,"exp":"Market segmentation means dividing a market into subsets of consumers with shared characteristics. Chapter 2 shows the kinds of shared characteristics used to do it. Targeting comes AFTER segmentation: it is choosing which segment to pursue.","options":["target marketing","market segmentation","consumer behavior","the marketing concept","market evaluation"]},{"id":"2-2","n":2,"type":"mcq","q":"Qantas Airlines targets four distinct segments: coach passengers, premium economy, business and first class, and uses ________ to clearly differentiate between the options targeted at each segment.","answer":0,"exp":"The airline example shows four classes (Coach, Premium Economy, Business, First), each with its own features and price. Making each option clearly distinct in customers' minds is positioning.","options":["positioning","behavioral targeting","geographic segmentation","reverse targeting","countersegmentation"]},{"id":"2-3","n":3,"type":"mcq","q":"The ________ of a product or service is the process by which a company creates a distinct image and identity for its products, services, and brands in consumers' minds.","answer":2,"exp":"Positioning is creating a distinct image and identity for a product in consumers' minds. The airline classes in Chapter 2 show this: each class is positioned differently.","options":["segmentation","target","positioning","psychographic inventory","concentrated marketing strategy"],"book":"Textbook: segmentation and targeting decide WHO the company serves. Positioning decides HOW it wants those consumers to see the product.","depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 2, p. 27","page":27},{"id":"2-4","n":4,"type":"mcq","q":"Which of the following is NOT one of the characteristics of an effective target market?","answer":3,"exp":"Chapter 2 says segments should be Identifiable, Profitable and Reachable. A segment that is 'inaccessible' cannot be reached, so it is not effective.","options":["identifiable","sizeable","reachable","inaccessible","congruent with the marketer's objectives and resources"],"book":"Textbook criteria for an effective target segment: identifiable, sizeable (profitable), stable, accessible (reachable), and congruent with the company's objectives and resources.","depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 2, p. 37","page":37},{"id":"2-5","n":5,"type":"mcq","q":"A financial firm advertising in the Wall Street Journal should use which segmentation system to find out which geographic areas of the country include substantial numbers of their segment?","answer":2,"exp":"PRIZM is geodemographic segmentation. It links location, demographics and consumer behavior, so it shows which areas (zip codes) contain many members of the firm's segment.","options":["Nielsen MyBestSegments","VALS","PRIZM","Nielsen NetRatings","Magazine Readers Index (MRI)"],"depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 2, p. 33","page":33},{"id":"2-6","n":6,"type":"mcq","q":"Consumers' characteristics can be classified as either behavioral or cognitive. In this context, behavioral data can be determined from direct questioning and categorized by a single objective measure. Examples of behavioral data include ________.","answer":3,"exp":"Chapter 2: quantitative (behavioral) factors can be measured objectively by asking or observing. Examples are education, quantity purchased and gender. Benefits wanted and psychographics are cognitive, so they are not behavioral.","options":["demographics, gender, and benefits wanted","usage rate, level of involvement, and psychographics","personality traits, lifestyles, and family life cycle","education, quantity of product purchased, and gender","level of involvement, awareness of product alternatives, and benefits wanted"],"depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 2, p. 27","page":27},{"id":"2-7","n":7,"type":"mcq","q":"Consumers' characteristics can be classed as either behavioral or cognitive. In this context, cognitive factors are abstract, can be determined only through more complex psychological and attitudinal questioning, and generally have no single, universal definitions. Examples of cognitive factors include ________.","answer":4,"exp":"Chapter 2: cognitive factors are abstract and need psychological or attitudinal questions. Examples are cultural values, personality traits and benefits sought. Demographics, education and usage rate are behavioral (quantitative).","options":["demographics, gender, and benefits wanted","usage rate, level of involvement, and education","personality traits, lifestyles, and gender","education, usage situation, and social class","cultural values, personality traits, and benefits sought"],"depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 2, p. 27","page":27},{"id":"2-8","n":8,"type":"mcq","q":"Consumers' characteristics can be classed as either consumer-intrinsic or consumptionspecific. In this context, consumer-intrinsic features stem from the consumer's physical, social, and psychological characteristics. Examples of consumer-intrinsic features include ________.","answer":2,"exp":"Consumer-intrinsic means characteristics of the person themselves: personality, cultural values, income. Consumption-specific means attitudes toward particular products or buying situations.","options":["demographics, gender, and benefits sought","usage rate, level of involvement, and psychographics","personality traits, cultural values, and income","education, consumption-specific attitudes, and social class","attitudes regarding shopping, awareness of product alternatives, and benefits wanted"],"depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 2, p. 27","page":27},{"id":"2-9","n":9,"type":"mcq","q":"Consumers' characteristics can be classed as either consumer-intrinsic or consumptionspecific. In this context, consumption-specific features are attitudes and preferences toward specific products or buying situations. Examples of consumption-specific features include ________.","answer":1,"exp":"Consumption-specific factors are about buying and using. Examples are attitudes toward shopping, benefits sought in products and how often you do leisure activities.","options":["demographics, gender, and benefits sought","attitudes toward shopping, benefits sought in products, and frequency of leisure activities","personality traits, lifestyles, and income","education, usage situation, and gender","personality traits, awareness of product alternatives, and attitudes toward political issues"],"depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 2, p. 27","page":27},{"id":"2-10","n":10,"type":"mcq","q":"In reference to consumer characteristics, ________ can be determined from direct questioning or observation and categorized by a simple objective measure, whereas ________ are abstract and can be determined only through more complex psychological and attitudinal questioning.","answer":1,"exp":"Chapter 2: behavioral (quantitative) data is measured by direct questions or observation with an objective measure. Cognitive data is abstract and needs complex attitudinal questioning.","options":["consumption-based features; consumer-intrinsic features","behavioral data; cognitive data","product-specific features; consumption-based features","cognitive data; behavioral data","consumer-intrinsic features; consumption-based features"],"depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 2, p. 27","page":27},{"id":"2-11","n":11,"type":"mcq","q":"Another term for psychographic characteristics is ________.","answer":1,"exp":"Chapter 2: psychographics = consumers' LIFESTYLES (activities, interests and opinions).","options":["age","lifestyles","benefits sought","use-situation factors","use-related factors"]},{"id":"2-12","n":12,"type":"mcq","q":"Age, gender, and income are bases of which of the following types of segmentation?","answer":3,"exp":"Age, gender and income are all on the Demographic Segmentation list in Chapter 2.","options":["geographic segmentation","sociocultural segmentation","benefit segmentation","demographic segmentation","psychological segmentation"]},{"id":"2-13","n":13,"type":"mcq","q":"________ is/are defined by computing an index based on three quantifiable variables: income, education, and occupation.","answer":1,"exp":"Social class is measured with an index of income, education and occupation.","options":["Demographics","Social class","Psychographics","Personality traits","Sociocultural values"],"book":"Textbook: social class is usually measured as a weighted index of education, occupation and income.","depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 2, p. 29","page":29},{"id":"2-14","n":14,"type":"mcq","q":"When Colgate commonly targets age groups into four segments and offers each one its own toothpaste, they are using ________ segmentation.","answer":0,"exp":"Colgate makes a different toothpaste for each age group. Segmenting by age is demographic segmentation.","options":["demographic","sociocultural","psychological","physiological","use-related"]},{"id":"2-15","n":15,"type":"mcq","q":"________ information is often the most accessible and cost-effective way to identify a target market.","answer":0,"exp":"Demographics are the easiest and cheapest data to get, and they determine many product needs. That makes them the most accessible way to identify a target market.","options":["Demographic","Sociocultural","Psychological","Physiological","Benefit"],"depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 2, p. 28","page":28},{"id":"2-16","n":16,"type":"mcq","q":"Many marketers target the generation that is 18 to 34 years old, known as the ________.","answer":0,"exp":"Chapter 2: marketers target age groups. Consumers aged 18–34 are the Millennials (also shown as an age group in the Ch.1 technology chart).","options":["millennial Generation","generation X","generation Z","net gen","baby boomers"],"depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 2, p. 28","page":28},{"id":"2-17","n":17,"type":"mcq","q":"Which of the following is NOT an example of the recent shift in traditional gender roles in product targeting and advertising?","answer":2,"exp":"Chapter 2 says gender roles have blurred. Ads show men and women in each other's traditional roles, and products such as skin care and snacks are marketed to both genders. TV shows targeting women is an old practice, not part of this shift.","options":["Van Gogh Blue vodka targeting women","Frito-Lay targeting women","television shows targeting women","department stores targeting men with grooming products","Dove Men+Care"]},{"id":"2-18","n":18,"type":"mcq","q":"Traditionally, the ________ has been the focus of most marketing efforts.","answer":1,"exp":"Chapter 2: the household and family has traditionally been the main focus of marketing. Life-cycle stages and major occasions change what a family consumes.","options":["mother","family","individual","child","teen"],"depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 2, p. 28","page":28},{"id":"2-19","n":19,"type":"mcq","q":"________ is felt by many marketers to be a strong indicator of the ability to pay for a product.","answer":4,"exp":"Income and wealth are seen as the best indicator of a consumer's ability to pay.","options":["Age","Gender","Occupation","Education","Income"],"depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 2, p. 27","page":27},{"id":"2-20","n":20,"type":"mcq","q":"As a base for market segmentation, ________ is/are commonly computed as a weighted index of education, occupation, and income.","answer":0,"exp":"Social class is usually computed as a weighted index of education, occupation and income.","options":["social class","family life cycle","personality traits","sociocultural values","psychographics"],"depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 2, p. 29","page":29},{"id":"2-21","n":21,"type":"mcq","q":"________ implies a hierarchy in which individuals in the same class generally have the degree of status, whereas members of other classes have either higher or lower status.","answer":3,"exp":"Social class is a hierarchy. People in the same class have about the same status, and people in other classes have higher or lower status.","options":["Usage rate","Subculture","Family life cycle","Social class","Religion"],"depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 2, p. 29","page":29},{"id":"2-22","n":22,"type":"mcq","q":"Marketers segment some populations on the basis of cultural heritage and ________ because members of the same culture tend to share the same values, beliefs, and customs.","answer":2,"exp":"Chapter 2: members of the same culture or ethnicity often share the same values, beliefs and customs, so marketers can segment by ethnicity.","options":["demographics","social class","ethnicity","personality traits","sociocultural values"]},{"id":"2-23","n":23,"type":"mcq","q":"PRIZM is an example of ________ segmentation.","answer":1,"exp":"PRIZM combines location, demographics and consumer behavior. That makes it geodemographic segmentation.","options":["demographic","geodemographic","psychographic","sociocultural","use-related"]},{"id":"2-24","n":24,"type":"mcq","q":"The most popular use of geography in strategic targeting is ________ - a hybrid segmentation scheme based on the premise that people who live close to one another are likely to have similar financial means, tastes, preferences, lifestyles, and consumption habits.","answer":1,"exp":"Geodemographics is based on the idea that people who live near each other have similar income, tastes, lifestyles and consumption habits.","options":["countersegmentation","geodemographics","behavioral targeting","social class","psychographics"],"depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 2, p. 33","page":33},{"id":"2-26","n":26,"type":"mcq","q":"Lifestyles, also known as ________, include consumers' activities, interests, and opinions.","answer":0,"exp":"Chapter 2: psychographics = consumers' lifestyles, meaning their activities, interests and opinions.","options":["psychographics","demographics","geodemographics","personality traits","benefit segmentation"]},{"id":"2-27","n":27,"type":"mcq","q":"Level of agreement with which of the following statements is associated with the Personal Controls Psychographic Factor?","answer":1,"exp":"In Table 2.1, 'I seek fun and enjoyment in life' is one of the Personal Values statements. These statements measure internal drivers (controls) like fun, belonging and security. The other options belong to Gift Giving, Buying Online and Apparel & Fashion.","options":["I generally give gifts because people expect me to.","I seek fun and enjoyment in life.","The look of a website is an important factor in my buying decisions.","My friends often ask me for advice on fashion.","I am more conventional than experimental."]},{"id":"2-28","n":28,"type":"mcq","q":"The widely used segmentation system combining lifestyle and values is ________.","answer":1,"exp":"VALS, short for Values And LifeStyles, segments consumers by their values (motivations) and lifestyles (resources).","options":["PALS","VALS","LAPS","SALS","CALS"],"depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 2, p. 31","page":31},{"id":"2-29","n":29,"type":"mcq","q":"The VALS typology classifies the American adult population into eight distinctive subgroups based on their motivations and level of resources. The three primary motivations include ________.","answer":1,"exp":"The VALS framework in Chapter 2 has three primary motivations across the top: Ideals, Achievement and Self-Expression.","options":["ideals, education, and achievement","ideals, achievement, and self-expression","knowledge, education, and self-expression","independence, aptitude, and achievement","aptitude, ideals, and self-expression"]},{"id":"2-30","n":30,"type":"mcq","q":"In the VALS typology, ________ are motivated by ideals and tend to have low resources. They are generally slow to change as well as technology averse, and tend to prefer familiar products and established brands.","answer":1,"exp":"On the VALS chart, Believers are motivated by Ideals and have low resources. They are conservative and loyal to familiar, established brands.","options":["Innovators","Believers","Thinkers","Strivers","Experiencers"],"depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 2, p. 31","page":31},{"id":"2-31","n":31,"type":"mcq","q":"In the VALS typology, Achievers are motivated by achievement and tend to have high resources. They have goal-oriented lifestyles that center on family and career. This segment tends to prefer ________.","answer":4,"exp":"Achievers are motivated by achievement and have high resources. They like premium products that show their success to peers.","options":["familiar products and established brands","value over luxury, and tend to buy basic products","products that offer durability, functionality, and value","products that reflect cultivated tastes for upscale niche products and services","premium products that demonstrate success to their peers"],"depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 2, p. 31","page":31},{"id":"2-32","n":32,"type":"mcq","q":"In the VALS typology, Thinkers are motivated by ideals and tend to have high resources. They tend to be well educated and actively seek out information in the decision-making process. This segment tends to prefer ________.","answer":2,"exp":"Thinkers are motivated by ideals, have high resources and are well informed. They want products that offer durability, functionality and value.","options":["familiar products and established brands","value over luxury, and tend to buy basic products","products that offer reliability and value","products that reflect cultivated tastes for upscale niche products and services","premium products that demonstrate success to their peers"],"book":"Textbook: Thinkers favor durability, functionality and value. They look for reliable, practical products.","depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 2, p. 31","page":31},{"id":"2-33","n":33,"type":"mcq","q":"In the VALS typology, Makers are motivated by self-expression and tend to have low resources. They choose hands-on constructive activities and spend leisure time with family and close friends. This segment tends to prefer ________.","answer":1,"exp":"Makers are motivated by self-expression, have low resources and are practical and hands-on. They prefer value over luxury and buy basic products.","options":["familiar products and established brands","value over luxury, and tend to buy basic products","products that offer durability, functionality, and value","products that reflect cultivated tastes for upscale niche products and services","premium products that demonstrate success to their peers"],"depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 2, p. 31","page":31},{"id":"2-34","n":34,"type":"mcq","q":"In the VALS typology, Innovators are successful, take-charge people with high self-esteem. Because they have such abundant resources, they exhibit all three primary motivations in varying degrees. They are change leaders and are the most receptive to new ideas and technologies. This segment tends to prefer ________.","answer":3,"exp":"Innovators sit at the top with the most resources. They prefer upscale niche products that show cultivated taste.","options":["familiar products and established brands","value over luxury, and tend to buy basic products","products that offer durability, functionality, and value","products that reflect cultivated tastes for upscale niche products and services","premium products that demonstrate success to their peers"],"depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 2, p. 31","page":31},{"id":"2-35","n":35,"type":"mcq","q":"When Colgate offers Colgate Total, which provides 12-hours of germ fighting; Colgate Max Fresh, which fights bad breath; and Colgate Sensitive Pro-Relief for people who have sensitive gums, they are segmenting based on ________.","answer":1,"exp":"Each Colgate product delivers a different benefit: germ fighting, fresh breath or sensitivity relief. That is benefit segmentation.","options":["usage rate","benefits","personality","culture","values"]},{"id":"2-36","n":36,"type":"mcq","q":"Marketers of many products such as soup, laundry detergent, beer, and dog food have found that a relatively small group of heavy users accounts for a disproportionately large percentage of the total product usage. Targeting these heavy users specifically is an example of ________.","answer":3,"exp":"Targeting the small group of heavy users who consume most of the product is usage-rate segmentation.","options":["benefit-based segmentation","demographic segmentation","cross-cultural segmentation","usage-rate segmentation","usage situation segmentation"],"depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 2, p. 35","page":35},{"id":"2-38","n":38,"type":"mcq","q":"Sunshine Cruise Lines markets Adventure Cruises as a way to see multiple islands from the comfort of a single ship, alleviating the hassle of flying between islands and staying in a different hotel every couple of nights. Targeting consumers who view this convenience as appealing is an example of ________.","answer":4,"exp":"Sunshine sells convenience (no flights, no hotel changes). Targeting people who want that benefit is benefit segmentation.","options":["countersegmentation","cultural segmentation","usage rate segmentation","demographic segmentation","benefit segmentation"]},{"id":"2-39","n":39,"type":"mcq","q":"Rosetta Car Rental exercises ________ when it stocks more convertible cars in California than in New York.","answer":1,"exp":"Stocking more convertibles in sunny California than in New York divides the market by location. That is geographic segmentation.","options":["demographic segmentation","geographic segmentation","sociocultural segmentation","psychographic segmentation","usage-situation segmentation"]},{"id":"2-40","n":40,"type":"mcq","q":"Beyond flavor and the presence or absence of sugar, Nicorette offers a gum that will whiten your teeth while satisfying your nicotine craving and helping you quit smoking. This gum has been positioned on the basis of ________.","answer":4,"exp":"The gum is positioned on an extra benefit: whiter teeth while quitting smoking. Benefits sought are 'important for positioning'.","options":["demographic segmentation","family life cycle segmentation","psychographic segmentation","mass marketing","benefit segmentation"]},{"id":"2-41","n":41,"type":"mcq","q":"________ segmentation differentiates among heavy users, medium users, light users, and nonusers of a specific product, service or brand.","answer":2,"exp":"Usage-rate segmentation divides consumers into heavy, medium and light users (and nonusers).","options":["Brand awareness","Brand loyalty","Usage rate","Sociocultural","Benefit"]},{"id":"2-42","n":42,"type":"mcq","q":"Research has shown that 25 percent of beer drinkers account for about 75 percent of all beer consumed. This is an example of a justification for ________ segmentation.","answer":1,"exp":"A small group of heavy users drinks most of the beer. That is why marketers use usage-rate segmentation and compete hard for heavy users.","options":["benefit","usage rate","psychographic","lifestyle","usage-situation"],"depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 2, p. 35","page":35},{"id":"2-44","n":44,"type":"mcq","q":"The greeting card industry capitalizes on occasions to sell products; this is a perfect example of ________ segmentation.","answer":2,"exp":"Greeting cards are bought for occasions such as birthdays and holidays. That is usage-occasion (usage situation) segmentation, the example in Chapter 2.","options":["lifestyle","benefit","usage situation","demographic","geographic"]},{"id":"2-45","n":45,"type":"mcq","q":"Which of the following is NOT tracked during online targeting?","answer":4,"exp":"Online and mobile targeting tracks digital behavior: sites visited, pages viewed, how often you come back, and lifestyle clues taken from that data. A purchase made in a physical store is not tracked online.","options":["the sites consumers visit","the pages consumers look at on a site","lifestyles and personalities","how often consumers return to a site","brick-and-mortar purchases"],"depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 2, p. 40","page":40},{"id":"2-46","n":46,"type":"mcq","q":"________ consists of looking at merchandise at a physical store, scanning its barcode with smartphones and using these devices to check the items' prices and purchase the items online.","answer":3,"exp":"Showrooming means looking at products in a physical store and then using your phone to compare prices and buy online.","options":["Mobile Targeting","Geofencing","Psychographics","Showrooming","GPS marketing"],"depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 2, p. 40","page":40},{"id":"2-47","n":47,"type":"mcq","q":"________ are measures that predict consumers' future purchases on the basis of past buying information and other data.","answer":1,"exp":"Chapter 2 defines predictive analytics as measures that predict consumers' future purchases from past buying information and other data.","options":["Showroom analytics","Predictive analytics","Geofencing analytics","Social analytics","Consumer spying analytics"]},{"id":"2-51","n":51,"type":"mcq","q":"Promotional alerts sent to the smartphones of customers, who opted into this service, when the customers near or enter the store is called ________.","answer":4,"exp":"Geofencing sends promotional alerts to opted-in customers' phones when they come near or enter the store.","options":["psychographic segmentation","geodemographic segmentation","real-time bidding","showrooming","geofencing"],"depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 2, p. 40","page":40},{"id":"2-52","n":52,"type":"mcq","q":"Most marketers prefer to target consumer segments that are ________.","answer":1,"exp":"Chapter 2: segments should be Identifiable, PROFITABLE and Reachable. Marketers want segments that are profitable.","options":["small","profitable","unpredictable","inaccessible","expensive to pursue"]},{"id":"2-53","n":53,"type":"mcq","q":"Peter wants to segment the target audience for his frozen yogurt shop based on benefits sought. Unlike demographic variables, the benefits sought variable is difficult to measure, so it is not as ________.","answer":2,"exp":"Benefits sought are abstract and hard to measure, so they are less IDENTIFIABLE than demographics. Identifiable is the first criterion in Chapter 2.","options":["profitable","stable in terms of consumption patterns","identifiable","inaccessible","expensive to pursue"],"depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 2, p. 37","page":37},{"id":"2-54","n":54,"type":"mcq","q":"The image and unique identity of a product, service or brand in consumers' minds is called its ________.","answer":0,"exp":"A product's image and unique identity in consumers' minds is its position. Each airline class in Chapter 2 holds a distinct position.","options":["position","perception","segment","target","imposition"]},{"id":"2-56","n":56,"type":"mcq","q":"In the MUSEUM MINI CASE, dividing museum attendees into frequent users, less frequent users, and non-users is known as ________.","answer":2,"exp":"Dividing visitors into frequent, less frequent and non-users is usage-rate segmentation.","options":["geographic segmentation","benefit segmentation","usage rate segmentation","sociocultural segmentation","psychographic segmentation"]},{"id":"2-63","n":63,"type":"tf","q":"Targeting is the process of dividing a market into subsets of consumers with common needs or characteristics.","answer":false,"exp":"FALSE. Dividing the market into subsets is SEGMENTATION. Targeting is choosing which of those segments to pursue."},{"id":"2-64","n":64,"type":"tf","q":"When Qantas Airlines offers First Class, Business Class, Premium Economy, and Economy, with different features and amenities for each segment, they are engaged in positioning.","answer":true,"exp":"TRUE. Chapter 2: each Qantas class has its own features and price, which positions it distinctly for its segment."},{"id":"2-66","n":66,"type":"tf","q":"Positioning is the process by which a company creates a distinct image and identity for its products, services, and brands in consumers' minds.","answer":true,"exp":"TRUE. That is the definition of positioning, shown by the airline classes in Chapter 2."},{"id":"2-67","n":67,"type":"tf","q":"Marketers use segmentation research to identify the most appropriate media in which to place advertisements.","answer":true,"exp":"TRUE. Chapter 2 (Reachable Market Segments) shows that segmentation research tells marketers which media each segment uses, for example stay-at-home vs working mothers on smartphones.","depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 2, p. 27","page":27},{"id":"2-68","n":68,"type":"tf","q":"Most marketers prefer to target consumer segments that are relatively stable in terms of demographic and psychological factors and that have needs that are likely to grow larger over time.","answer":true,"exp":"TRUE. Effective segments are identifiable, profitable and reachable. The textbook adds that they should be stable and growing.","depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 2, p. 37","page":37},{"id":"2-69","n":69,"type":"tf","q":"Every segment that can be identified is inherently viable and profitable as a basis for targeted marketing.","answer":false,"exp":"FALSE. A segment must ALSO be profitable and reachable. Being identifiable is not enough."},{"id":"2-70","n":70,"type":"tf","q":"Personality traits and cultural values are consumer-intrinsic cognitive factors.","answer":true,"exp":"TRUE. Chapter 2: personality traits and cultural values are consumer-intrinsic AND cognitive (abstract) factors."},{"id":"2-71","n":71,"type":"tf","q":"Geographic characteristics serve as the core of almost all segmentations.","answer":false,"exp":"FALSE. DEMOGRAPHICS are the core of most segmentation, because they determine needs for products."},{"id":"2-72","n":72,"type":"tf","q":"Demographic variables can reveal ongoing trends that signal business opportunities, such as shifts in age and income distribution.","answer":true,"exp":"TRUE. Demographic data shows trends such as an aging population or rising income, and these trends signal opportunities.","depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 2, p. 28","page":28},{"id":"2-73","n":73,"type":"tf","q":"When it comes to financial investments people who are older and closer to retirement tend to be more cautious.","answer":true,"exp":"TRUE. Age influences buying priorities. Older people near retirement are more cautious with investments."},{"id":"2-74","n":74,"type":"tf","q":"Consumers in different social classes tend to vary in terms of values, product preferences, and buying habits.","answer":true,"exp":"TRUE. Social class affects values, product preferences and buying habits."},{"id":"2-75","n":75,"type":"tf","q":"The family life cycle is a classification of the phases most families go through, and each stage represents an important target segment to many marketers.","answer":true,"exp":"TRUE. The family life cycle describes the stages families go through. Each stage changes what the family consumes, so each stage is a target segment.","depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 2, p. 29","page":29},{"id":"2-76","n":76,"type":"tf","q":"Education, occupation, and income tend to be closely correlated.","answer":true,"exp":"TRUE. Education, occupation and income are closely related, which is why they are combined into the social class index.","depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 2, p. 28","page":28},{"id":"2-77","n":77,"type":"tf","q":"A composite variable that includes marital status, size of family, age of family members (focusing on the age of the oldest or youngest child), and employment status of the head of household classifies the family into a \"typical\" stage.","answer":true,"exp":"TRUE. The family life cycle is a composite of marital status, family size, children's ages and employment status.","depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 2, p. 29","page":29},{"id":"2-78","n":78,"type":"tf","q":"Social Class is the division of members of a society into a hierarchy of distinct status classes, so that members of each class have relatively the same status and members of all other classes have either higher or lower status.","answer":true,"exp":"TRUE. That is the definition of social class as a status hierarchy.","depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 2, p. 29","page":29},{"id":"2-80","n":80,"type":"tf","q":"Culturally distinct segments cannot be prospects for the same product and can only be targeted efficiently with unique promotional appeals for unique products.","answer":false,"exp":"FALSE. Chapter 2 says the opposite: marketers may target culturally distinct segments with the SAME product, using different promotional appeals."},{"id":"2-82","n":82,"type":"tf","q":"P$YCLE segments consumers based on the household's lifestage class.","answer":false,"exp":"FALSE. P$YCLE segments households by FINANCIAL behavior (wealth and income). LifeStage groups belong to PRIZM.","depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 2, p. 33","page":33},{"id":"2-83","n":83,"type":"tf","q":"Colgate, probably the world's most sophisticated marketer of personal care products, markets its offerings according to consumers' need in caring for their teeth and maintaining oral hygiene.","answer":true,"exp":"TRUE. Colgate segments by the benefits consumers want in oral care."},{"id":"2-84","n":84,"type":"tf","q":"Consumers belonging to the VALS segment called Believers are motivated by desire for achievement and have moderate resources, so they are trendy and seek approval from others.","answer":false,"exp":"FALSE. Believers are motivated by IDEALS and have low resources. They are conservative and traditional. The trendy approval-seekers described are Strivers.","depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 2, p. 31","page":31},{"id":"2-86","n":86,"type":"tf","q":"Showrooming sends promotional alerts to the smartphones of customers who opt-in when the customers enter or near the store.","answer":false,"exp":"FALSE. That describes GEOFENCING. Showrooming means checking products in a store and then buying online.","depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 2, p. 40","page":40},{"id":"2-87","n":87,"type":"tf","q":"Web crawlers are programs that capture content across the Internet and transmit it to the data broker's servers.","answer":true,"exp":"TRUE. That is the definition of web crawlers in Chapter 2."},{"id":"2-88","n":88,"type":"tf","q":"Geofencing consists of sending promotional alerts to the smartphones of customers who are near or had entered the store.","answer":true,"exp":"TRUE. That is geofencing.","depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 2, p. 40","page":40},{"id":"2-89","n":89,"type":"tf","q":"An impression is a customer that becomes available for real-time bidding.","answer":true,"exp":"TRUE. In the ad exchange, ad impressions go into a pool and are sold through real-time bidding.","depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 2, p. 40","page":40}],"book":{"first":26,"last":47,"count":22}},{"id":3,"title":"Consumer Motivation and Personality","exam":"midterm","slides":[{"img":"slides/ch3/01.jpg","title":"Consumer Behavior","text":"Consumer Behavior Twelfth Edition, Global Edition Chapter 3 Consumer Motivation and Personality Copyright © 2019 Pearson Education, Ltd. All Rights Reserved."},{"img":"slides/ch3/02.jpg","title":"Learning Objectives (1 of 2)","text":"Learning Objectives (1 of 2) 3.1 To understand how motives, needs, and goals shape consumer behavior. 3.2 To understand the systems of classifying needs. 3.3 To understand the impact of hidden motives on consumer behavior. 3.4 To understand why and how consumers use technology."},{"img":"slides/ch3/03.jpg","title":"Learning Objectives (2 of 2)","text":"Learning Objectives (2 of 2) 3.5 To understand personality development. 3.6 To understand how personality traits shape consumer behavior. 3.7 To understand brand personification. 3.8 To understand the impact of self-image on consumer behavior."},{"img":"slides/ch3/04.jpg","title":"Motivation","text":"Motivation Defined The driving force within individuals that impels them to act."},{"img":"slides/ch3/05.jpg","title":"The Motivation Process","text":"The Motivation Process"},{"img":"slides/ch3/06.jpg","title":"Example: Brand Personification","text":"Example: Brand Personification Source: Procter & Gamble Co."},{"img":"slides/ch3/07.jpg","title":"Learning Objective 3.1","text":"Learning Objective 3.1 3.1 To understand how motives, needs, and goals shape consumer behavior."},{"img":"slides/ch3/08.jpg","title":"Needs and Goals","text":"Needs and Goals Needs Biogenic Psychogenic Goals Generic Product-specific"},{"img":"slides/ch3/09.jpg","title":"Need Arousal","text":"Need Arousal Physiological arousal Cognitive arousal Source: The Kraft Heinz Company"},{"img":"slides/ch3/10.jpg","title":"Selecting Goals","text":"Selecting Goals Factors personal experiences and knowledge physical capacity cultural norms and values goal accessibility Approach objects Avoidance objects"},{"img":"slides/ch3/11.jpg","title":"Factors That Motivate Shopping","text":"Factors That Motivate Shopping Seeking specific goods Recreational shopping Activity-specific shopping Demand-specific shopping"},{"img":"slides/ch3/12.jpg","title":"Cannot Attain Goals?","text":"Cannot Attain Goals? Substitute goals Frustration Defense mechanisms"},{"img":"slides/ch3/13.jpg","title":"Frustration and Defense Mechanisms","text":"Frustration and Defense Mechanisms Defined Frustration is the feeling that results from failure to achieve a goal, and defense mechanisms are cognitive and behavioral ways to handle frustration."},{"img":"slides/ch3/14.jpg","title":"Defense Mechanisms","text":"Defense Mechanisms Aggression Rationalization Regression Projection Daydreaming Identification Withdrawal"},{"img":"slides/ch3/15.jpg","title":"Which Defense Mechanism is Used?","text":"Which Defense Mechanism is Used? Source: Rami Hanafi, Viewmasters/Ving"},{"img":"slides/ch3/16.jpg","title":"Learning Objective 3.2","text":"Learning Objective 3.2 3.2 To understand the systems of classifying needs."},{"img":"slides/ch3/17.jpg","title":"Murray’s Psychogenic Needs","text":"Murray’s Psychogenic Needs Table 3.1 Strategic Applications Of Murray’s List Need Illustrative Characteristics Promotional Applications Achievement: accomplish tasks, succeed, and overcome obstacles. Do the best and work hard in any undertaking. Be able to do things better than others. Messages that encourage and illustrate success(e.g., advertising education). Exhibition: shock or thrill others and be the center of attention. Tell amusing jokes at parties. Say things that others regard as witty and clever. Messages showing attention from others when they notice one’s possessions (e.g.,expensive cars). Affiliation: spend time, form strong friendships and attachments with others. Be loyal to and share things with friends. Help friends in trouble. Be confided in by others and told about their troubles. Messages showing people enjoying themselves in large groups (e.g., vacations, shopping situations). Power/Dominance: control, influence, and lead others. Seek leadership in groups. Supervise and direct the actions of others. Messages showing actual or symbolic dominance (e.g., being a chief executive; owninga powerful car). Change: seek new experiences and avoid routine. Doing new and different activities, like eating in new restaurants, going on trips, and avoiding conventional situations. Messages stressing novelty, uniqueness, and breaking with routines (e.g., adventure travel and active vacations). Order: keeping things neat and organized. Planning and organizing the details in any undertaking. Setting definite times for activities. Promoting devices that save space and keep things firmly in place (e.g., dividers and organizers for closets, drawers, and garages). Source: Data based on the works of Henry Murray"},{"img":"slides/ch3/18.jpg","title":"Appeal to Which Need? (1 of 2)","text":"Appeal to Which Need? (1 of 2) Source: Photo courtesy of The Container Store"},{"img":"slides/ch3/19.jpg","title":"Maslow’s Hierarchy of Needs","text":"Maslow’s Hierarchy of Needs"},{"img":"slides/ch3/20.jpg","title":"Appeal to Which Need? (2 of 2)","text":"Appeal to Which Need? (2 of 2) Source: BlueFox.io"},{"img":"slides/ch3/21.jpg","title":"Validity and Applications","text":"Validity and Applications Major problem: cannot be tested empirically Western culture; other societies rank needs differently Goods and services satisfy each need level Different appeals for the same product can be based on different needs"},{"img":"slides/ch3/22.jpg","title":"Discussion Questions (1 of 2)","text":"Discussion Questions (1 of 2) What are three types of products related to more then one level of Maslow’s Hierarchy of Needs? For each type of product, consider two brands. How do marketers attempt to differentiate their product from the competition?"},{"img":"slides/ch3/23.jpg","title":"Learning Objective 3.3","text":"Learning Objective 3.3 3.3 To understand the impact of hidden motives."},{"img":"slides/ch3/24.jpg","title":"Motivational Research","text":"Motivational Research Defined A “term of art” that refers to qualitative studies conducted by Dr. Ernest Dichter in the 1950s and 1960s, which were designed to uncover consumers’ subconscious or hidden motivations in the context of buying and consumption"},{"img":"slides/ch3/25.jpg","title":"Examples of Insights","text":"Examples of Insights Cigarettes and life savers – sexual symbolism Convertible cars – surrogate mistresses Baking cakes – reproductive yearnings"},{"img":"slides/ch3/26.jpg","title":"Projective Techniques","text":"Projective Techniques Projective techniques Storytelling Sentence Completion Thematic Apperception Test Picture Drawing Photo Sorts"},{"img":"slides/ch3/27.jpg","title":"Learning Objective 3.4","text":"Learning Objective 3.4 3.4 To understand why and how consumers use technology."},{"img":"slides/ch3/28.jpg","title":"Motives for Online Interactions","text":"Motives for Online Interactions Interest in buying and comparing products’ features Personalizing products is fun Desire for good customer service Win prizes and receive free samples"},{"img":"slides/ch3/29.jpg","title":"Learning Objective 3.5","text":"Learning Objective 3.5 3.5 To understand personality development."},{"img":"slides/ch3/30.jpg","title":"Personality","text":"Personality Heredity and early childhood experiences? Social and environmental influences? Unified whole versus. specific traits Reflects Individual Differences No two individuals are exactly alike Personality enables marketers to categorize consumers into different groups Consistent and enduring, but can change"},{"img":"slides/ch3/31.jpg","title":"Three Approaches","text":"Three Approaches Freudian concepts Neo-Freudian premises Measuring distinct traits"},{"img":"slides/ch3/32.jpg","title":"C A D Scale","text":"C A D Scale Defined The C A D scale measures the extent to which individuals are Compliant, Aggressive, and Detached"},{"img":"slides/ch3/33.jpg","title":"Learning Objective 3.6","text":"Learning Objective 3.6 3.6 To understand how personality traits shape consumer behavior."},{"img":"slides/ch3/34.jpg","title":"Personality Traits","text":"Personality Traits Innovators or laggards Close-minded vs. Open-minded (Dogmatism) Conformity vs. Individuality (Inner- vs. Other-directed; Need for uniqueness Table 3.3 High Need for Uniqueness When I travel, I’m always seeking out unusual gifts for myself. I’m happy when other people tell me that my taste is ”different” and ”uncommon.” I work at maintaining my own unique persona. Some of my acquaintances think I’m somewhat of a weirdo in my seeking to be different. Standing out and being different is important to me. I stop buying brands when everyone starts to buy them. Being different is my own personal trademark."},{"img":"slides/ch3/35.jpg","title":"Other Personality Factors","text":"Other Personality Factors Optimum stimulation level (O S L) Sensation seeking, Novelty seeking Need for Cognition Visualizers versus. Verbalizers Materialism Compulsions and Fixations Ethnocentrism Source: Duralee Fabrics, L T D."},{"img":"slides/ch3/36.jpg","title":"Learning Objective 3.7","text":"Learning Objective 3.7 3.7 To understand brand personification."},{"img":"slides/ch3/37.jpg","title":"Brand Personality","text":"Brand Personality Attachment and avoidance anxiety Underlying dimensions of brand personality Excitement Sophistication Affection Popularity Competence Product personality and gender Product personality and geography"},{"img":"slides/ch3/38.jpg","title":"Learning Objective 3.8","text":"Learning Objective 3.8 3.8 To understand the impact of self-image on consumer behavior."},{"img":"slides/ch3/39.jpg","title":"Discussion Questions (2 of 2)","text":"Discussion Questions (2 of 2) How are possessions an extension of the self? How do consumers use self-altering products? What are the two types of vanity? How does vanity shape consumption behavior?"},{"img":"slides/ch3/40.jpg","title":"Altering the Self","text":"Altering the Self"}],"questions":[{"id":"3-1","n":1,"type":"mcq","q":"The driving force within individuals that impels them to action is known as ________.","answer":2,"exp":"Chapter 3 defines motivation as the driving force within individuals that impels them to act.","options":["a goal","tension","motivation","a need","personality"]},{"id":"3-2","n":2,"type":"mcq","q":"Motivation is produced by a state of tension, which exists as the result of ________.","answer":4,"exp":"The motivation process in Chapter 3 runs: unfulfilled needs, wants and desires create TENSION, tension creates a drive, the drive leads to behavior, and the behavior leads to goal fulfillment. So tension comes from unfulfilled needs.","options":["peer pressure","environmental forces","satisfied desires","unrealized desires","unfulfilled needs"]},{"id":"3-5","n":5,"type":"mcq","q":"The physiological needs for food, water, and air are called ________ needs.","answer":1,"exp":"Biogenic needs are the physiological needs for food, water, air and shelter. We are born with them, so they are INNATE (primary) needs.","options":["secondary","innate","acculturated","psychogenic","acquired"],"depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 3, p. 50","page":50},{"id":"3-6","n":6,"type":"mcq","q":"Needs that we learn in response to our parents, social environment, and interactions with the environment are called ________ needs.","answer":3,"exp":"Psychogenic needs are learned from our parents, culture and social environment. Examples are needs for esteem, prestige and affection. They are psychological (acquired) needs, not innate.","options":["primary","biogenic","acculturated","psychological","innate"],"depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 3, p. 50","page":50},{"id":"3-7","n":7,"type":"mcq","q":"________ are the sought-after results of motivated behavior.","answer":1,"exp":"Goals are the sought-after results of motivated behavior. Needs drive the behavior, and goals are where it is heading.","options":["Motivations","Goals","Rewards","Behaviors","Targets"]},{"id":"3-8","n":8,"type":"mcq","q":"________ are outcomes that consumers seek in order to fulfill their physiological and psychological needs.","answer":2,"exp":"Generic goals are general outcomes that satisfy physiological or psychological needs, for example 'I want a sandwich'.","options":["Broad-spectrum goals","Objective goals","Generic goals","Subjective goals","Product-specific goals"],"depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 3, p. 50","page":50},{"id":"3-9","n":9,"type":"mcq","q":"________ are sought outcomes to be achieved by using a given product or service.","answer":4,"exp":"Product-specific goals are outcomes reached through a specific product or brand, for example 'I want a Subway turkey sandwich'.","options":["General goals","Objective goals","Generic goals","Subjective goals","Product-specific goals"],"depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 3, p. 50","page":50},{"id":"3-10","n":10,"type":"mcq","q":"Barry wants a sandwich for lunch. Tom wants a turkey sandwich with lettuce, tomato, and mayonnaise from Subway. Barry has a(n) ________, whereas Tom has a(n) ________.","answer":0,"exp":"Barry wants 'a sandwich' (a generic goal). Tom wants a specific Subway sandwich (a product-specific goal). Chapter 3 lists both types.","options":["generic goal; product-specific goal","objective goal; subjective goal","product-specific goal; objective goal","subjective goal; generic goal","product-specific goal; generic goal"],"depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 3, p. 50","page":50},{"id":"3-11","n":11,"type":"mcq","q":"Jill gets good grades because her parents will punish her if she doesn't receive at least a 3.5 GPA. Julia gets good grades because she likes the sense of accomplishment she feels when she gets a straight-A report card. Jill's goal is referred to as a(n) ________, whereas Julia's goal is referred to as a(n) ________.","answer":3,"exp":"Chapter 3 lists approach objects and avoidance objects. Jill is moving AWAY from punishment, which is an avoidance object. Julia is moving TOWARD the feeling of accomplishment, which is an approach object.","options":["approach object; avoidance object","avoidance object; defensive object","positive object; negative object","avoidance object; approach object","physical object; psychological object"],"depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 3, p. 51","page":51},{"id":"3-12","n":12,"type":"mcq","q":"A negative goal is one from which behavior is directed away, and is often referred to as a(n) ________.","answer":4,"exp":"A negative goal that behavior moves away from is an avoidance object.","options":["declining object","approach object","interactive object","autonomous object","avoidance object"],"depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 3, p. 51","page":51},{"id":"3-13","n":13,"type":"mcq","q":"Needs and goals are ________; neither exists without the other.","answer":1,"exp":"Needs and goals are interdependent. A need pushes you toward a goal, and a goal only matters because it satisfies a need.","options":["independent","interdependent","interactive","autonomous","mutually exclusive"]},{"id":"3-14","n":14,"type":"mcq","q":"The category of shopping motivations where consumers do not have an urgent-product need in mind, but go shopping for the personal enjoyment of shopping is known as ________.","answer":0,"exp":"Recreational shopping is shopping for the enjoyment of it, without an urgent need for a product.","options":["recreational shopping","activity-specific shopping","fill-in shopping","demand-specific shopping","self-image shopping"],"depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 3, p. 52","page":52},{"id":"3-17","n":17,"type":"mcq","q":"A(n) ________ can take the place of a primary goal when an individual cannot attain a specific goal or type of goal that is expected to satisfy certain needs.","answer":2,"exp":"Chapter 3: when a goal cannot be reached, people may adopt a substitute goal that replaces the primary one.","options":["specific goal","biogenic goal","substitute goal","secondary goal","objective goal"]},{"id":"3-18","n":18,"type":"mcq","q":"Failure to achieve a goal often results in feelings of","answer":4,"exp":"Chapter 3: frustration is the feeling that results from failing to achieve a goal.","options":["achievement","aspiration","self-awareness","personal promotion","frustration"]},{"id":"3-19","n":19,"type":"mcq","q":"Aggression and rationalization are examples of ________ that people sometimes adopt to protect their egos from feelings of failure when they do not attain their goals.","answer":1,"exp":"Aggression and rationalization appear on the defense mechanisms list. Chapter 3 defines defense mechanisms as the ways people handle frustration and protect the ego.","options":["fulfillment mechanisms","defense mechanisms","substitute goals","subjective criteria","secondary behaviors"]},{"id":"3-20","n":20,"type":"mcq","q":"An individual may redefine a frustrating situation by assigning blame for his or her own failures and inabilities on other objects or persons. This is known as ________.","answer":3,"exp":"Projection means blaming other people or objects for your own failure.","options":["aggression","rationalization","withdrawal","projection","regression"],"depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 3, p. 53","page":53},{"id":"3-21","n":21,"type":"mcq","q":"People sometimes resolve frustration by inventing plausible reasons for being unable to attain their goals or deciding that the goal is not really worth pursuing. This is known as ________.","answer":1,"exp":"Rationalization means inventing believable reasons for failing, or deciding the goal was not worth it anyway.","options":["aggression","rationalization","withdrawal","projection","regression"],"depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 3, p. 53","page":53},{"id":"3-23","n":23,"type":"mcq","q":"According to Maslow's hierarchy of needs, protection, order, and stability are examples of ________.","answer":3,"exp":"On Maslow's pyramid, Safety and Security Needs = protection, order and stability.","options":["self-actualization needs","egoistic needs","social needs","safety and security needs","physiological needs"]},{"id":"3-24","n":24,"type":"mcq","q":"Savings accounts, insurance policies, and education are all means by which individuals satisfy the need for ________.","answer":2,"exp":"Savings, insurance and education all protect your future. They meet the Safety and Security level.","options":["self-actualization","self-esteem","safety and security","social acceptance","self-fulfillment"],"depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 3, p. 56","page":56},{"id":"3-25","n":25,"type":"mcq","q":"According to Maslow, ________ are the first and most basic level of human needs.","answer":1,"exp":"Physiological needs (food, water, air, shelter) are the base of Maslow's pyramid.","options":["egoistic needs","physiological needs","self-actualization needs","safety and security needs","social needs"]},{"id":"3-26","n":26,"type":"mcq","q":"Almost all personal care and grooming products, as well as most clothes, are bought to satisfy ________.","answer":2,"exp":"Grooming products and clothes are mostly bought to be accepted and liked by others. That is the Social level (affection, friendship, belonging) in Chapter 3.","options":["growth needs","physiological needs","social needs","self-actualization needs","safety and security needs"],"depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 3, p. 51","page":51},{"id":"3-27","n":27,"type":"mcq","q":"Egoistic needs can take either an inward or an outward orientation, or both. Inwardly directed ego needs reflect an individual's need for ________.","answer":2,"exp":"Ego needs include prestige, status and self-esteem. INWARD ego needs are about how you see yourself: self-esteem, self-acceptance, independence.","options":["reputation","prestige","self-esteem","recognition from others","status"],"book":"Textbook: inwardly directed ego needs = self-acceptance, self-esteem, success, independence. Outwardly directed = prestige, reputation, status, recognition from others.","depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 3, p. 56","page":56},{"id":"3-28","n":28,"type":"mcq","q":"Egoistic needs can take either an inward or an outward orientation, or both. Outwardly directed ego needs reflect an individual's need for ________.","answer":2,"exp":"Ego needs include prestige and status. OUTWARD ego needs are about how others see you: reputation, prestige, recognition.","options":["self-acceptance","personal satisfaction with a job well done","reputation","independence","self-esteem"],"book":"Textbook: outwardly directed ego needs = prestige, reputation, status, recognition from others.","depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 3, p. 56","page":56},{"id":"3-29","n":29,"type":"mcq","q":"According to Maslow's hierarchy-of-needs theory, the need for ________ refers to an individual's desire to fulfill his or her potential.","answer":0,"exp":"Self-actualization (self-fulfillment), at the top of the pyramid in Chapter 3, is the desire to reach your full potential.","options":["self-actualization","safety and security","physiological well-being","social acceptance","egoistic fulfillment"]},{"id":"3-30","n":30,"type":"mcq","q":"People with a high ________ tend to be more self-confident, actively research their environments, and value feedback.","answer":3,"exp":"Murray's list describes Achievement as accomplishing tasks, succeeding and overcoming obstacles. People with high achievement needs are self-confident, research their environment and value feedback.","options":["need for prestige","need for power","need for affiliation","need for achievement","need for affection"]},{"id":"3-31","n":31,"type":"mcq","q":"Oftentimes consumer research respondents may be unaware of their motives or are unwilling to reveal them when asked directly. In such situations, researchers use ________ to delve into the consumer's unconscious or hidden motivations.","answer":1,"exp":"When people can't or won't reveal their motives, researchers use projective techniques: storytelling, sentence completion, TAT, picture drawing and photo sorts.","options":["quantitative techniques","projective techniques","Likert scales","telephone surveys","none of the above"],"depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 3, p. 59","page":59},{"id":"3-32","n":32,"type":"mcq","q":"The psychoanalyst that adapted Freud's psychoanalytical techniques to study motivations and consumer buying habits was ________.","answer":1,"exp":"Chapter 3: motivational research refers to the qualitative studies of Dr. Ernest Dichter in the 1950s–60s, which aimed to uncover hidden motives.","options":["Abraham Maslow","Ernest Dichter","Henry Murray","Karen Horney","Henry Ford"]},{"id":"3-33","n":33,"type":"mcq","q":"________ is based on the premise that consumers are not always aware of the reasons for their actions.","answer":1,"exp":"Motivational research was designed to uncover consumers' subconscious or hidden motivations. It assumes people aren't always aware of why they buy.","options":["Psychological research","Motivational research","Physiological research","Market research","none of the above"]},{"id":"3-34","n":34,"type":"mcq","q":"________ refers to a person's unique psychological makeup and how it consistently influences the way a person responds to his/her environment.","answer":2,"exp":"Personality is a person's unique inner makeup that consistently shapes how they respond to the environment.","options":["Self-image","Consumer image","Personality","Mirror image","none of the above"]},{"id":"3-35","n":35,"type":"mcq","q":"The emphasis in the definition of personality is on inner characteristics, which are ________.","answer":2,"exp":"Chapter 3: personality reflects individual differences, because no two individuals are exactly alike. Inner characteristics are the ones that make a person different from others.","options":["those characteristics of an individual that are not readily apparent to others","those characteristics which individuals try to hide from others","those characteristics that distinguish one individual from others","those characteristics that make one individual similar to others","those characteristics that constitute the individual's ideal self-image"]},{"id":"3-36","n":36,"type":"mcq","q":"Knowing why consumers interact with brands online allows marketers to increase engagement that ultimately leads to sales. What motivates consumers to interact with brands over the Internet?","answer":4,"exp":"Chapter 3 lists the motives for online interactions: buying and comparing features, enjoying personalizing products, good customer service, and winning prizes and samples. So 'all of the above'.","options":["interested in buying and comparing products' features online","find personalizing products fun","receiving good customer service","winning prizes and receiving free product samples","all of the above"]},{"id":"3-37","n":37,"type":"mcq","q":"In the study of personality, three distinct properties are of central importance. They are ________.","answer":0,"exp":"Chapter 3 gives the three properties of personality: it reflects individual differences, it is consistent and enduring, and it can change.","options":["that personality reflects individual differences, personality is consistent and enduring, and personality can change","that personality reflects similarities between individuals, personality is consistent and enduring, and personality can change","that personality reflects individual differences, personality is consistent and enduring, and personality does not change","that personality reflects similarities between individuals, personality is consistent and enduring, and personality does not change","that personality reflects individual differences, personality is inconsistent and fleeting, and personality can change"]},{"id":"3-38","n":38,"type":"mcq","q":"The mother who comments about her teenage son, that her child \"has been impulsive from the day he was born,\" is supporting the contention that personality ________.","answer":0,"exp":"'Impulsive since the day he was born' means the trait has stayed the same over time. Personality is consistent and ENDURING.","options":["is enduring","can change","reflects individual differences","is more apparent in childhood than adulthood","develops as a result of social circumstances"]},{"id":"3-39","n":39,"type":"mcq","q":"Stacy notes that, after her freshman year of college, her daughter is more mature and is willing to listen to points of view other than those with which she agrees. This supports the contention that personality ________.","answer":2,"exp":"The daughter matured and became more open-minded, so her personality CAN CHANGE.","options":["is consistent","reflects individual differences","can change","is more apparent in childhood than in adulthood","is enduring"]},{"id":"3-40","n":40,"type":"mcq","q":"Sigmund Freud's psychoanalytic theory of personality is built on the premise that ________ are at the heart of human motivations and personality.","answer":4,"exp":"Freudian concepts say that unconscious needs and drives are at the heart of motivation and personality.","options":["social relationships","conscious decision-making processes","efforts to avoid anxiety","efforts to overcome inferiority","unconscious needs or drives"],"book":"Textbook: Freud's theory puts unconscious needs or drives, especially biological drives, at the center of human motivation.","depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 3, p. 58","page":58},{"id":"3-41","n":41,"type":"mcq","q":"According to Freud's theories, the ________ is a conceptualization of the basic biogenic needs for which the individual seeks immediate satisfaction without concern for the specific means of satisfaction.","answer":1,"exp":"In Freud's theory, the ID is the 'warehouse' of basic biogenic drives that want immediate satisfaction, regardless of how.","options":["ego","id","super-id","self-image","superego"],"book":"Textbook: Id = primitive, impulsive drives. Superego = internal moral code. Ego = the conscious control that balances the two.","depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 3, p. 61","page":61},{"id":"3-42","n":42,"type":"mcq","q":"Using sexual imagery to sell a product primarily appeals to Freud's conceptualization of the ________.","answer":3,"exp":"Sexual imagery appeals to primitive, pleasure-seeking drives. That is the ID in Freud's theory.","options":["ego","self-image","superego","id","super-id"],"book":"Textbook: Id = primitive, impulsive drives (thirst, hunger, sex) seeking immediate satisfaction.","depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 3, p. 61","page":61},{"id":"3-43","n":43,"type":"mcq","q":"According to Freud's theories, the ________ is responsible for ensuring that the individual satisfies needs in a socially acceptable fashion.","answer":0,"exp":"In Freud's theory, the SUPEREGO is the moral and ethical code. It makes sure needs are satisfied in socially acceptable ways.","options":["superego","id","ego","super-id","self-image"],"depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 3, p. 61","page":61},{"id":"3-44","n":44,"type":"mcq","q":"According to Freud's theories, the ________ functions as an internal monitor that helps balance the impulses stimulated by biogenic needs and the constraints applied by the sociocultural setting.","answer":2,"exp":"In Freud's theory, the EGO is the conscious internal monitor that balances the id's impulses against the superego's social and moral limits.","options":["super-id","self-image","ego","superego","id"],"depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 3, p. 61","page":61},{"id":"3-45","n":45,"type":"mcq","q":"Neo-Freudian theorists believe that ________.","answer":2,"exp":"Neo-Freudian premises say that SOCIAL RELATIONSHIPS, not only unconscious drives, are fundamental to how personality forms.","options":["consumption situations are extensions of the consumer's personality","human drives are largely unconscious","social relationships are fundamental to the formation and development of personality","consumers are primarily unaware of their true reasons for making decisions","consumer purchases are a reflection of an individual's personality"],"depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 3, p. 61","page":61},{"id":"3-46","n":46,"type":"mcq","q":"Alfred Adler, a neo-Freudian theorist, believed that ________.","answer":2,"exp":"Adler, a neo-Freudian, believed people seek rational goals, which he called 'style of life', and try to overcome feelings of inferiority.","options":["unconscious needs and drives are at the heart of human motivation","people continuously attempt to establish significant and rewarding relationships with others","human beings are seeking to attain various rational goals, which he called style of life","personality is measured in terms of specific psychological characteristics called traits","personality is largely impacted by child-parent relationships and the individual's desire to conquer feelings of anxiety"],"depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 3, p. 62","page":62},{"id":"3-47","n":47,"type":"mcq","q":"According to Karen Horney, ________ individuals desire independence, self-reliance, and self-sufficiency.","answer":3,"exp":"In the CAD scale, DETACHED people move away from others. They want independence, self-reliance and freedom from obligations.","options":["compliant","subversive","aggressive","detached","obtrusive"],"depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 3, p. 62","page":62},{"id":"3-48","n":48,"type":"mcq","q":"Consumer research using Karen Horney's CAD theory found that ________ individuals were less likely to be brand loyal and more likely to try different brands.","answer":2,"exp":"Detached personalities on the CAD scale value independence, so they are less brand loyal and more willing to try different brands.","options":["submissive","aggressive","detached","compliant","subversive"],"depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 3, p. 62","page":62},{"id":"3-49","n":49,"type":"mcq","q":"Recent research using Karen Horney's CAD theory found that ________ personalities tend to prefer name-brand products such as Bayer aspirin.","answer":3,"exp":"Compliant personalities on the CAD scale move toward others. They prefer well-known, name brands such as Bayer.","options":["submissive","aggressive","detached","compliant","subversive"],"depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 3, p. 62","page":62},{"id":"3-50","n":50,"type":"mcq","q":"The degree of a consumer's willingness to adopt new products and services shortly after they have been introduced is known as ________.","answer":1,"exp":"Chapter 3 begins with 'innovators or laggards'. Consumer innovativeness is how willing a person is to adopt new products soon after launch.","options":["consumer ethnocentrism","consumer innovativeness","consumer understanding","consumer perception","consumer materialism"],"depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 3, p. 63","page":63},{"id":"3-52","n":52,"type":"mcq","q":"The degree of rigidity towards information and opinion contradictory to one's beliefs and views - or close-mindedness; the opposite of being open-minded - is known as ________.","answer":0,"exp":"Dogmatism = close-mindedness: rigidity toward information that contradicts your beliefs.","options":["dogmatism","inner-directedness","social character","innovativeness","need for uniqueness"]},{"id":"3-53","n":53,"type":"mcq","q":"Which of the following is NOT a statement that is illustrative of opinions of an individual with a high need for uniqueness?","answer":4,"exp":"Table 3.3 in Chapter 3 lists ALL of these statements (unusual gifts, being told your taste is 'different', maintaining a unique persona, being seen as weird) as signs of a HIGH need for uniqueness.","options":["I like to seek out unusual gifts for myself.","I enjoy it when others tell me my taste is different or uncommon.","I work at maintaining my own unique persona.","Some of my acquaintances think I'm somewhat weird in my seeking to be different.","All the above are illustrative of an individual with a high need for uniqueness"]},{"id":"3-55","n":55,"type":"mcq","q":"________ define themselves by acquiring possessions.","answer":2,"exp":"Materialism: highly materialistic consumers define themselves and their success through their possessions.","options":["Object visualizers","Spatial visualizers","Highly materialistic consumers","Verbalizers","Highly dogmatic consumers"],"depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 3, p. 66","page":66},{"id":"3-56","n":56,"type":"mcq","q":"Georgiana collects stationery from hotels and restaurants she has visited around the world. She is passionate about her collection and goes out of her way to acquire more, even if she needs to plan a special trip to do so. This is an example of ________.","answer":2,"exp":"Georgiana is passionately focused on one collection and goes far out of her way to add to it. That is a fixation (fixated consumption), listed with compulsions in Chapter 3.","options":["ethnocentrism","innovativeness","fixated consumption","materialistic consumption","compulsive consumption"],"book":"Textbook: fixated consumers are passionately interested in a product category, go to great lengths to get more items, and show off their collections. This is socially acceptable, unlike compulsive consumption.","depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 3, p. 67","page":67},{"id":"3-57","n":57,"type":"mcq","q":"Which of the following is NOT a characteristic of compulsive buyers?","answer":3,"exp":"Compulsive consumption means feeling compelled to buy, buying unneeded items despite having little money, and hiding it. The claim that compulsive buyers are mostly male (75%) is NOT a characteristic.","options":["Compulsive buyers feel compelled to spend all or part of their money.","Compulsive buyers buy products they do not need despite having little money left.","Compulsive buyers feel others would be horrified if they knew about their shopping","Compulsive buyers are more likely to be males (75% of compulsive buyers).","Compulsive buyers knowingly write checks that will bounce."],"book":"Textbook: research finds compulsive buying is more common among women.","depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 3, p. 67","page":67},{"id":"3-58","n":58,"type":"mcq","q":"The likelihood of a consumer accepting or rejecting foreign-made products is known as ________.","answer":0,"exp":"Consumer ethnocentrism is the tendency to accept or reject foreign-made products.","options":["consumer ethnocentrism","consumer innovativeness","consumer understanding","consumer perception","consumer materialism"],"depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 3, p. 61","page":61},{"id":"3-60","n":60,"type":"mcq","q":"Renee likes to try on some of the newest clothes each season to see how she looks in the styles, and prefers to buy clothes that are very unusual. Renee is best described as ________.","answer":0,"exp":"Renee seeks novelty and unusual styles. People with a HIGH optimum stimulation level enjoy novel, complex and unusual experiences.","options":["having a high optimum stimulation level","highly dogmatic","highly ethnocentric","having a low optimum stimulation level","minimally ethnocentric"],"depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 3, p. 65","page":65},{"id":"3-61","n":61,"type":"mcq","q":"\"Buy American\" and \"Keep America Working\" are both taglines used by companies to attract the ________ consumer.","answer":1,"exp":"'Buy American' appeals to consumers who prefer home-country products, meaning ethnocentric consumers.","options":["dogmatic","ethnocentric","innovative","variety-seeking","compulsive"],"depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 3, p. 61","page":61},{"id":"3-62","n":62,"type":"mcq","q":"The notion of ________ suggests that consumers like traits or characteristics to different brands.","answer":0,"exp":"Brand personality: consumers give brands human-like traits, such as excitement or sophistication.","options":["brand personality","consumer dogmatism","consumer innovation","consumer need for cognition","sensation seeking"],"depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 3, p. 69","page":69},{"id":"3-63","n":63,"type":"mcq","q":"________ is communicating human features of the product in advertising.","answer":4,"exp":"Brand personification means communicating human features of a product in advertising, as Mr. Clean does in Chapter 3.","options":["Brand zealotry","Extended self","Sophistication","Color","Brand personification"]},{"id":"3-64","n":64,"type":"mcq","q":"Which of the following is NOT one of the five underlying dimensions of brand personality?","answer":4,"exp":"Chapter 3 lists the underlying dimensions of brand personality: Excitement, Sophistication, Affection, Popularity and Competence. 'Cosmopolitanism' is NOT one of them.","options":["sophistication","excitement","popularity","affection","cosmopolitanism"]},{"id":"3-65","n":65,"type":"mcq","q":"________ is how consumers actually see themselves, whereas ________ refers to how consumers would like to see themselves.","answer":1,"exp":"Actual self-image = how you actually see yourself. Ideal self-image = how you would like to see yourself. These are the self-image ideas behind Chapter 3.","options":["Ideal self-image; ideal social self-image","Actual self-image; ideal self-image","Social self-image; self-image","Actual social self-image; ideal social self-image","Ideal self-image; actual social self-image"],"book":"Textbook: four self-images: actual self-image, ideal self-image, social self-image (how you think others see you) and ideal social self-image (how you'd like others to see you).","depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 3, p. 70","page":70},{"id":"3-66","n":66,"type":"mcq","q":"________ reflects how consumers would like to see themselves, whereas ________ refers to how consumers would like others to see them.","answer":0,"exp":"Ideal self-image = how you'd like to see yourself. Ideal social self-image = how you'd like OTHERS to see you.","options":["Ideal self-image; ideal social self-image","Actual self-image; ideal self-image","Social self-image; self-image","Actual social self-image; ideal social self-image","Ideal self-image; actual social self-image"],"book":"Textbook: social self-image = how you feel others see you. Ideal social self-image = how you'd like others to see you.","depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 3, p. 71","page":71},{"id":"3-67","n":67,"type":"mcq","q":"Jenna buys a pair of sought-after, vintage Levi jeans to enrich her self-image. Which of the following is NOT one of the ways the possession of the vintage jeans might be viewed as an extension of the self?","answer":0,"exp":"Chapter 3 asks how possessions extend the self. The textbook lists the ways: actually, symbolically, by conferring status or rank, by bestowing feelings of immortality, and by giving magical powers. 'Ideally' is not one of them.","options":["ideally","actually","symbolically","by conferring status or rank","by feelings of immortality"],"book":"Textbook: possessions extend the self actually, symbolically, by conferring status or rank, by bestowing feelings of immortality, and by endowing magical powers.","depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 3, p. 71","page":71},{"id":"3-68","n":68,"type":"mcq","q":"Consumers use ________ to attempt to express new versions of themselves and/or to take on the appearances of other types of people.","answer":4,"exp":"Self-altering products, such as cosmetics, hair styles and the face filters in Chapter 3, let consumers express new versions of themselves or look like someone else.","options":["brand personification","anthropomorphism","vanity","status","self-altering products"],"depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 3, p. 71","page":71},{"id":"3-69","n":69,"type":"mcq","q":"The type of self-image that characterizes the way consumers see themselves is ________.","answer":0,"exp":"Actual self-image = the way consumers actually see themselves.","options":["actual self-image","ideal self-image","social self-image","ideal social self-image","self-image awareness"],"depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 3, p. 70","page":70},{"id":"3-70","n":70,"type":"mcq","q":"The type of self-image that characterizes the way consumers would like to see themselves is ________.","answer":1,"exp":"Ideal self-image = the way consumers would LIKE to see themselves.","options":["actual self-image","ideal self-image","social self-image","ideal social self-image","self-image awareness"],"depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 3, p. 70","page":70},{"id":"3-71","n":71,"type":"mcq","q":"The type of self-image that characterizes the way consumers feel others see them is ________.","answer":2,"exp":"Social self-image = the way consumers feel OTHERS see them.","options":["actual self-image","ideal self-image","social self-image","ideal social self-image","self-image awareness"],"depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 3, p. 71","page":71},{"id":"3-72","n":72,"type":"mcq","q":"The type of self-image that characterizes the way consumers would like others to see them is ________.","answer":3,"exp":"Ideal social self-image = the way consumers would like others to see them.","options":["actual self-image","ideal self-image","social self-image","ideal social self-image","self-image awareness"],"depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 3, p. 71","page":71},{"id":"3-75","n":75,"type":"mcq","q":"Gloria is particularly excited to try new and different group exercise classes at her gym. Gloria is probably best described as ________.","answer":3,"exp":"Gloria is excited by new and different classes. That is novelty seeking, one of the optimum-stimulation traits in Chapter 3.","options":["highly ethnocentric","highly dogmatic","inner-directed","novelty seeking","high in her need for cognition"],"depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 3, p. 65","page":65},{"id":"3-78","n":78,"type":"mcq","q":"Jenny wants a bottle of Revlon Berry Bon Bon nail polish to match her new sweater. This is an example of a ________.","answer":3,"exp":"Jenny wants a SPECIFIC brand and shade (Revlon Berry Bon Bon). That is a product-specific goal.","options":["generic goal","general goal","needs-driven goal","product-specific goal","subjective goal"]},{"id":"3-79","n":79,"type":"mcq","q":"Patti wants to paint her nails in order to be attractive. This is an example of a(n) ________ goal.","answer":3,"exp":"Patti is moving TOWARD a positive outcome (being attractive). That is an approach object.","options":["negative","avoidance objects","utilitarian","approach objects","prevention-focused"],"depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 3, p. 51","page":51},{"id":"3-84","n":84,"type":"mcq","q":"After several weeks of exercising, Peter is still not seeing the desired weight-loss results and decides that he is just too busy to go to the gym enough, and that his employer is really to blame for his failure. Which defense mechanism has Peter demonstrated?","answer":1,"exp":"Peter blames his employer for his own failure. Putting the blame on others is projection.","options":["rationalization","projection","repression","aggression","withdrawal"],"depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 3, p. 53","page":53},{"id":"3-85","n":85,"type":"tf","q":"When L'Oreal appeals to self-esteem with the tagline, \"Because you're worth it,\" they are appealing to physiological needs.","answer":false,"exp":"FALSE. 'Because you're worth it' appeals to self-esteem, which is an EGO need on Maslow's pyramid. It is not a physiological need."},{"id":"3-86","n":86,"type":"tf","q":"Marketers can take advantage of people's frustrations by portraying their products as means to resolve a particular set of frustrations.","answer":true,"exp":"TRUE. Frustration comes from failing to reach a goal. Marketers present products as ways to resolve that frustration."},{"id":"3-87","n":87,"type":"tf","q":"When the Kaplan Bar Review course tells prospective law students they can \"conquer the bar\" or achieve a goal by taking the Kaplan course, they are trying to trigger a product-specific goal.","answer":true,"exp":"TRUE. Linking a goal (passing the bar) to one specific course (Kaplan) creates a product-specific goal.","depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 3, p. 50","page":50},{"id":"3-88","n":88,"type":"tf","q":"According to Maslow, individuals seek to satisfy higher-level needs before lower-level needs emerge.","answer":false,"exp":"FALSE. Maslow says people satisfy LOWER-level needs first. Higher needs emerge only after the lower ones are met."},{"id":"3-89","n":89,"type":"tf","q":"According to Maslow, once a lower-level need is met, it never becomes dominant again.","answer":false,"exp":"FALSE. A lower need can become dominant again. If you lose your job, for example, safety needs come back. Chapter 3 also notes the hierarchy can't be tested empirically.","depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 3, p. 56","page":56},{"id":"3-90","n":90,"type":"tf","q":"The achievement need suggests that behavior is strongly influenced by the desire for friendship, acceptance, and belonging.","answer":false,"exp":"FALSE. Friendship, acceptance and belonging describe the AFFILIATION need in Murray's list. Achievement is about accomplishing tasks and succeeding."},{"id":"3-91","n":91,"type":"tf","q":"Murray's Psychogenic Needs includes power, affiliation, and achievement.","answer":true,"exp":"TRUE. Murray's list of psychogenic needs includes achievement, affiliation and power (dominance)."},{"id":"3-92","n":92,"type":"tf","q":"Researchers usually rely on a combination of various research techniques to try to establish the presence and strength of various motives.","answer":true,"exp":"TRUE. Researchers combine several techniques, such as the projective techniques in Chapter 3 together with other methods, to establish which motives are present and how strong they are."},{"id":"3-95","n":95,"type":"tf","q":"Marketers are able to change consumers' personalities to conform to their products.","answer":false,"exp":"FALSE. Personality is consistent and enduring. Marketers CAN'T change it. They match products and appeals to personality types instead."},{"id":"3-96","n":96,"type":"tf","q":"According to Freud, the superego acts to restrain or inhibit the impulsive forces of the id.","answer":true,"exp":"TRUE. In Freud's theory, the superego, our moral code, restrains the id's impulses.","depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 3, p. 61","page":61},{"id":"3-97","n":97,"type":"tf","q":"Compliant individuals are those who move against others; they desire to excel and win admiration.","answer":false,"exp":"FALSE. Compliant people move TOWARD others (they want love and approval). Moving AGAINST others to excel and win admiration is the AGGRESSIVE type.","depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 3, p. 62","page":62},{"id":"3-98","n":98,"type":"tf","q":"Personality traits are characteristics that set people apart from one another.","answer":true,"exp":"TRUE. Personality reflects individual differences. Traits are what set people apart."},{"id":"3-99","n":99,"type":"tf","q":"People who are high in dogmatism are more likely to prefer innovative products to established or traditional alternatives.","answer":false,"exp":"FALSE. Highly DOGMATIC (close-minded) people prefer established, traditional products. Open-minded people prefer innovative ones.","depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 3, p. 63","page":63},{"id":"3-100","n":100,"type":"tf","q":"Consumers whose actual lifestyles are equivalent to their OSL scores are likely to seek rest or relief.","answer":false,"exp":"FALSE. If a person's actual lifestyle MATCHES their OSL, they are satisfied. People who are UNDER-stimulated seek more stimulation, and people who are OVER-stimulated seek rest.","depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 3, p. 65","page":65},{"id":"3-102","n":102,"type":"tf","q":"Variety seeking measures a person's craving for or enjoyment of thinking.","answer":false,"exp":"FALSE. A person's craving for or enjoyment of thinking is NEED FOR COGNITION. Variety (novelty) seeking is a different trait.","depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 3, p. 66","page":66},{"id":"3-103","n":103,"type":"tf","q":"Examples of compulsive consumption include eating disorders, shopping, gambling, and alcoholism.","answer":true,"exp":"TRUE. Compulsive consumption includes compulsive shopping, gambling, eating disorders and alcoholism.","depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 3, p. 67","page":67},{"id":"3-105","n":105,"type":"tf","q":"Consumers who have strong links to particular brands see such brands as representing an aspect of themselves.","answer":true,"exp":"TRUE. Brand attachment: consumers strongly linked to a brand see it as part of themselves."},{"id":"3-106","n":106,"type":"tf","q":"People who are preoccupied with their self-worth and have a negative view of people (high anxiety and high avoidance) prefer exciting brand personalities, while those with high anxiety and a favorable view of people prefer sincere brand personalities.","answer":true,"exp":"TRUE. Chapter 3 lists attachment and avoidance anxiety as factors in brand personality. Anxious people with a negative view of others prefer exciting brands, and anxious people with a positive view prefer sincere brands.","depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 3, p. 51","page":51}],"book":{"first":48,"last":75,"count":28}},{"id":4,"title":"Consumer Perception and Positioning","exam":"midterm","slides":[{"img":"slides/ch4/01.jpg","title":"MKT301 Consumer Behavior","text":"MKT301 Consumer Behavior Chapter 4 Consumer Perception and Positioning Strategic Marketing in the Saudi Context \"Today's focus: Understanding how consumers see, notice, and interpret the world—and how brands leverage these psychological processes to build distinct positions in the mind.\""},{"img":"slides/ch4/02.jpg","title":"Perception about Saudi Arabia","text":"Perception about Saudi Arabia"},{"img":"slides/ch4/03.jpg","title":"Perception about Saudi Arabia","text":"Perception about Saudi Arabia"},{"img":"slides/ch4/04.jpg","title":"Perception beats reality","text":"Perception beats reality"},{"img":"slides/ch4/05.jpg","title":"Perception of Saudi Arabia","text":"02 Perception of Saudi Arabia Stereotypes vs. Emerging Reality Class Discussion Prompt 1 First impression: Which stereotypes usually show up first in the global mind? 2 Disruption: What specific visual or verbal cues in modern ads challenge these beliefs? 3 Positioning: How is the \"brand message\" being rebuilt through new associations? From oil economy to tech hub AlUla, Red Sea, NEOM 70% of population under 30 Digital & Innovation Tourism & Heritage Youth & Culture"},{"img":"slides/ch4/06.jpg","title":"Digital Context in Saudi Arabia","text":"99% Internet Penetration Virtually universal access shapes exposure daily 34.1M Social Media Identities Jan 2025 Data: Active engagement across platforms The Perception Algorithm \"In a digitally saturated market like KSA, algorithmic feeds determine salience. If the algorithm doesn't show it, the consumer doesn't perceive it—making digital distribution a core part of perception strategy.\" Teaching Point: Unlike traditional shelf space, digital shelf space is dynamic. Perception is now filtered through personalization engines before it even reaches the sensory organs. Source: Saudi Internet Report 2024; DataReportal Jan 2025 03 Digital Context in Saudi Arabia How high connectivity shapes what people notice and believe"},{"img":"slides/ch4/07.jpg","title":"Sensation vs. Perception","text":"Sensation vs. Perception Understanding the difference between raw data and interpreted reality 04 Sensation The Input The immediate and direct response of sensory organs to stimuli. Raw Data: Unprocessed signals Channels: Light, sound, odor, taste, texture Nature: Physiological & Objective Core Question \"What is detected?\" Perception The Meaning The process of selecting, organizing, and interpreting sensory input. Processed: Meaning is assigned Filters: Experience, expectations, motives Nature: Psychological & Subjective Core Question \"What does it mean?\" MANAGERIAL INSIGHT Two consumers can sense the exact same product (visuals, price) but perceive completely different value based on their individual context."},{"img":"slides/ch4/08.jpg","title":"Perception","text":"Perception The process by which an individual selects, organizes, and interprets stimuli into a meaningful and coherent picture of the world concepts in Perception Sensation Absolute threshold Differential threshold"},{"img":"slides/ch4/09.jpg","title":"How Perception Works (video)","text":"8"},{"img":"slides/ch4/10.jpg","title":"Sensory Cues in Marketing","text":"Sensory Cues in Marketing Perception begins before performance: The 5-Sense Framework Vision Color psychology Shape & Contrast Visual layout Saudi Context Almarai's consistent white & blue packaging signals purity, hygiene, and freshness on the shelf. Sound Sonic branding Music tempo Voice tone Saudi Context The distinct \"STC\" sonic logo at the end of ads creates instant brand recognition without visuals. Smell Ambient scent Product fragrance Freshness cues Saudi Context The aroma of Arabic coffee (Gahwa) in hotel lobbies immediately signals hospitality and luxury. Taste Flavor profile Sampling Aftertaste Saudi Context Ramadan tastings in supermarkets (e.g., Danube) allow trial to overcome risk for new food products. Touch Material texture Product weight Temperature Saudi Context The weight and matte finish of premium device boxes at Jarir implies high-tech quality. Key Insight: Conflicting sensory cues (e.g., looks good but feels cheap) damage trust. 05"},{"img":"slides/ch4/11.jpg","title":"From Sensory Stimuli to Interpretation","text":""},{"img":"slides/ch4/12.jpg","title":"The Nature of Perception","text":"7 The Nature of Perception"},{"img":"slides/ch4/13.jpg","title":"Sensory Thresholds","text":"Sensory Thresholds The science of what gets noticed and what gets missed Absolute Threshold The lowest level at which an individual can experience a sensation. It is the point where a person can detect the difference between \"something\" and \"nothing.\" Marketing Implication If your stimulus is below this level, it is effectively invisible. (e.g., A billboard text too small to read at 100km/h). Differential Threshold (J.N.D.) The minimal difference that can be detected between two similar stimuli. Governed by Weber's Law: the stronger the initial stimulus, the greater the change needed to be noticed. Strategic Rule Negative change (price hike)? Keep it below JND. Positive change (discount)? Make it above JND. 06 VS"},{"img":"slides/ch4/14.jpg","title":"Ultrasonic Ringtones (absolute threshold)","text":""},{"img":"slides/ch4/15.jpg","title":"Example: Visual Identity","text":"Example: Visual Identity"},{"img":"slides/ch4/16.jpg","title":"Sensory Adaptation","text":"08 Sensory Adaptation Why Ads Stop Working Over Time The \"Clutter\" Problem Adaptation occurs when consumers get used to repeated stimuli. As exposure increases, attention automatically drops. The brain filters out the \"noise\" to focus on what's new. The Consequence Advertising effectiveness decays even if you spend more money. High frequency without variation leads to invisibility, not impact. Class Discussion \"Think about your scrolling today. What was the last ad you simply stopped noticing because you've seen it too many times?\" Rotate Creatives Change visuals/copy every 2-3 weeks to refresh attention. Vary Channels Don't just use Instagram; mix in TikTok, outdoor, or audio. Switch Sensory Format Move from static image to video, or add sound/haptic cues. Use Unexpected Media Place ads where they aren't expected (e.g., inside games, packaging). Marketer's Playbook"},{"img":"slides/ch4/17.jpg","title":"Selective Exposure","text":"Selective Exposure Consumers actively seek out messages that reinforce their identity and beliefs while avoiding painful or contradictory information. They self-select media channels. Strategy Target identity-aligned media. If users are on TikTok for entertainment, disruptive hard-sell ads are blocked out. Selective Attention People have heightened awareness of stimuli that meet their immediate needs or interests. The majority of ads are filtered out as \"noise\" due to lack of relevance. Strategy Relevance drives attention. Use personalization to match current \"pain points\" (e.g., displaying food ads before iftar). Selective Defense Consumers subconsciously screen out stimuli that they find psychologically threatening. They may distort information that contradicts their positive self-image. Strategy Avoid threatening the user's ego. Frame corrective messages (e.g., health, debt) as supportive rather than judgmental. 09 Perceptual Selection Why consumers notice some stimuli and ignore others"},{"img":"slides/ch4/18.jpg","title":"Guerilla Marketing","text":"15 Guerilla Marketing"},{"img":"slides/ch4/19.jpg","title":"Organization- Gestalt Principles","text":"Organization- Gestalt Principles Good Figure Objects groupped together tend to be perceived as a single ﬁgure. Tendency to simplify. Similarity Objects tend to be grouped together if they are similar. Closure Visual connection or continuity between sets of elements which do not actually touch each other in a composition. Proximity Objects tend to be grouped together if they are close to each other. Continuation When there is an intersection between two or more objects, people tend to perceive each object as a single uninterrupted object. Symmetry The object tend to be perceived as symmetrical shapes that form around their center. Click here to see examples"},{"img":"slides/ch4/20.jpg","title":"Perceptual Organization: Gestalt Principles","text":"Perceptual Organization: Gestalt Principles How the brain uses \"shortcuts\" to make sense of complex stimuli Figure–Ground The eye differentiates an object from its surrounding area. A form, silhouette, or shape is naturally perceived as figure (object), while the surrounding area is perceived as ground (background). Marketing Application Ensure the product or CTA button \"pops\" against the background. Don't let the message get lost in clutter. Proximity & Similarity Elements that are close together (proximity) or share visual characteristics like color/shape (similarity) are perceived as a unified group or related set. Marketing Application Group related products on shelves or website menus. Use consistent color coding for product lines (e.g., Almarai dairy vs juice). Closure The brain automatically fills in gaps to create a complete picture. We perceive incomplete shapes as whole by supplying the missing information. Marketing Application Use in logos (like IBM or WWF) or \"teaser\" campaigns where parts are hidden to engage the consumer's brain to \"solve\" the image. \"The whole is different from the sum of its parts.\" — Kurt Koffka 10"},{"img":"slides/ch4/21.jpg","title":"Consumer Imagery","text":"11 Consumer Imagery How Quality is Inferred Through Cues Quality Signal Types Intrinsic Cues Physical characteristics of the product itself (size, color, flavor, aroma, materials). These are hard to change without altering the product. Extrinsic Cues External attributes used to judge quality when intrinsic evaluation is difficult (price, brand image, store reputation, country of origin). KEY INSIGHT When consumers cannot \"test\" a product (like electronics or packaged food) before buying, they rely almost entirely on extrinsic cues to reduce risk. Intrinsic: Fresh taste, expiration date, packaging seal Extrinsic: \"Trusted Quality\" slogan, premium price image Store Image: Organized layout signals authenticity Service: Warranty & after-sales support (Risk reduction) Country of Origin \"Made in Saudi\": New cue for local pride & quality Support: Signals freshness and local economic support"},{"img":"slides/ch4/22.jpg","title":"Country of Origin","text":"32"},{"img":"slides/ch4/23.jpg","title":"Product Placement: 007","text":"007"},{"img":"slides/ch4/24.jpg","title":"Perceived Risk","text":"Perceived Risk Uncertainty that causes consumer hesitation Concept & Application Functional Risk Will it perform as expected? (e.g., Will the laptop battery last?) Physical Risk Is it safe to use? (e.g., Food safety, side effects, flammability) Financial Risk Is it worth the cost? (e.g., Overpaying, hidden fees, bad value) Social Risk What will others think? (e.g., Embarrassment from a \"cheap\" brand) Psychological Risk Will it hurt my ego? (e.g., Feeling guilty or \"not good enough\") Time Risk Will it waste my time? (e.g., Setup difficulty, returns process) Saudi Market Remedies Cash on Delivery (COD) Reduces financial risk & trust barriers for online purchases. \"Official Store\" Badges Platforms like Noon/Amazon use badges to eliminate functional risk (fakes). Local Agent Warranties \"2-Year Agent Warranty\" is a critical cue for electronics reliability. Clear Return Policies No-questions-asked returns reduce the fear of making a wrong choice. 12"},{"img":"slides/ch4/25.jpg","title":"Positioning Framework","text":"Positioning Framework Designing a distinct place in the consumer's mind STRATEGIC TOOL The Positioning Statement Pattern For [Target Segment], [Brand] is the [Frame of Reference] that [Point of Difference], because [Reason to Believe]. For Digital-First Saudis Frame of Reference Digital Enabler / Telco Point of Difference Unmatched 5G Speed & Coverage Reason to Believe Largest infrastructure investment in MENA. For Health-Conscious Families Frame of Reference Fresh Food Partner Point of Difference Trustworthy Quality Every Day Reason to Believe \"Quality you can trust\" heritage & local farms. For Tech & Knowledge Seekers Frame of Reference One-Stop Tech Hub Point of Difference Latest Gadgets + Reliability Reason to Believe Authorized reseller warranty & after-sales service. For Urban Socializers Frame of Reference The \"Third Place\" Point of Difference Premium Global Experience Reason to Believe Consistent standards & brand prestige. 13"},{"img":"slides/ch4/26.jpg","title":"Positioning Process","text":"Positioning Process A systematic approach from market insight to execution \"Positioning is not what you do to a product. Positioning is what you do to the mind of the prospect.\" — Ries & Trout 14 1 Identify Competition Define the competitive frame of reference. Who are we fighting against? (e.g., Coke vs Pepsi or Coke vs Water?) 2 Identify Attributes Determine which attributes and benefits consumers use to evaluate options in this category. 3 Diagnose Perceptions Conduct research (surveys, reviews) to map where brands currently sit in consumer minds. 4 Choose Position Select Points of Difference (POD) & Points of Parity (POP). Articulate the \"Reason to Believe\". 5 Implement (4Ps) Align Product, Price, Place, and Promotion to deliver the chosen position consistently. 6 Monitor & Reposition Track changes over time. Reposition when market dynamics or consumer needs shift."},{"img":"slides/ch4/27.jpg","title":"Perceptual Mapping: KSA Dairy Market","text":"Perceptual Mapping: KSA Dairy Market Visualizing competitive positioning based on consumer perception Example: Yogurt/Labneh Category Class Activity: Draw Your Map 1 Choose a Category Pick a sector familiar in Saudi Arabia (e.g., Coffee Shops, Fast Food, Airlines). 2 Select Two Axes What matters most? (e.g., Convenience vs. Experience, Healthy vs. Indulgent). 3 Plot 5 Brands Place competitors like Starbucks, Barn's, Dunkin', etc., based on perception, not just reality. 4 Find the Gap Where is the \"white space\" (un-owned position) for a new entrant? 15"}],"questions":[{"id":"4-1","n":1,"type":"mcq","q":"Individuals act and react on the basis of ________, not on the basis of ________.","answer":2,"exp":"'Perception beats reality'. People act and react on the basis of their PERCEPTIONS, not on objective reality.","options":["objective reality; their previous experiences","their previous experiences; their perceptions","their perceptions; objective reality","their perceptions; their previous experiences","their previous experiences; peer pressure"]},{"id":"4-2","n":2,"type":"mcq","q":"Your interpretation of visual and sensory input about polo shirts may be different from your classmate's because perception is ________.","answer":1,"exp":"Chapter 4: perception is psychological and SUBJECTIVE. It is filtered through each person's experience, expectations and motives. Two people can sense the same polo shirt and perceive it differently.","options":["objective","subjective","irrelevant","noise","based on personality traits"]},{"id":"4-3","n":3,"type":"mcq","q":"The process by which an individual selects, organizes, and interprets stimuli into a meaningful and coherent picture of the world is known as ________.","answer":1,"exp":"Chapter 4 defines perception as the process by which an individual selects, organizes and interprets stimuli into a meaningful and coherent picture of the world.","options":["observation","perception","realization","rationalization","understanding"]},{"id":"4-4","n":4,"type":"mcq","q":"________ can simply be described as \"how we see the world around us.\"","answer":1,"exp":"Perception is simply 'how we see the world around us': how we select, organize and interpret stimuli.","options":["Knowledge","Perception","Motivation","Attitude","Understanding"]},{"id":"4-5","n":5,"type":"mcq","q":"________ is/are the immediate and direct response of the sensory organs to stimuli.","answer":1,"exp":"Chapter 4: SENSATION is the immediate and direct response of the sensory organs to stimuli. It is raw data that answers 'What is detected?'","options":["Sensory receptors","Sensation","Sensory input","Sensory adaptation","Sensory blocking"]},{"id":"4-6","n":6,"type":"mcq","q":"Products, packages, brand names, advertisements, and commercials are examples of ________.","answer":3,"exp":"Chapter 4 shows sensory stimuli (sights, sounds, smells, tastes, textures). Products, packages, brand names and ads are all marketing stimuli.","options":["sensations","receptors","realities","stimuli","intensities"]},{"id":"4-7","n":7,"type":"mcq","q":"As sensory input ________, our ability to detect changes in input or intensity ________.","answer":0,"exp":"When there is less sensory input, our sensitivity to changes goes up. When there is a lot of input, it becomes harder to notice small changes. This is the logic behind thresholds and Weber's law in Chapter 4.","options":["decreases; increases","increases; increases","decreases; remains constant","remains constant; decreases","increases; remains constant"],"book":"Textbook: as sensory input decreases, our ability to detect changes in input or intensity increases.","depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 4, p. 78","page":78},{"id":"4-9","n":9,"type":"mcq","q":"The point at which a person can detect a difference between \"something\" and \"nothing\" is that person's ________ for that stimulus.","answer":1,"exp":"Chapter 4: the ABSOLUTE threshold is the lowest level at which a person can experience a sensation, the point between 'something' and 'nothing'.","options":["adaptation level","absolute threshold","just noticeable difference","differential threshold","sensory adaptation"]},{"id":"4-10","n":10,"type":"mcq","q":"Two people driving together may spot a billboard at different times. This means they have different ________.","answer":0,"exp":"Each person has their own absolute threshold, the lowest level at which they detect a stimulus. That is why two passengers can notice the same billboard at different moments.","options":["absolute thresholds","differential thresholds","just noticeable differences","adaptation levels","sensory adaptations"]},{"id":"4-11","n":11,"type":"mcq","q":"John drives by the same billboard every day on his way to work. He has seen the billboard so many times that he no longer notices it. This is an example of ________.","answer":0,"exp":"Sensory adaptation happens when consumers get used to a repeated stimulus and stop noticing it. That is what happened with John's daily billboard.","options":["sensory adaptation","just noticeable difference","differential threshold","perceptual blocking","absolute threshold"]},{"id":"4-12","n":12,"type":"mcq","q":"Sensory adaptation is of concern to national advertisers, who try to continuously change their advertising campaigns. They are concerned that consumers will ________.","answer":1,"exp":"Advertisers worry that consumers will GET USED TO their ads and stop noticing them. That's why Chapter 4 recommends rotating creatives every 2–3 weeks.","options":["get bored of their competitors' ads","get used to their ads","not understand their ads as intended","develop positive reactions to their ads","become more attuned to competing advertising"]},{"id":"4-13","n":13,"type":"mcq","q":"Some TV ads change sensory input by using silence or louder sounds in their ads to generate attention. This is a form of advertising used in order to overcome ________.","answer":2,"exp":"Silence or sudden loud sound is a change in sensory format. The playbook in Chapter 4 uses it to overcome sensory adaptation.","options":["sensation","preference for competitive advertisements","sensory adaptation","the just noticeable difference","perceptual blocking"]},{"id":"4-14","n":14,"type":"mcq","q":"Brand names stamped on eggs in supermarkets, featured on video screens in taxis, placed on subway tunnels in between stations, and featured on doctor's examination tables are examples of ________.","answer":3,"exp":"Placing brand names in unexpected spots (eggs, taxis, subway tunnels, exam tables) is ambush (guerilla) marketing, as in the giant luggage in Chapter 4. Chapter 4 also says to 'use unexpected media'.","options":["sensory adaptation","objective reality","viral advertising","ambush marketing","experiential marketing"],"book":"Textbook: ambush marketing = placing ads in places where consumers don't expect them, to break through clutter and sensory adaptation.","depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 4, p. 114","page":114},{"id":"4-15","n":15,"type":"mcq","q":"The minimal difference that can be detected between two similar stimuli is called the ________.","answer":1,"exp":"Chapter 4: the DIFFERENTIAL threshold (JND) is the minimal difference that can be detected between two similar stimuli.","options":["perceptual threshold","differential threshold","sensory threshold","absolute threshold","sensation threshold"]},{"id":"4-16","n":16,"type":"mcq","q":"Weber's law states that ________.","answer":2,"exp":"Weber's law: the stronger the initial stimulus, the greater the change needed to be noticed.","options":["the stronger the initial stimulus, the weaker the second stimulus must be to exceed the JND","the JND of a second stimulus is inversely related to the strength of the original stimulus","the stronger the initial stimulus, the greater the second stimulus must be to exceed the JND","consumers who buy the same products regularly are more likely than those who buy less frequently to notice changes in price, packaging, or product attributes","the more information consumers are given about a particular product, the more likely they are to purchase that product"]},{"id":"4-17","n":17,"type":"mcq","q":"Which of the following is true of JND?","answer":4,"exp":"Chapter 4's strategic rule: for a negative change such as a price hike, keep it BELOW the JND so it goes unnoticed.","options":["Decreasing prices below consumers' JND is likely to cause a significant rise in sales.","Making product improvements that far exceed consumers' JND is likely to maximize company revenues.","There is no JND for decreased product volume sold in existing packaging.","Making drastic changes to a company's logo to an extent well beyond consumers' JND allows companies to update their image without losing their ready recognition.","Increasing prices below consumers' JND is likely to go unnoticed by consumers."]},{"id":"4-18","n":18,"type":"mcq","q":"Which of the following is NOT an implication of the JND for logos?","answer":4,"exp":"Logo updates are usually kept below the JND, using small gradual changes to keep consumers' recognition (see the Heinz visual identity in Chapter 4). 'Always make dramatic changes' is NOT an implication of the JND.","options":["Marketers usually make numerous small changes.","Marketers minimize noticeable changes to maintain consumer recognition.","Marketers who make dramatic changes to logos may anger customers.","Marketers who crossed the differential threshold have chosen to return to their original logos.","Marketers should always try to cross the JND and make dramatic changes."],"depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 4, p. 83","page":83},{"id":"4-20","n":20,"type":"mcq","q":"Which of the following is true of the relationship between consumers' perceptions and their expectations?","answer":3,"exp":"Chapter 4: perception is filtered through experience, EXPECTATIONS and motives. Consumers tend to perceive products according to what they expect.","options":["Individual motivation does not affect perception.","Ads with irrelevant sexuality generally lead to better recall of the product advertised due to the attention-getting nature of the sexual content.","People tend to make observations and arrive at conclusions completely independent of their expectations.","Consumers tend to perceive products and product attributes according to their own expectations.","What consumers expect to see is completely dependent on their objective, first-hand experience with the particular product or advertising medium."]},{"id":"4-21","n":21,"type":"mcq","q":"Which of the following is true of the relationship between consumers' perceptions and their motives?","answer":3,"exp":"Selective attention: people are more aware of stimuli that meet their immediate needs. The stronger the need, the more attention they pay to related stimuli. Example: food ads before iftar.","options":["In general, there is a heightened awareness of stimuli that are irrelevant to consumers' needs.","The stronger the consumer's need, the greater the tendency to ignore related stimuli in the environment.","In general, there is decreased awareness of stimuli that are relevant to consumers' needs.","The stronger the consumer's need, the greater the tendency to pay attention to related stimuli in the environment.","Consumers tend to pay equal attention to all advertising, regardless of their needs at any given time."]},{"id":"4-22","n":22,"type":"mcq","q":"Which of the following does NOT provoke attention?","answer":2,"exp":"Shock, contrast and unusual images or sounds GET attention. Adaptation is the reverse: attention drops as people get used to a stimulus.","options":["shocking images","contrasting images","adaptation","unrealistic images","contrasting sounds"]},{"id":"4-23","n":23,"type":"mcq","q":"Which of the following is NOT true about the effect of expectations and motives on perceptions?","answer":1,"exp":"Stimuli that conflict sharply with expectations usually get MORE attention, not less. Contrast grabs attention. Perception is shaped by expectations and motives.","options":["People see what they expect to see based on familiarity, previous experience, and expectations.","Stimuli that conflict sharply with expectations often receive less attention than those that conform to expectations.","Irrelevant sexuality can distract from the ad's main message.","People tend to perceive the things they need or want.","There is decreased awareness of stimuli that are irrelevant to a consumer's needs."]},{"id":"4-24","n":24,"type":"mcq","q":"________ is a concept related to perception. People actively seek out messages that they find pleasant and actively avoid painful or threatening ones.","answer":1,"exp":"Selective exposure: consumers actively seek messages that reinforce their beliefs and are pleasant to them, and avoid painful or threatening ones.","options":["Selective attention","Selective exposure","Perceptual defense","Perceptual blocking","Perceptual organization"]},{"id":"4-25","n":25,"type":"mcq","q":"After buying a Mini Cooper, Kate began paying more attention to advertisements for Mini and spent more time on websites reading about how much Mini drivers love their cars because she was sympathetic to these messages and found them pleasant. This is an example of ________.","answer":1,"exp":"Kate seeks out pleasant messages that support her Mini purchase. That is selective exposure.","options":["selective attention","selective exposure","perceptual defense","perceptual blocking","perceptual organization"]},{"id":"4-26","n":26,"type":"mcq","q":"________ refers to consumers' heightened awareness of stimuli that meet their needs or interests, and minimal awareness of stimuli irrelevant to their needs.","answer":0,"exp":"Selective attention: heightened awareness of stimuli that meet your needs or interests. Irrelevant stimuli are filtered out as 'noise'.","options":["Selective attention","Selective exposure","Perceptual defense","Perceptual blocking","Perceptual organization"]},{"id":"4-27","n":27,"type":"mcq","q":"Listening to the radio on the way home from work, Paul is particularly aware of an ad for McDonald's because he is getting hungry. This is an example of ________.","answer":0,"exp":"Paul is hungry, so he notices the McDonald's ad. Needs drive attention, which is selective attention.","options":["selective attention","selective exposure","perceptual defense","perceptual blocking","perceptual organization"]},{"id":"4-28","n":28,"type":"mcq","q":"Consumers subconsciously screen out stimuli that they find psychologically threatening, even though exposure has already taken place. This is consistent with the perception factor of ________.","answer":2,"exp":"Selective (perceptual) defense: consumers subconsciously screen out stimuli they find psychologically threatening, even after exposure has happened.","options":["selective attention","selective exposure","perceptual defense","perceptual differentiation","perceptual organization"]},{"id":"4-29","n":29,"type":"mcq","q":"Canada requires tobacco firms to feature graphic health warnings on cigarette packs. In a perception context, this is to try to combat ________ where people no longer pay attention to the warning labels on packets.","answer":2,"exp":"Smokers screen out threatening health warnings. That is perceptual (selective) defense in Chapter 4. Graphic warnings are designed to break through this defense.","options":["selective attention","selective exposure","perceptual defense","perceptual differentiation","perceptual organization"]},{"id":"4-30","n":30,"type":"mcq","q":"________ is consumers' heightened awareness of stimuli that meet their needs or interests and minimal awareness of stimuli irrelevant to their needs.","answer":0,"exp":"Selective attention: awareness of stimuli that meet your needs, and minimal awareness of irrelevant ones.","options":["Selective attention","Selective exposure","Perceptual defense","Perceptual blocking","Perceptual differentiation"]},{"id":"4-31","n":31,"type":"mcq","q":"________ occurs when consumers tune into messages that they find pleasant or with which they are sympathetic, and they actively avoid painful or threatening ones.","answer":1,"exp":"Selective exposure: tuning into pleasant messages and avoiding painful or threatening ones.","options":["Defensive stimulation","Selective exposure","Perceptual defense","Perceptual blocking","Perceptual organization"]},{"id":"4-32","n":32,"type":"mcq","q":"In the figure and ground principle of Gestalt psychology, ________.","answer":4,"exp":"Figure–ground: the figure is the clear, distinct object. The GROUND is the background, perceived as indefinite, hazy and continuous.","options":["the ground is usually perceived as distinct and central to the image","the common line that separates the figure and the ground is generally attributed to the ground","figure and ground relationships are always interpreted in the same way","figure typically appears to be subordinate to ground and, therefore, less important","the ground is usually perceived as indefinite, hazy, and continuous"],"depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 4, p. 88","page":88},{"id":"4-33","n":33,"type":"mcq","q":"In product placement scenarios, marketers place an advertised product into a TV show or film by having it used by the cast, integrated into the plot, or associated with a character. In product placements, the product is considered the ________ and the show is the ________.","answer":1,"exp":"In product placement, like the Land Rover in the James Bond film in Chapter 4, the PRODUCT is the figure and the show is the ground.","options":["entertainment; brand","figure; ground","ground; perceptual organization","perceptual block; perceptual organization","ground; figure"]},{"id":"4-34","n":34,"type":"mcq","q":"To simplify life, people have a natural tendency to select stimuli from the environment and organize them into groups and perceive them as a unified whole. In a perception context, this is known as ________.","answer":3,"exp":"Organizing stimuli into groups and seeing them as a unified whole is grouping.","options":["figure-ground","perceptual blocking","perceptual mapping","grouping","closure"]},{"id":"4-35","n":35,"type":"mcq","q":"Individuals express their need for ________ by organizing their perceptions so that they form a complete picture.","answer":0,"exp":"Closure: the brain fills in gaps to see a complete picture. People have a need for closure.","options":["closure","interpretation","grouping","figure-ground patterns","exposure"]},{"id":"4-36","n":36,"type":"mcq","q":"When stimuli are highly ambiguous, an individual will usually ________.","answer":2,"exp":"Interpretation is the final stage of perception. When a stimulus is ambiguous, people interpret it according to their own needs, wishes and interests.","options":["ignore them","block them out","interpret them according to one's own needs, wishes, and interests","take extra time to understand the intentions of the source","consult others as to their meaning"],"book":"Textbook: the more ambiguous the stimulus, the more the perceiver projects their own needs and interests onto it.","depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 4, p. 89","page":89},{"id":"4-40","n":40,"type":"mcq","q":"________ refers to consumers' perceptions of all the components of products, services, and brands, and to how consumers evaluate the quality of marketers' offerings.","answer":2,"exp":"Consumer imagery covers how consumers perceive products and brands and how they infer quality from intrinsic and extrinsic cues.","options":["First impressions","Consumers' stereotypes","Consumer imagery","Symbolic features","Symbolic attributes"],"depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 4, p. 96","page":96},{"id":"4-47","n":47,"type":"mcq","q":"________ are physical characteristics of the product itself, such as size, color, flavor, or aroma.","answer":0,"exp":"Intrinsic cues are physical characteristics of the product itself, such as size, color, flavor, aroma and materials.","options":["Intrinsic cues","Product quality ratings","Extrinsic cues","Brand images","Brand personalities"]},{"id":"4-53","n":53,"type":"mcq","q":"When consumers buy the most expensive model to mitigate the risk of purchasing a poorly performing product, they are using ________ to minimize their risk.","answer":3,"exp":"When consumers are unsure, they use extrinsic cues such as a premium price as signs of quality. Buying the most expensive model relies on the price–quality relationship to reduce risk.","options":["information-seeking behavior","brand loyalty","narrow categorization","the price-quality relationship","perceptual blocking"]},{"id":"4-54","n":54,"type":"mcq","q":"Consumers are more likely to view price as an indicator of quality if ________.","answer":0,"exp":"Chapter 4's key insight: when consumers can't evaluate a product directly (little information), they rely on extrinsic cues such as price to judge quality.","options":["they have little information to go on","they are confident in their ability to make the product or service choice","they are experts","they are familiar with the store where the product is purchased","they are familiar with the product or service"]},{"id":"4-56","n":56,"type":"mcq","q":"________ is the uncertainty that consumers face when they cannot foresee the consequences of their purchase decisions.","answer":4,"exp":"Perceived risk is the uncertainty that makes consumers hesitate because they can't foresee the consequences of a purchase.","options":["Reference price","Service quality","Brand image","Intrinsic cues","Perceived risk"]},{"id":"4-58","n":58,"type":"mcq","q":"Barry has avoided purchasing a new laptop because prices keep falling and he is worried that, if he buys a laptop today, the same laptop will be cheaper in six months. Barry perceives ________ associated with the purchase of a new laptop.","answer":0,"exp":"Financial risk is 'Is it worth the cost?' Barry worries he'll overpay because prices are falling.","options":["financial risk","social risk","psychological risk","functional risk","time risk"]},{"id":"4-59","n":59,"type":"mcq","q":"Alice needs a new cell phone, but is anxious about which phone she should buy. Many of her friends own popular phone models but complain about lost calls, short battery life, and poor predictive text functionality. While she has identified several attractive phone models, she doesn't feel like she can really try the phones out in such an artificial setting and is nervous that she might pick a phone that doesn't work as well as she had hoped. Alice perceives ________ associated with the purchase of a new phone.","answer":3,"exp":"Functional risk is 'Will it perform as expected?' Alice worries the phone won't work as well as she hopes.","options":["financial risk","social risk","psychological risk","functional risk","time risk"]},{"id":"4-60","n":60,"type":"mcq","q":"________ is time spent in product search that may be wasted if the product does not perform as expected.","answer":4,"exp":"Time risk is 'Will it waste my time?' For example, time spent searching is wasted if the product fails.","options":["Financial risk","Social risk","Psychological risk","Functional risk","Time risk"]},{"id":"4-61","n":61,"type":"mcq","q":"If a mobile phone company, Mobile Power, offers a money-back guarantee to offset concerns that the product will not be worth its cost, it is an attempt to help mitigate consumers' perception of ________.","answer":1,"exp":"A money-back guarantee answers 'Is it worth the cost?', which is financial risk. It works like cash on delivery and clear return policies in the Saudi remedies.","options":["functional risk","financial risk","psychological risk","social risk","physical risk"]},{"id":"4-62","n":62,"type":"mcq","q":"If a cell phone company, Mobile Power, offers a warranty on their phones as an assurance their phones will perform as expected, it is likely an attempt to help mitigate consumers' perception of ________.","answer":0,"exp":"A warranty is an assurance that the product will perform. It reduces functional risk, like the '2-Year Agent Warranty' cue in Chapter 4.","options":["functional risk","financial risk","psychological risk","social risk","physical risk"]},{"id":"4-63","n":63,"type":"mcq","q":"Ragu, a spaghetti sauce maker, has decided to launch its most popular sauce flavors in a small pouch format that is more convenient to use. When they choose to launch their most popular sauce flavors, which are \"tried and true,\" instead of new flavors, Ragu is seeking to minimize consumers' perception of ________.","answer":1,"exp":"Launching 'tried and true' flavors assures consumers the product will perform (taste) as expected. That reduces functional risk.","options":["physical risk","functional risk","psychological risk","financial risk","time risk"]},{"id":"4-65","n":65,"type":"mcq","q":"If a consumer is concerned he will be embarrassed when his friends see him with a particular product, he is perceiving ________ risk.","answer":1,"exp":"Embarrassment in front of friends is SOCIAL risk ('What will others think?'), and the hurt to one's ego is PSYCHOLOGICAL risk.","options":["financial","psychological and social","time","physical","functional"]},{"id":"4-66","n":66,"type":"mcq","q":"Jeff wants to switch mobile phone carriers, but is concerned that if he switches, he may have to compare all the different carriers' calling plans again if he experiences a lot of \"dropped calls\" with his new provider. He is perceiving ________ risk.","answer":2,"exp":"Jeff dreads redoing all the comparisons. That is time risk.","options":["financial","psychological","time","physical","functional"]},{"id":"4-67","n":67,"type":"mcq","q":"Lola is concerned about the impact of genetically modified ingredients found in foods on the long-term health of her family members. She is perceiving ________ risk.","answer":3,"exp":"Worrying about the long-term health of family members is physical risk.","options":["financial","psychological","time","physical","functional"]},{"id":"4-68","n":68,"type":"mcq","q":"Which of the following is NOT a way for a consumer to overcome perceived risk?","answer":0,"exp":"Consumers reduce risk by seeking information, staying brand loyal, relying on store image, buying the most expensive model, or using cues such as official-store badges, warranties and COD. Choosing at random does NOT reduce risk.","options":["use a random choice model","seek information","remain brand loyal","rely on store image","buy the most expensive model"]},{"id":"4-69","n":69,"type":"mcq","q":"The ________ of a product or service is the process by which a company creates a distinct image and identity for its products, services, and brands in consumers' minds.","answer":2,"exp":"Positioning means 'designing a distinct place in the consumer's mind'. It creates a distinct image and identity for the brand.","options":["segmentation","target","positioning","psychographic inventory","concentrated marketing strategy"]},{"id":"4-70","n":70,"type":"mcq","q":"The image and unique identity of a product, service, or brand in consumers' minds is called its ________.","answer":0,"exp":"A brand's distinct image in consumers' minds is its POSITION. As Ries & Trout say in Chapter 4, 'Positioning is what you do to the mind of the prospect.'","options":["position","perception","segment","target","imposition"]},{"id":"4-73","n":73,"type":"mcq","q":"What is the THIRD step in the positioning process?","answer":4,"exp":"In the positioning process in Chapter 4, step 3 is 'Diagnose Perceptions': research how consumers currently see competing brands.","options":["Create a positioning statement focused on the benefits and value that the product provides.","Determine the target market's preferred combination of attributes.","Define the market in which the product or brand competes, relevant buyers and competitors.","Develop a distinctive, differentiating, value-based positioning concept.","Research how consumers perceive competing offerings on relevant attributes."]},{"id":"4-82","n":82,"type":"mcq","q":"________ is the process by which a company intentionally changes the distinct image and identity that its product or brand occupies in consumers' minds.","answer":2,"exp":"Step 6 of the positioning process is 'Monitor & Reposition'. Repositioning means intentionally changing a brand's place in consumers' minds when the market or consumer needs shift.","options":["Premier position","Positioning against competition","Repositioning","Key attribute positioning","Umbrella positioning"]},{"id":"4-89","n":89,"type":"mcq","q":"A juice brand places its ads in health and wellness magazines, assuming readers interested in health are more likely to seek out information from those ads than readers of news magazines. This phenomenon is known as ________.","answer":0,"exp":"Readers of health magazines choose content that matches their interests and then seek out related ads. That is selective exposure: choosing identity-aligned media.","options":["selective exposure","perceptual defense","selective organization","the contrast effect","perceptual blocking"]},{"id":"4-90","n":90,"type":"tf","q":"For each individual, reality is a totally personal phenomenon, based on that person's needs, wants, values, and personal experiences.","answer":true,"exp":"TRUE. Perception beats reality. Each person's 'reality' is personal, based on their needs, wants, values and experiences."},{"id":"4-91","n":91,"type":"tf","q":"Marketers are much more interested in what consumers objectively know about their products than what they perceive.","answer":false,"exp":"FALSE. Marketers care more about what consumers PERCEIVE, because perception beats reality."},{"id":"4-92","n":92,"type":"tf","q":"The minimal difference that can be detected between two similar stimuli is called the absolute threshold.","answer":false,"exp":"FALSE. That is the DIFFERENTIAL threshold (JND). The absolute threshold is the 'something vs nothing' point."},{"id":"4-93","n":93,"type":"tf","q":"According to Weber's law, a consumer will notice a 25 cent rise in the price of a 50 cent product more than a 25 cent rise in the price of a $10 product.","answer":true,"exp":"TRUE. Weber's law: a 25-cent rise on a 50-cent product is a 50% change and very noticeable. The same 25 cents on $10 is only 2.5%."},{"id":"4-94","n":94,"type":"tf","q":"The marketer's objective is to far exceed consumers' JND for product improvements in order to engender greater brand loyalty from consumers.","answer":false,"exp":"FALSE. Chapter 4's rule: positive changes should be made ABOVE the JND so they are noticed. Going FAR beyond it wastes money and doesn't build more loyalty.","book":"Textbook: marketers want improvements to be noticed (just above the JND) without spending wastefully on changes far beyond it.","depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 4, p. 82","page":82},{"id":"4-96","n":96,"type":"tf","q":"People perceive all stimuli to which they are exposed.","answer":false,"exp":"FALSE. Consumers filter out most stimuli as 'noise' through selective exposure, attention and defense."},{"id":"4-97","n":97,"type":"tf","q":"People usually see what they expect to see, and what they expect to see is usually based on familiarity, previous experience, or expectations.","answer":true,"exp":"TRUE. Perception is filtered by experience and expectations. People tend to see what they expect to see."},{"id":"4-99","n":99,"type":"tf","q":"In one type of perceptual defense, individuals sometimes unconsciously distort information that is not consistent with their needs, values, and beliefs.","answer":true,"exp":"TRUE. Selective defense: consumers may distort information that contradicts their self-image, needs or beliefs."},{"id":"4-100","n":100,"type":"tf","q":"People tend to experience the numerous stimuli they select from the environment as separate and discrete sensations.","answer":false,"exp":"FALSE. Gestalt psychology: people ORGANIZE stimuli into groups and wholes. 'The whole is different from the sum of its parts.'"},{"id":"4-101","n":101,"type":"tf","q":"Deliberate blurring of figure and ground, as when Absolut Vodka embeds the distinct shape of its bottle in a print image, can result in greater engagement of the audience with the advertisement.","answer":true,"exp":"TRUE. Deliberately blurring figure and ground makes the audience work to find the figure, which raises engagement.","depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 4, p. 89","page":89},{"id":"4-102","n":102,"type":"tf","q":"Completed messages or tasks are better remembered than those that are incomplete.","answer":false,"exp":"FALSE. Because of the need for closure, INCOMPLETE messages (teasers) are remembered BETTER. The brain wants to finish them.","depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 4, p. 88","page":88},{"id":"4-105","n":105,"type":"tf","q":"Positioning is more important to the ultimate success of a product than are its actual characteristics.","answer":true,"exp":"TRUE. 'Positioning is not what you do to a product. Positioning is what you do to the mind of the prospect'. Perception of the product matters more than its actual characteristics."},{"id":"4-108","n":108,"type":"tf","q":"When consumers evaluate concrete attributes of a product, such as performance and durability, they rely less on price and brand name as indicators of quality than when they evaluate the product's prestige and symbolic value.","answer":true,"exp":"TRUE. When consumers can evaluate intrinsic attributes such as performance, they rely less on extrinsic cues like price and brand name."},{"id":"4-111","n":111,"type":"tf","q":"Consumers are influenced by risks that exist whether they perceive the risks or not.","answer":false,"exp":"FALSE. Consumers are influenced only by risks they PERCEIVE, whether or not those risks actually exist."},{"id":"4-112","n":112,"type":"tf","q":"The amount of risk perceived depends on the specific consumer.","answer":true,"exp":"TRUE. Perceived risk depends on the consumer. Different people feel different types and levels of risk for the same purchase."}],"book":{"first":76,"last":115,"count":40}},{"id":5,"title":"Consumer Learning","exam":"midterm","slides":[{"img":"slides/ch5/01.jpg","title":"Consumer Behavior","text":"Consumer Behavior Twelfth Edition, Global Edition Chapter 5 Consumer Learning Copyright © 2019 Pearson Education, Ltd. All Rights Reserved."},{"img":"slides/ch5/02.jpg","title":"Learning Objectives (1 of 2)","text":"Learning Objectives (1 of 2) 5.1 To understand the elements of learning. 5.2 To understand behavioral learning, classical conditioning, and the roles of stimulus generalization and discrimination in marketing. 5.3 To understand instrumental conditioning and the objectives and methods of reinforcement. 5.4 To understand observational learning. 5.5 To understand how consumers process information."},{"img":"slides/ch5/03.jpg","title":"Learning Objectives (2 of 2)","text":"Learning Objectives (2 of 2) 5.6 To understand cognitive learning as a form of consumer decision-making. 5.7 To understand the impact of involvement and passive learning on purchase decisions. 5.8 To understand how to measure the outcomes of consumer learning."},{"img":"slides/ch5/04.jpg","title":"Learning through Ads","text":"Learning through Ads Source: Avocados From Mexico, Mexican Hass Avocado Importers Association"},{"img":"slides/ch5/05.jpg","title":"Learning Objective 5.1","text":"Learning Objective 5.1 5.1 To understand the elements of learning."},{"img":"slides/ch5/06.jpg","title":"Learning","text":"Learning Intentional versus. incidental learning Four elements: Motives Cues Responses Reinforcement Compare the two introductions of Febreze."},{"img":"slides/ch5/07.jpg","title":"Learning Objective 5.2","text":"Learning Objective 5.2 5.2 To understand behavioral learning, classical conditioning, and the roles of stimulus generalization and discrimination in developing in marketing."},{"img":"slides/ch5/08.jpg","title":"Classical Conditioning","text":"Classical Conditioning"},{"img":"slides/ch5/09.jpg","title":"Applications of Classical Conditioning","text":"Applications of Classical Conditioning Associative learning Need for repetition Advertising wear-out Three-hit theory Stimulus generalization Stimulus discrimination"},{"img":"slides/ch5/10.jpg","title":"Stimulus Generalization","text":"Stimulus Generalization Product line extensions Product form extensions Family branding Licensing Source: Procter & Gamble Co."},{"img":"slides/ch5/11.jpg","title":"Top Licensing Companies","text":"Top Licensing Companies"},{"img":"slides/ch5/12.jpg","title":"Stimulus Discrimination","text":"Stimulus Discrimination Brand differentiation Market leaders want consumers to distinguish between products and imitators Relevant, meaningful, valuable differentiation"},{"img":"slides/ch5/13.jpg","title":"Learning Objective 5.3","text":"Learning Objective 5.3 5.3 To understand instrumental conditioning and the objectives and methods of reinforcement."},{"img":"slides/ch5/14.jpg","title":"Instrumental Conditioning","text":"Instrumental Conditioning"},{"img":"slides/ch5/15.jpg","title":"Discussion Questions","text":"Discussion Questions What is the difference between positive reinforcement and negative reinforcement? What is the difference between negative reinforcement and punishment? What is the difference between extinction and forgetting?"},{"img":"slides/ch5/16.jpg","title":"Incentivized Advertising","text":"Incentivized Advertising Provides consumers with rewards for watching ads Consumers may try to avoid ads"},{"img":"slides/ch5/17.jpg","title":"Reinforcement Schedules (1 of 2)","text":"Reinforcement Schedules (1 of 2) Continuous Fixed ratio Variable ratio"},{"img":"slides/ch5/18.jpg","title":"Reinforcement Schedules (2 of 2)","text":"Reinforcement Schedules (2 of 2)"},{"img":"slides/ch5/19.jpg","title":"Extinction and Forgetting","text":"Extinction and Forgetting Defined Extinction occurs when a learned response is no longer reinforced and the link between the stimulus and the expected reward is eliminated. Forgetting is the point at which the link between the stimulus and the expected reward ceases to exist."},{"img":"slides/ch5/20.jpg","title":"Customer Satisfaction and Retention","text":"Customer Satisfaction and Retention Satisfaction and repeat patronage Frequent shopper programs Shaping"},{"img":"slides/ch5/21.jpg","title":"Mass Versus. Distributed Learning","text":"Mass Versus. Distributed Learning Should a learning schedule be spread out over a period of time? Should a learning schedule be “bunched up” all at once?"},{"img":"slides/ch5/22.jpg","title":"Learning Objective 5.4","text":"Learning Objective 5.4 5.4 To understand observational learning."},{"img":"slides/ch5/23.jpg","title":"Observational Learning","text":"Observational Learning Defined Also known as modeling, observational learning occurs when people observe and later imitate observed behaviors"},{"img":"slides/ch5/24.jpg","title":"Learning Objective 5.5","text":"Learning Objective 5.5 5.5 To understand how consumers process information."},{"img":"slides/ch5/25.jpg","title":"Sensory Store","text":"Sensory Store Defined A location in the brain where the sensory input lasts for just a second or two. If it is not processed immediately, it is lost."},{"img":"slides/ch5/26.jpg","title":"Cognitive Learning (1 of 2)","text":"Cognitive Learning (1 of 2)"},{"img":"slides/ch5/27.jpg","title":"Information Processing","text":"Information Processing Storing information Sensory store Short-term storage Long-term storage Rehearsal Encoding Information overload Retention Chunking Retrieval"},{"img":"slides/ch5/28.jpg","title":"Information Overload","text":"Information Overload Defined A situation that occurs when consumers receive too much information and find it difficult to encode and store it"},{"img":"slides/ch5/29.jpg","title":"Retaining Information","text":"Retaining Information Brand imprinting Sound symbolism Linguistic characteristics"},{"img":"slides/ch5/30.jpg","title":"Chunking Aids Retrieval","text":"Chunking Aids Retrieval Source: Swedish Tourist Association"},{"img":"slides/ch5/31.jpg","title":"Environmental Triggers","text":"Environmental Triggers Defined Cues in the environment that remind a person of something, and then she or he talks about it"},{"img":"slides/ch5/32.jpg","title":"Content That Captivates","text":"Content That Captivates"},{"img":"slides/ch5/33.jpg","title":"Learning Objective 5.6","text":"Learning Objective 5.6 5.6 To understand cognitive learning as a form of consumer decision-making."},{"img":"slides/ch5/34.jpg","title":"Cognitive Learning (2 of 2)","text":"Cognitive Learning (2 of 2) Defined The premise that learning occurs in the form of sequential, mental processing of information when people face problems they wish to resolve."},{"img":"slides/ch5/35.jpg","title":"Discussion Question","text":"Discussion Question How would the ad influence the behavior of an individual who was engaged in a cognitive learning process? Figure 5 .11 Cognitive Learning: Crest Pro-Health"},{"img":"slides/ch5/36.jpg","title":"Representations of Cognitive Learning (1 of 2)","text":"Representations of Cognitive Learning (1 of 2) Generic States of Cognitive Learning Knowledge Evaluation Behavior Tri-Component Attitude Model Definition—All attitudes have three components Cognitive Affective Conative Aida Definition—Developed to explain how marketing and selling messages engage consumers Attention Interest and Desire Action Innovation Adoption Definition—Developed by Everett Rogers to explain how consumers adopt new products and services Awareness Interest and Evaluation Trial and Adoption"},{"img":"slides/ch5/37.jpg","title":"Representations of Cognitive Learning (2 of 2)","text":"Representations of Cognitive Learning (2 of 2) Innovation Decision-Making Definition—Revised vversion of Everett Rogers’ consumer adoption process Knowledge Persuasion Decision, Implementation, and Confirmation Consumer Decision-Making Definition—The stages consumers pass through when making logical decisions Need Recognition Search and Evaluation Purchase and Post-Purchase Evaluation Simple Consumer Journey Definition—The nonlinear circular consumer journal includes a post-purchase stage in which a consumer enters into a relationship with a brand that often plays out on social media Consider Evaluate Buy and Post-Purchase Experience— Enjoy, Advocate, and Bond Expanded Consumer Journey Definition—nonlinear consumer journey in which consumers backtrack, skip steps, reject, or opt out at any stage Need/Want Recognition, Awareness/ Knowledge, Consider/Examine Search/Learn, Like/Trust, Sees Value/ Willing to Pay, Commit/Plan Consumer, Satisfaction/Dissatisfaction, Loyal/Repeat Buyer, Engage/Interact, Actively Advocate"},{"img":"slides/ch5/38.jpg","title":"Learning Objective 5.7","text":"Learning Objective 5.7 5.7 To understand the impact of involvement and passive learning on purchase decisions."},{"img":"slides/ch5/39.jpg","title":"Hemispheric Lateralization","text":"Hemispheric Lateralization Source: Mercedes-Benz U S A, L L C"},{"img":"slides/ch5/40.jpg","title":"Passive Learning","text":"Passive Learning Defined A form of learning in which consumers receive information from repeated exposures which is fully processed after a product is purchased"},{"img":"slides/ch5/41.jpg","title":"Learning Objective 5.8","text":"Learning Objective 5.8 5.8 To understand how to measure the outcomes of consumer learning."},{"img":"slides/ch5/42.jpg","title":"Measures","text":"Measures Aided recall (recognition) Unaided recall (recall) How does the Starch Readership Ad Study measure ad effectiveness?"},{"img":"slides/ch5/43.jpg","title":"Brand Equity","text":"Brand Equity Defined The intrinsic value of a brand name. This value stems from the foundations of brand loyalty: the consumer’s perception of the brand’s superiority, the social esteem that using it provides, and the customer’s trust and identification with the brand."}],"questions":[{"id":"5-1","n":1,"type":"mcq","q":"From a marketing perspective, the process by which individuals acquire the purchase and consumption knowledge and experience that they apply to future related behavior, which evolves and changes as consumers acquire knowledge from experience, observation, and interactions with others to impact future behavior, is known as ________.","answer":3,"exp":"Consumer learning is the process by which people gain purchase and consumption knowledge and experience that they apply to future behavior. It can be intentional or incidental.","options":["brand loyalty","brand equity","positive reinforcement","consumer learning","perceptual blocking"]},{"id":"5-2","n":2,"type":"mcq","q":"Jonas knows the tagline from the local oil change vendor, which is frequently advertised on the radio station he listens to. However, Jonas does not have a car and is not interested in oil changes. His awareness of the tagline was most likely due to ________.","answer":1,"exp":"Jonas wasn't trying to learn the tagline. He picked it up by accident from repeated radio ads. Chapter 5 calls this INCIDENTAL learning, as opposed to intentional learning.","options":["intentional learning","incidental learning","cognitive learning","negative reinforcement","punishment"]},{"id":"5-3","n":3,"type":"mcq","q":"Uncovering ________ is the primary objective of marketers as they seek to teach consumers how they can fill their needs by buying certain products and brands.","answer":0,"exp":"Motives are the first of the four elements of learning in Chapter 5 (motives, cues, responses, reinforcement). Marketers try to uncover consumer motives and then show how their products meet them.","options":["consumer motives","consumer cues","covert responses","overt responses","reinforcement mechanisms"],"depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 5, p. 117","page":117},{"id":"5-4","n":4,"type":"mcq","q":"Learning acquired by accident or without much effort is known as ________ learning.","answer":3,"exp":"Incidental learning is learning that happens by accident or without much effort.","options":["intentional","instrumental","intrinsic","incidental","inexplicable"]},{"id":"5-5","n":5,"type":"mcq","q":"Unfilled needs lead to ________, which spurs learning.","answer":3,"exp":"Unfilled needs create motivation (motives), which spurs learning. Motives are the first element in Chapter 5.","options":["feedback","cues","response","motivation","reinforcement"],"depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 5, p. 117","page":117},{"id":"5-6","n":6,"type":"mcq","q":"________ serve to stimulate learning, and ________ are the stimuli that direct them.","answer":1,"exp":"Of the four elements in Chapter 5, MOTIVES stimulate learning and CUES are the stimuli that give those motives direction.","options":["Motives; reinforcements","Motives; cues","Cues; responses","Cues; motives","Responses; reinforcements"],"depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 5, p. 117","page":117},{"id":"5-7","n":7,"type":"mcq","q":"In the marketplace, price, styling, packaging, advertising, and store displays all serve as ________ to help consumers fulfill their needs in product-specific ways.","answer":1,"exp":"Price, styling, packaging, ads and displays are CUES. They direct consumers toward fulfilling needs in product-specific ways.","options":["feedback","cues","response","motivation","reinforcement"],"depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 5, p. 117","page":117},{"id":"5-8","n":8,"type":"mcq","q":"How individuals react to a drive or cue constitutes their ________.","answer":2,"exp":"A response is how a person reacts to a drive or cue, meaning the behavior that results.","options":["perceptual organization","learning","response","motivation","reinforcement"],"depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 5, p. 117","page":117},{"id":"5-9","n":9,"type":"mcq","q":"________ increase(s) the likelihood that a specific response will occur in the future as the result of particular cues or stimuli.","answer":4,"exp":"Reinforcement increases the chance that a specific response will happen again when the same cues appear.","options":["Feedback","Cues","Response","Motivation","Reinforcement"],"depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 5, p. 117","page":117},{"id":"5-10","n":10,"type":"mcq","q":"At Gino's Italian Bistro, waitresses ask each table whether or not they have dined at Gino's before. A manager is sure to stop by every table with first-time guests to thank them for trying the restaurant and to encourage them to return. In this case, the manager visit constitutes ________ for the guests, making them feel like restaurant staff really care about the quality of their experience.","answer":4,"exp":"The manager's thank-you visit rewards the guests' choice and makes a repeat visit more likely. That is reinforcement.","options":["feedback","a cue","a response","a motive","reinforcement"],"depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 5, p. 117","page":117},{"id":"5-11","n":11,"type":"mcq","q":"There are two different theories on how people learn - the ________ theories and the ________ theories.","answer":2,"exp":"The two families of learning theory are behavioral theories and cognitive theories.","options":["behavioral; affective","cognitive; rational","behavioral; cognitive","emotional; affective","experiential; intentional"],"depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 5, p. 119","page":119},{"id":"5-12","n":12,"type":"mcq","q":"Which of the following theories assumes that learning takes place as the result of responses to external events?","answer":0,"exp":"Behavioral learning treats learning as responses to external events or stimuli. It focuses on inputs and outcomes, not on the mental process.","options":["behavioral learning","episodic learning","incidental learning","operational learning","none of the above"],"depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 5, p. 119","page":119},{"id":"5-13","n":13,"type":"mcq","q":"Classical conditioning and instrumental conditioning theory are examples of ________.","answer":1,"exp":"Classical conditioning and instrumental conditioning are the two behavioral learning theories under LO 5.2–5.3.","options":["perception theories","behavioral learning theories","cognitive associative theory","involvement theory","cognitive learning theories"],"depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 5, p. 119","page":119},{"id":"5-14","n":14,"type":"mcq","q":"Which of the following was demonstrated by Ivan Pavlov?","answer":0,"exp":"Pavlov's dogs (meat paste, bell, salivation) are the classical conditioning experiment shown in Chapter 5.","options":["classical conditioning","instrumental conditioning","behavioral conditioning","psychological conditioning","none of the above"]},{"id":"5-15","n":15,"type":"mcq","q":"If you usually listen to the 6 o'clock news while waiting for dinner to be served, you would tend to associate the news with dinner, and eventually the sound of the 6 o'clock news alone might cause your mouth to water even if dinner was not being prepared. This is known as ________.","answer":1,"exp":"This is the second example in Chapter 5. The 6 o'clock news (conditioned stimulus) is paired with dinner aromas (unconditioned stimulus) until the news alone causes salivation. That is classical conditioning.","options":["instrumental conditioning","classical conditioning","conditional learning","behavioral learning","perceptual learning"]},{"id":"5-16","n":16,"type":"mcq","q":"In a consumer behavior context, a(n) ________ might consist of a well-known brand symbol that implies quality, whereas a(n) ________ might consist of new products bearing the wellknown symbol.","answer":0,"exp":"The well-known brand symbol already brings up 'quality'. It is the UNCONDITIONED stimulus. The new product that carries the symbol becomes the CONDITIONED stimulus (Chapter 5 logic, applied through stimulus generalization in Chapter 5).","options":["unconditioned stimulus; conditioned stimulus","conditioned stimulus; conditioned response","unconditioned stimulus; unconditioned response","conditioned stimulus; unconditioned stimulus","unconditioned response; conditioned response"],"depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 5, p. 120","page":120},{"id":"5-17","n":17,"type":"mcq","q":"In a consumer behavior context, the previously acquired consumer perception of an existing product is the ________. When consumers try a new product by the same brand because they believe that the new product embodies the same attribute with which the brand's existing products are associated, this is the ________.","answer":4,"exp":"The existing perception of the brand is the automatic response, the UNCONDITIONED response. Trying the new product because it carries the brand's attributes is the learned CONDITIONED response.","options":["unconditioned stimulus; conditioned stimulus","conditioned stimulus; conditioned response","unconditioned stimulus; unconditioned response","conditioned stimulus; unconditioned stimulus","unconditioned response; conditioned response"],"depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 5, p. 120","page":120},{"id":"5-19","n":19,"type":"mcq","q":"When a professor reviews a certain topic many times, he or she is practicing ________.","answer":3,"exp":"Reviewing a topic many times is repetition. Chapter 5 lists the need for repetition as a key application of conditioning: it strengthens the association.","options":["psychological conditioning","stimulus-response conditioning","subliminal conditioning","repetition conditioning","none of the above"]},{"id":"5-20","n":20,"type":"mcq","q":"At some point, an individual can become satiated with numerous exposures to the same message, and both attention and retention will decline. This effect is known as ________.","answer":0,"exp":"Advertising wear-out: after too many exposures to the same ad, consumers get tired of it and attention and retention fall.","options":["advertising wear-out","the three-hit theory","stimulus differentiation","rehearsal","stimulus generalization"],"depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 5, p. 121","page":121},{"id":"5-21","n":21,"type":"mcq","q":"HSBC positions itself as \"the world's local bank.\" A recent advertising campaign consisted of about 20 ads centered on the theme that \"different values make the world a richer place.\" HSBC's use of different cosmetic variations in their ads while repeating the same advertising theme is an attempt to avoid ________.","answer":0,"exp":"HSBC repeats one theme with different cosmetic variations (new visuals each time) to avoid advertising wear-out.","options":["advertising wearout","the three-hit theory","stimulus differentiation","rehearsal","stimulus generalization"],"book":"Textbook: cosmetic variations change the look of an ad. Substantive variations change the content. Both help fight wear-out.","depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 5, p. 121","page":121},{"id":"5-22","n":22,"type":"mcq","q":"Marketing scholars who believe the ________ believe three exposures to an advertisement are needed: one to make consumers aware of the product, a second to show consumers the relevance of the product, and a third to remind them of the product.","answer":2,"exp":"The three-hit theory: exposure 1 creates awareness, exposure 2 shows relevance, and exposure 3 reminds the consumer.","options":["three-experience rule","three-introduction principle","three-hit theory","three-knock standard","three-exposure norm"],"depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 5, p. 121","page":121},{"id":"5-23","n":23,"type":"mcq","q":"Chelsea confuses a private-label brand's packaging with that of a national brand is an example of ________.","answer":1,"exp":"Chelsea responds to the private label the same way she responds to the national brand because the packages look alike. That is stimulus generalization.","options":["stimulus discrimination","stimulus generalization","cosmetic variations","advertising wearout","cognitive processing"]},{"id":"5-24","n":24,"type":"mcq","q":"Stimulus generalization refers to ________.","answer":2,"exp":"Stimulus generalization is responding the same way to slightly different stimuli.","options":["the tendency for stimuli to be similar in nature","the fact that most conditioned stimuli are similar to unconditioned stimuli","the tendency to respond the same way to slightly different stimuli","the tendency for extinction to occur when an unconditioned stimulus does not follow a stimulus similar to a conditioned stimulus","none of the above"],"depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 5, p. 119","page":119},{"id":"5-25","n":25,"type":"mcq","q":"Lucy finds Coca-Cola to be refreshing and tasty. When she attributes this perception to all colas in red cans, she is engaging in ________.","answer":3,"exp":"Lucy gives Coke's qualities to every cola in a red can. That is stimulus generalization.","options":["stimulus discrimination","advertising wearout","cosmetic variations","stimulus generalization","interference"]},{"id":"5-26","n":26,"type":"mcq","q":"Manufacturers of private-label brands try to make their packaging closely resemble the national brand leaders. They are hoping that consumers will engage in ________ and attribute the qualities of the leading national brand to the private-label brand by virtue of their similar packaging appearance.","answer":2,"exp":"Private labels copy the leader's packaging so that consumers GENERALIZE and give the leader's quality to the copy.","options":["stimulus discrimination","advertising wearout","stimulus generalization","family branding","interference"]},{"id":"5-27","n":27,"type":"mcq","q":"In ________, the marketer adds related products to an already established brand.","answer":2,"exp":"Product line extensions add related products to an already established brand.","options":["product form extensions","product category extensions","product line extensions","product differentiation","product generalization"],"depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 5, p. 122","page":122},{"id":"5-28","n":28,"type":"mcq","q":"Campbell's Soup Company continues to add new food products to its product line under the Campbell's brand name. This is known as ________.","answer":1,"exp":"Family branding means marketing a whole line of products under one brand name, as Campbell's does.","options":["corporate marketing","family branding","capital branding","licensing","positioning"],"depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 5, p. 122","page":122},{"id":"5-29","n":29,"type":"mcq","q":"Family branding, licensing, product line extensions, and product form extensions are all marketing strategies based on ________.","answer":0,"exp":"Chapter 5: product line extensions, product form extensions, family branding and licensing all rely on stimulus generalization.","options":["stimulus generalization","the spacing effect","stimulus discrimination","extinction","none of the above"]},{"id":"5-30","n":30,"type":"mcq","q":"________ results in the selection of a specific stimulus from among similar stimuli.","answer":1,"exp":"Stimulus discrimination is the opposite of generalization. The consumer picks out ONE specific stimulus from among similar ones.","options":["Stimulus generalization","Stimulus discrimination","Stimulus recognition","Stimulus selection","Stimulus differentiation"]},{"id":"5-31","n":31,"type":"mcq","q":"When Breyers sells Oreo Cookies and Cream ice cream, Breyers purchases ground Oreo cookies for inclusion in the ice cream and prominently displays the Oreo name on its packaging. This is an example of ________.","answer":1,"exp":"Breyers pays to use the Oreo name on its ice cream. Using another brand's well-known name is LICENSING.","options":["shaping","licensing","modeling","family branding","chunking"],"depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 5, p. 122","page":122},{"id":"5-32","n":32,"type":"mcq","q":"________ strategies are designed to distinguish a product or brand from that of competitors on the basis of an attribute that is relevant, meaningful, and valuable to consumers.","answer":1,"exp":"Product (brand) differentiation means standing apart from competitors on an attribute that is relevant, meaningful and valuable to consumers.","options":["Stimulus differentiation","Product differentiation","Stimulus discrimination","Stimulus generalization","Product discrimination"]},{"id":"5-33","n":33,"type":"mcq","q":"The product imitator hopes that the consumer will ________, whereas the market leader wants the consumer to ________ among similar stimuli.","answer":1,"exp":"Imitators want consumers to GENERALIZE so the copy seems as good as the leader. Leaders want consumers to DISCRIMINATE between products and imitators.","options":["discriminate; select","generalize; discriminate","select; generalize","discriminate; generalize","discriminate; differentiate"]},{"id":"5-34","n":34,"type":"mcq","q":"It is difficult to overthrow a brand leader once stimulus discrimination has occurred because the leader has had a longer period to teach consumers to associate the brand name with the product. In general, the longer the period of learning, ________.","answer":1,"exp":"The longer consumers have learned to link a brand with a product, the more likely they are to DISCRIMINATE and refuse imitators. That is why leaders are hard to overthrow.","options":["the less likely the consumer is to discriminate","the more likely the consumer is to discriminate","the more likely the consumer is to generalize","the more diverse the evoked set","the more likely the consumer is to engage in vicarious learning"],"depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 5, p. 120","page":120},{"id":"5-35","n":35,"type":"mcq","q":"When Listerine, who makes mouthwash in the form of a liquid, introduced Listerine PocketPacks, a solid form of the product, it was an example of a ________.","answer":0,"exp":"Listerine took the same product into a new FORM (liquid to strips). That is a product form extension.","options":["product form extension","product category extension","product line extension","product differentiation strategy","product generalization strategy"],"depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 5, p. 123","page":123},{"id":"5-36","n":36,"type":"mcq","q":"According to B.F. Skinner, ________.","answer":3,"exp":"Skinner's instrumental (operant) conditioning: people learn by trial and error and are 'rewarded' for choosing the right behavior. The jeans example in Chapter 5 shows this.","options":["the kind of learning most characteristic of human beings is problem solving, which enables individuals to gain some control over their environment","conditioned learning results when a stimulus that is paired with another stimulus that elicits a known response serves to produce the same response when used alone","a person's level of involvement during message processing is a critical factor in determining which route to persuasion is likely to be effective","most individual learning occurs in a controlled environment in which individuals are \"rewarded\" for choosing an appropriate behavior","each aspect of the marketing mix must reinforce the others if cues are to serve as the stimuli that guide consumer actions in the direction desired by the marketer"],"depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 5, p. 125","page":125},{"id":"5-37","n":37,"type":"mcq","q":"From a marketer's perspective, ________ involves obtaining the desired result from using a particular product or service.","answer":2,"exp":"Positive reinforcement is getting a desired result from using the product, like the 'perfect fit' reward in the jeans example.","options":["the central route to persuasion","punishment","positive reinforcement","the peripheral route to persuasion","negative reinforcement"],"depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 5, p. 125","page":125},{"id":"5-38","n":38,"type":"mcq","q":"If Sarah has a cold and takes Advil, and her cold goes away, increasing the likelihood she will take Advil if she has another cold, the removal of the unpleasant stimulus (the cold) is an example of ________.","answer":4,"exp":"Negative reinforcement means removing an unpleasant stimulus, like the cold going away. It still INCREASES the behavior. It is not the same as punishment.","options":["the central route to persuasion","copy testing","positive reinforcement","the peripheral route to persuasion","negative reinforcement"],"depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 5, p. 125","page":125},{"id":"5-39","n":39,"type":"mcq","q":"In cases of ________, behavior is unlearned due to a lack of reinforcement.","answer":4,"exp":"Extinction: a learned response is no longer reinforced, so the link between the stimulus and the reward is eliminated.","options":["forgetting","information overload","decay","advertising wearout","extinction"]},{"id":"5-40","n":40,"type":"mcq","q":"Forgetting is often related to the passage of time, and it is also referred to as ________.","answer":2,"exp":"Forgetting is often caused by the passage of time. This is called decay.","options":["encoding","relationship marketing","decay","advertising wearout","perceptual blocking"],"depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 5, p. 127","page":127},{"id":"5-41","n":41,"type":"mcq","q":"Gino's Italian Bistro offers all patrons a free after-dinner drink. This is an example of a ________ reward schedule.","answer":4,"exp":"A free drink for EVERY patron, every time, is a continuous reinforcement schedule.","options":["systematic","variable ratio","random","fixed ratio","continuous"]},{"id":"5-42","n":42,"type":"mcq","q":"Big Y Supermarkets sometimes issued poker chips of different values when consumers checked out at their supermarkets. The consumers did not get one each time they checked out, and they did not know what the reward was going to be if they got one. This is an example of a ________ reward schedule.","answer":1,"exp":"The rewards came randomly and their value was unknown. That is a variable ratio schedule, the same logic as slot machines.","options":["systematic","variable ratio","random","fixed ratio","continuous"]},{"id":"5-43","n":43,"type":"mcq","q":"Francis gets a free sandwich after she purchases nine sandwiches at Subway. This is an example of a ________ reward schedule.","answer":3,"exp":"A reward after every Nth purchase (the 10th sandwich) is a fixed ratio schedule.","options":["systematic","variable ratio","random","fixed ratio","continuous"]},{"id":"5-44","n":44,"type":"mcq","q":"Reinforcement performed before the desired consumer behavior actually takes place is called ________.","answer":3,"exp":"Shaping means reinforcing behaviors that must happen before the target behavior. Example: rewarding store visits before a purchase.","options":["positioning","licensing","preemptive reward","shaping","passive learning"],"depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 5, p. 125","page":125},{"id":"5-45","n":45,"type":"mcq","q":"________ produce(s) more initial learning, whereas ________ usually persist(s) longer.","answer":1,"exp":"Massed learning ('bunched up' all at once) produces more INITIAL learning. Distributed learning (spread out over time) LASTS longer.","options":["The central route to persuasion; the peripheral route to persuasion","Massed learning; distributed learning","Product form extensions; product line extensions","Modeling; vicarious learning","Recognition; recall"],"depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 5, p. 128","page":128},{"id":"5-46","n":46,"type":"mcq","q":"If Mark wants an immediate impact as he introduces his product, he should use ________; if he wants long-term, regular buying behavior, he should use ________.","answer":1,"exp":"For an immediate launch impact, use MASSED learning. For long-term regular buying, use DISTRIBUTED learning.","options":["the central route to persuasion; the peripheral route to persuasion","massed learning; distributed learning","product form extensions; product line extensions","modeling; vicarious learning","recognition; recall"],"depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 5, p. 128","page":128},{"id":"5-47","n":47,"type":"mcq","q":"________ is the process through which individuals learn behavior by observing the behavior of others and the consequences of such behavior.","answer":2,"exp":"Observational learning, also called modeling: people observe and later imitate others' behaviors and their consequences.","options":["Massed learning","Passive learning","Observational learning","Positioning","Distributed learning"]},{"id":"5-48","n":48,"type":"mcq","q":"When a child learns about social and consumer behavior by observing his/her parents and/or older siblings, it is an example of ________.","answer":2,"exp":"A child imitating parents or siblings is observational learning, or modeling.","options":["massed learning","passive learning","observational learning","positioning","distributed learning"]},{"id":"5-49","n":49,"type":"mcq","q":"What type of learning theory emphasizes that people are problem solvers who actively use information from the world around them to master the environment?","answer":2,"exp":"Cognitive learning sees people as problem solvers who mentally process information to resolve problems.","options":["instrumental conditioning","classical conditioning","cognitive learning","operant conditioning","none of the above"]},{"id":"5-50","n":50,"type":"mcq","q":"Cognitive learning theory ________.","answer":0,"exp":"Chapter 5: cognitive learning is sequential, mental processing of information when people face problems they want to resolve. They have a goal, so they search and process.","options":["occurs when a person has a goal and must search for and process data in order to make a decision or solve a problem","suggests that a person's level of involvement during message processing is a critical factor in determining which route to persuasion is likely to be effective","suggests that most individual learning occurs in a controlled environment in which individuals are \"rewarded\" for choosing an appropriate behavior","suggests that each aspect of the marketing mix must reinforce the others if cues are to serve as the stimuli that guide consumer actions in the direction desired by the marketer","suggests that conditioned learning results when a stimulus that is paired with another stimulus that elicits a known response serves to produce the same response when used alone"]},{"id":"5-51","n":51,"type":"mcq","q":"________ is the stage of real memory in which information is processed and held for just a brief period.","answer":2,"exp":"Short-term store, or working memory, holds information for a brief time while it is being processed.","options":["Sensory store","Rehearsal","Short-term store","Recall","Long-term store"],"depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 5, p. 129","page":129},{"id":"5-52","n":52,"type":"mcq","q":"The amount of information available for delivery from short-term storage to long-term storage depends on the amount of ________ it is given.","answer":1,"exp":"How much information moves from short-term to long-term storage depends on REHEARSAL.","options":["recall","rehearsal","retrieval","shaping","encoding"],"depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 5, p. 129","page":129},{"id":"5-53","n":53,"type":"mcq","q":"The purpose of rehearsal is to hold information in short-term storage long enough for ________ to take place.","answer":4,"exp":"Rehearsal keeps information in short-term storage long enough to be ENCODED into long-term storage (the model in Chapter 5).","options":["recall","retrieval","involvement","comprehension","encoding"],"depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 5, p. 129","page":129},{"id":"5-54","n":54,"type":"mcq","q":"________, or the silent, mental repetition of information, must take place for information to have a chance to be transferred to the long-term store.","answer":1,"exp":"Rehearsal is the silent mental repetition of information. It must happen before information can transfer to the long-term store.","options":["Recall","Rehearsal","Involvement","Comprehension","Encoding"],"depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 5, p. 129","page":129},{"id":"5-55","n":55,"type":"mcq","q":"When consumers receive too much information and then find it difficult to encode and store it, ________ occurs.","answer":4,"exp":"Chapter 5 defines information overload: consumers receive too much information and find it difficult to encode and store.","options":["chunking","encoding","long-term store purging","short-term store purging","information overload"]},{"id":"5-56","n":56,"type":"mcq","q":"The process by which we recover information from long-term storage is known as ________.","answer":3,"exp":"Retrieval is the process of recovering information from long-term storage.","options":["retention","decoding","encoding","retrieval","processing"],"depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 5, p. 129","page":129},{"id":"5-57","n":57,"type":"mcq","q":"If Betty faces a problem with her teeth, seeks a solution from her dentist, accepts the information, and starts using a different toothpaste that solves the problem, she is engaging in a process called ________.","answer":4,"exp":"Betty has a problem, looks for information, processes it and solves the problem. That is cognitive learning.","options":["passive learning","observational learning","incidental learning","behavioral learning","cognitive learning"]},{"id":"5-58","n":58,"type":"mcq","q":"Which representation of cognitive learning includes awareness, interest and evaluation, trial and adoption?","answer":0,"exp":"Chapter 5: the Innovation Adoption model (Everett Rogers) is Awareness, Interest and Evaluation, Trial and Adoption.","options":["Innovation Adoption","AIDA","Tri-Component","Generic Cognitive Learning","Innovation Decision Making"]},{"id":"5-60","n":60,"type":"mcq","q":"________ suggests that the human brain is divided into two distinct cerebral hemispheres that operate together but specialize in the processing of different types of cognitions.","answer":1,"exp":"Hemispheric lateralization: the brain's left and right hemispheres work together but specialize in different types of cognition.","options":["An avatar","Hemispheric lateralization","Consumer involvement","Narrow categorization","Broad categorization"]},{"id":"5-61","n":61,"type":"mcq","q":"When consumers watch advertising on TV, they ________ information without active involvement.","answer":2,"exp":"TV is mainly pictorial and processed passively by the RIGHT brain, without active involvement.","options":["passively; left-brain","actively; left-brain","passively; right-brain","actively; right-brain","intentionally; left-brain"],"book":"Textbook: the right hemisphere handles nonverbal, pictorial, holistic information and the left handles verbal, analytical information. TV is low-involvement and right-brain, while print is high-involvement and left-brain.","depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 5, p. 134","page":134},{"id":"5-62","n":62,"type":"mcq","q":"________ suggests repeated exposure to TV commercials, or low involvement information processing, induces purchase decisions before the formation of consumer attitudes toward products.","answer":3,"exp":"Passive learning: repeated exposure through low-involvement processing leads to purchase, and the information is fully processed only AFTER the product is bought.","options":["Cognitive learning","Left-brain learning","Hemispheric lateralization","Passive learning","Central route learning"]},{"id":"5-63","n":63,"type":"mcq","q":"________ are considered high-involvement, so they are processed by the ________ side of the brain.","answer":1,"exp":"Print ads require active reading and verbal processing. That makes them high-involvement and processed by the LEFT brain.","options":["Print advertisements; right","Print advertisements; left","Television advertisements; right","Television advertisements; left","Pictorial advertisements; left"],"depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 5, p. 136","page":136},{"id":"5-64","n":64,"type":"mcq","q":"The right brain's passive processing of information, wherein a product is paired with a visual image repeatedly to produce a desired response, is consistent with ________.","answer":2,"exp":"Pairing a product with a visual image again and again, processed passively by the right brain, matches classical conditioning.","options":["observational learning","operant conditioning","classical conditioning","cognitive learning","high involvement processing"],"depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 5, p. 134","page":134},{"id":"5-65","n":65,"type":"mcq","q":"In ________ tests, the consumer is shown an ad and asked whether he or she remembers seeing it and can remember any of its salient points.","answer":3,"exp":"Recognition, or aided recall: the consumer is SHOWN the ad and asked whether they remember it.","options":["brand loyalty","low involvement processing","recall","recognition","discrimination"],"depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 5, p. 138","page":138},{"id":"5-66","n":66,"type":"mcq","q":"In ________ tests, the consumer is asked whether he or she has read a specific magazine or watched a specific television show, and, if so, if he or she can remember any ads or commercials seen, the product and brand advertised, and any notable points about the offerings promoted.","answer":2,"exp":"Recall, or unaided recall: the consumer is NOT shown the ad and must remember the ads and brands on their own.","options":["brand loyalty","low involvement processing","recall","recognition","discrimination"],"depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 5, p. 138","page":138},{"id":"5-67","n":67,"type":"mcq","q":"What are the three criteria used in the Starch Readership Ad Study?","answer":3,"exp":"The Starch Readership Ad Study measures noticing the ad, associating the ad with the brand, and involvement with the ad (reading most of it).","options":["involvement with the ad, discriminating the advertised brand from competitive brands, providing greater share of wallet to the advertised brand","involvement with the ad, shopping for the advertised product, loyalty to the advertised product","associating the ad with the brand advertised, shopping for the advertised product, loyalty to the advertised product","noticing the ad, associating the ad with the brand advertised, and involvement with the ad","noticing the ad, shopping for the advertised product, loyalty to the advertised product"],"book":"Textbook: Starch levels are 'Noted', 'Associated' and 'Read Most'.","depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 5, p. 138","page":138},{"id":"5-69","n":69,"type":"mcq","q":"A fast-food chain, Speedy Taco, adds chicken tacos to its menu of beef tacos. This constitutes ________.","answer":2,"exp":"Adding chicken tacos to a taco menu adds a related product under the same brand. That is a product LINE extension.","options":["product form extension","product category extension","product line extension","product reputation extension","product brand extension"]},{"id":"5-70","n":70,"type":"mcq","q":"Speedy Taco sells Speedy Tacos, Speedy Burritos and Speedy Mexican Pizzas, all under the same Speedy brand name. This is known as ________.","answer":0,"exp":"Selling several products (tacos, burritos, pizzas) under one brand name is family branding.","options":["family branding","stimulus generalization","product form extension","licensing","brand loyalty"]},{"id":"5-71","n":71,"type":"mcq","q":"Speedy Taco allows Continental Foods to put the Speedy name on its line of salsa and tortilla chips sold in grocery stores. Speedy Taco is engaging in ________.","answer":4,"exp":"Allowing another company to use your brand name on its products is LICENSING.","options":["family branding","co-branding","encoding","stimulus generalization","licensing"]},{"id":"5-81","n":81,"type":"tf","q":"Consumer learning is a process that continually evolves and changes as a result of newly acquired knowledge or from actual experience.","answer":true,"exp":"TRUE. Learning is a process that keeps evolving as consumers gain new knowledge and experience."},{"id":"5-82","n":82,"type":"tf","q":"The role of experience in learning indicates that all learning is deliberately sought.","answer":false,"exp":"FALSE. Chapter 5 separates INTENTIONAL learning from INCIDENTAL learning. Much learning happens by accident."},{"id":"5-83","n":83,"type":"tf","q":"Cues serve to direct consumer drives regardless of whether these cues are consistent with consumer expectations.","answer":false,"exp":"FALSE. Cues direct motives only when they are CONSISTENT with consumer expectations.","book":"Textbook: marketers must make sure cues (price, packaging, ads) are consistent with consumers' expectations, or they won't guide behavior.","depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 5, p. 117","page":117},{"id":"5-84","n":84,"type":"tf","q":"Learning occurs only when responses are overt.","answer":false,"exp":"FALSE. Responses can be covert (mental) as well as overt. Learning can happen without visible action.","book":"Textbook: a response may be a mental or attitudinal change that is not an observable behavior.","depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 5, p. 117","page":117},{"id":"5-85","n":85,"type":"tf","q":"Increasing the number of repetitions of a message increases the message's retention, without any known satiation point.","answer":false,"exp":"FALSE. Too many repetitions cause ADVERTISING WEAR-OUT. Satiation sets in and retention declines.","depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 5, p. 121","page":121},{"id":"5-86","n":86,"type":"tf","q":"Consumers exposed to substantively varied ads process more information about product attributes and have more positive thoughts about the product than those exposed to cosmetic variation.","answer":true,"exp":"TRUE. To fight wear-out, substantive variation (changing the content) leads to more information processing and more positive thoughts than cosmetic variation.","book":"Textbook: substantive variations change the content of the ad. Cosmetic variations only change its look.","depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 5, p. 121","page":121},{"id":"5-87","n":87,"type":"tf","q":"Instrumental learning theorists believe that learning occurs through a trial-and-error process, with habits formed as a result of rewards received for certain responses or behaviors.","answer":true,"exp":"TRUE. Instrumental conditioning means learning by trial and error. Responses that are rewarded become habits, like the 'perfect fit' jeans.","depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 5, p. 118","page":118},{"id":"5-88","n":88,"type":"tf","q":"Gloria recently tried a new brand of shampoo that ended up leaving her hair looking matted and greasy. This outcome is an example of negative reinforcement.","answer":false,"exp":"FALSE. A bad outcome that DISCOURAGES repeat behavior is PUNISHMENT. Negative reinforcement REMOVES an unpleasant state and so encourages behavior.","depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 5, p. 126","page":126},{"id":"5-89","n":89,"type":"tf","q":"Extinction and forgetting are the same thing.","answer":false,"exp":"FALSE. Chapter 5 defines them separately. Extinction happens when reinforcement STOPS. Forgetting happens when the stimulus–reward link fades over time (decay)."},{"id":"5-90","n":90,"type":"tf","q":"Companies that create personal connections with customers, and also offer diverse product lines and competitive prices, are the ones providing the best reinforcement, resulting in satisfaction and repeat patronage.","answer":true,"exp":"TRUE. Satisfaction and repeat patronage come from the best reinforcement: personal connections, a diverse product line and competitive prices.","depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 5, p. 127","page":127},{"id":"5-91","n":91,"type":"tf","q":"The promise of possibly receiving a reward provides positive reinforcement and encourages consumer patronage.","answer":true,"exp":"TRUE. Even the possibility of a reward acts as positive reinforcement and encourages patronage.","depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 5, p. 126","page":126},{"id":"5-92","n":92,"type":"tf","q":"Lotteries, sweepstakes, door prizes, and contests that require certain consumer behaviors for eligibility are examples of variable ratio reward schedules.","answer":true,"exp":"TRUE. Lotteries, sweepstakes and contests reward randomly. That is a variable ratio schedule.","depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 5, p. 126","page":126},{"id":"5-93","n":93,"type":"tf","q":"Reinforcement performed before the desired consumer behavior actually takes place is called modeling.","answer":false,"exp":"FALSE. Reinforcement given BEFORE the desired behavior is SHAPING. Modeling is observational learning.","depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 5, p. 125","page":125},{"id":"5-94","n":94,"type":"tf","q":"When a marketer's goal is long-term repeat buying on a regular basis, a massed learning schedule is preferable.","answer":false,"exp":"FALSE. Long-term regular buying calls for DISTRIBUTED learning. Massed learning gives a quick initial impact.","depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 5, p. 128","page":128},{"id":"5-95","n":95,"type":"tf","q":"A distributed ad campaign, with ads repeated on a regular basis, results in more long-term learning and is relatively immune to extinction.","answer":true,"exp":"TRUE. Distributed learning, with ads repeated regularly over time, leads to longer-lasting learning that resists extinction.","depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 5, p. 128","page":128},{"id":"5-97","n":97,"type":"tf","q":"Although it is relatively easy to get information into the consumer's sensory store, it is difficult to make a lasting impression.","answer":true,"exp":"TRUE. Information gets into the sensory store easily but lasts only a second or two. If it isn't processed right away, it is lost."},{"id":"5-98","n":98,"type":"tf","q":"Once information is perceived, it is first stored in the short-term store, then the long-term store, then the sensory store.","answer":false,"exp":"FALSE. The order is the opposite. Sensory store, then short-term store, then long-term store."},{"id":"5-99","n":99,"type":"tf","q":"Rehearsal is the process by which we select a word or visual image to represent a perceived object.","answer":false,"exp":"FALSE. Choosing a word or image to represent a perceived object is ENCODING. Rehearsal is silent mental repetition.","depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 5, p. 129","page":129},{"id":"5-100","n":100,"type":"tf","q":"When consumers are presented with information overload, they may encounter difficulty in encoding and storing it all.","answer":true,"exp":"TRUE. That is the definition of information overload in Chapter 5."},{"id":"5-101","n":101,"type":"tf","q":"High-involvement purchases are important to the consumer in terms of perceived risk, so they provoke extensive problem solving and information search.","answer":true,"exp":"TRUE. LO 5.7: high-involvement purchases matter a lot to the consumer and carry high perceived risk, so they lead to extensive problem solving and information search.","depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 5, p. 118","page":118},{"id":"5-102","n":102,"type":"tf","q":"Low-involvement purchases are not very important, hold little relevance, have little perceived risk, and provoke limited information processing.","answer":true,"exp":"TRUE. Low-involvement purchases are less important and low-risk, so consumers process little information. That is where passive learning happens.","depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 5, p. 118","page":118},{"id":"5-104","n":104,"type":"tf","q":"The purpose of recognition and recall tests is to determine whether consumers remember seeing an ad and the extent to which they have read it and can recall its content.","answer":true,"exp":"TRUE. Recognition (aided) and recall (unaided) tests measure whether consumers remember an ad and how much of its content they can recall.","depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 5, p. 138","page":138}],"book":{"first":116,"last":141,"count":26}},{"id":14,"title":"Consumer Decision-Making and Diffusion of Innovations","exam":"midterm","slides":[{"img":"slides/ch14/01.jpg","title":"Consumer Behavior","text":"Consumer Behavior Twelfth Edition, Global Edition Chapter 14 Consumer Decision-Making and Diffusion of Innovations Copyright © 2019 Pearson Education, Ltd. All Rights Reserved."},{"img":"slides/ch14/02.jpg","title":"Learning Objectives","text":"Learning Objectives 14.1 To understand the consumer decision-making process and consumer decision journey. 14.2 To understand the dynamics of buying gifts. 14.3 To understand how new products gain acceptance and how individuals decide whether or not to adopt them."},{"img":"slides/ch14/03.jpg","title":"Consumer Decision Making","text":"Consumer Decision Making"},{"img":"slides/ch14/04.jpg","title":"Consumer Journey","text":"Consumer Journey Defined The consumer journey is the newest way of describing the stages consumers pass through as they develop relationships with brands before, during, and after purchase."},{"img":"slides/ch14/05.jpg","title":"Learning Objective 14.1","text":"Learning Objective 14.1 14.1 To understand the consumer decision-making process and consumer decision journey."},{"img":"slides/ch14/06.jpg","title":"Input: External Influences","text":"Input: External Influences Marketing mix Sociocultural influences Family Peers Social class Reference groups Culture/subculture Communications"},{"img":"slides/ch14/07.jpg","title":"Process: Need Recognition","text":"Process: Need Recognition Actual state need recognition Desired state need recognition Discussion Question: Provide an example of when you experienced actual state need recognition. Provide an example of when you experienced desired state need recognition. How did you respond to each need?"},{"img":"slides/ch14/08.jpg","title":"Decision Spectrum","text":"Decision Spectrum Extensive Problem Solving A lot of information needed Must establish a set of criteria for evaluation Limited Problem Solving Criteria for evaluation established Fine tuning with additional information Routinized Response Behavior Usually make decisions based on what they already know Frequent, low risk purchases"},{"img":"slides/ch14/09.jpg","title":"Extensive Problem Solving versus. Routinized Response Behavior","text":"Extensive Problem Solving versus. Routinized Response Behavior Source: Gemological Institute of America Source: Pfizer, I n c."},{"img":"slides/ch14/10.jpg","title":"Consumer Involvement","text":"Consumer Involvement Defined The degree of personal relevance that the product or purchase holds for the consumer."},{"img":"slides/ch14/11.jpg","title":"Pre-Purchase Search","text":"Pre-Purchase Search Personal experience and external information Memory Search Shopping provides external information Men and women are different Seek opinions of friends (e.g. shopping selfies) Search greatest when product category knowledge is low Cognitive challenge may limit search"},{"img":"slides/ch14/12.jpg","title":"Information Search: Contextual Factors","text":"Information Search: Contextual Factors Task complexity Information organization Time constraints Consumers have limited information-processing capacity"},{"img":"slides/ch14/13.jpg","title":"Factors That Increase Search (1 of 2)","text":"Factors That Increase Search (1 of 2) Product Factors Long periods of time between successive purchases Frequent changes in product styling Frequent price changes Volume purchasing (large number of units) High price Many alternative brands Much variation in features"},{"img":"slides/ch14/14.jpg","title":"Factors That Increase Search (2 of 2)","text":"Factors That Increase Search (2 of 2) Situational Factors Experience: First-time purchase; no past experience because the product is new; unsatisfactory past experience within the product category Social Acceptability: The purchase is for a gift; the product is socially visible Value-Related Considerations: The purchase is discretionary rather than necessary; all alternatives have both desirable and undesirable consequences; family members disagree on product requirements or evaluation of alternatives; product usage deviates from important reference groups; the purchase involves ecological considerations; many sources of conflicting information. Consumer Factors Demographics: Education, income, occupation, age, wealth, and marital status Personality Traits: One’s degree of dogmatism, willingness to accept risk, product involvement, and novelty seeking"},{"img":"slides/ch14/15.jpg","title":"Evaluation of Alternatives: Brand-Sets","text":"Evaluation of Alternatives: Brand-Sets Evoked set (consideration set) Inept set Inert set"},{"img":"slides/ch14/16.jpg","title":"Excluded Products Include","text":"Excluded Products Include Unknown brands or models Unacceptable brands of poor quality or inappropriate positioning Brands that are perceived as not having special benefits Overlooked brands that have not been clearly positioned Brands that do not satisfy perceived needs"},{"img":"slides/ch14/17.jpg","title":"Evaluation of Alternatives: Attributes","text":"Evaluation of Alternatives: Attributes Product attribute examples Size Weight Sweetness Color Packaging Criteria to assess product may be advertised Price less important when products are “right” Brand credibility is affected by Perceived quality Information costs saved Perceived risk"},{"img":"slides/ch14/18.jpg","title":"Decision Rules","text":"Decision Rules Compensatory decision rules Each relevant attribute weighted Summated score for each brand Noncompensatory decision rules Conjunctive Lexicographic Disjunctive Affect Referral – no assessment of individual attributes"},{"img":"slides/ch14/19.jpg","title":"Recognition Heuristic","text":"Recognition Heuristic Defined Consumers choose the most familiar product or brand"},{"img":"slides/ch14/20.jpg","title":"Application of Decision Rules","text":"Application of Decision Rules Table 14.1 Applying the Decision Rules to Downloading Smartphone Apps Decision Rule Rationale Compensatory “I selected the smartphone app that came out as the best when I balanced the good ratings against the bad ratings.” Conjunctive “I selected the smartphone app that had no bad features.” Disjunctive “I picked the smartphone app that excelled in at least one attribute.” Lexicographic “I chose the smartphone app that scored the best on the attribute that I consider to be the most important.” Affect referral “I bought the smartphone app with the highest overall rating.” Recognition “I downloaded the smartphone app that seemed familiar.” Majority Vote “I downloaded the smartphone app that had the most downloads.”"},{"img":"slides/ch14/21.jpg","title":"Segmentation by Shopping Strategy","text":"Segmentation by Shopping Strategy Practical Loyalists Bottom-Line Price Shoppers Opportunistic Switchers Deal Hunters"},{"img":"slides/ch14/22.jpg","title":"Coping with Incomplete Information","text":"Coping with Incomplete Information Delay the decision until information is obtained Ignore missing information Change the decision strategy to accommodate missing information “Construct” the missing information"},{"img":"slides/ch14/23.jpg","title":"Decision Making: Output","text":"Decision Making: Output Three types of outputs: Trial Repeat purchase/brand loyalty Post-purchase evaluation Positive/negative disconfirmation of expectations Cognitive dissonance Discussion Question: How do consumers cope with cognitive dissonance?"},{"img":"slides/ch14/24.jpg","title":"Learning Objective 14.2","text":"Learning Objective 14.2 14.2 To understand the dynamics of buying gifts."},{"img":"slides/ch14/25.jpg","title":"Gifting Behavior","text":"Gifting Behavior Defined A gift exchange that takes place between a giver and a recipient. The definition is broad in nature and embraces gifts given voluntarily, gifts that are an obligation, gifts given to (and received from) others and gifts to oneself (“self-gifts”)."},{"img":"slides/ch14/26.jpg","title":"Types of Gifting","text":"Types of Gifting Intergroup Gifting Intercategory Gifting Intragroup Gifting Intrapersonal Gifting Discussion Question: What questions might one ask during the gifting process?"},{"img":"slides/ch14/27.jpg","title":"Learning Objective 14.3","text":"Learning Objective 14.3 14.3 To understand how new products gain acceptance and how individuals decide whether or not to adopt them."},{"img":"slides/ch14/28.jpg","title":"Diffusion of Innovations (1 of 2)","text":"Diffusion of Innovations (1 of 2) Defined The framework for exploring the evolution of consumers’ acceptance of new products throughout the social system."},{"img":"slides/ch14/29.jpg","title":"Diffusion of Innovations (2 of 2)","text":"Diffusion of Innovations (2 of 2) The process includes four elements: The innovation The channels of communication The social system Time"},{"img":"slides/ch14/30.jpg","title":"Innovation Adoption Process","text":"Innovation Adoption Process Defined Focuses on the stages through which an individual consumer passes when deciding to accept or reject a new product."},{"img":"slides/ch14/31.jpg","title":"Types of Innovations","text":"Types of Innovations Continuous innovation Dynamically continuous innovation Discontinuous innovation"},{"img":"slides/ch14/32.jpg","title":"Product Features That Affect Adoption","text":"Product Features That Affect Adoption Relative Advantage Compatibility Complexity Technical fear most widespread concern of innovators Rapid obsolescence, social rejection and physical harm are other fears Trial-ability Observability (communicability)"},{"img":"slides/ch14/33.jpg","title":"Encourages Trial","text":"Encourages Trial Source: Tempur Sealy International, I n c."},{"img":"slides/ch14/34.jpg","title":"The Adoption Process","text":"The Adoption Process Awareness Interest Evaluation Trial Adoption Discussion Question: What is missing from the model of Consumer Adoption?"}],"questions":[{"id":"14-2","n":2,"type":"mcq","q":"Purchasing a diamond represents ________ because consumers buy diamonds infrequently and have no established criteria for evaluating them.","answer":4,"exp":"Diamonds are expensive and bought rarely, and buyers have no established evaluation criteria. That is EXTENSIVE problem solving: lots of information needed and criteria must be set first.","options":["impulse purchase behavior","limited problem solving","habitual purchase behavior","routinized response behavior","extensive problem solving"]},{"id":"14-4","n":4,"type":"mcq","q":"The ________ process includes three components: input, process, and output.","answer":0,"exp":"The consumer decision-making model in Chapter 14 has three components: Input, Process and Output.","options":["consumer decision-making","routinized response","family branding","impulse purchase","consumer complexity"]},{"id":"14-5","n":5,"type":"mcq","q":"Extensive problem solving, limited problem solving, and routinized response behavior are three specific levels of ________.","answer":4,"exp":"The decision spectrum in Chapter 14 runs from Extensive Problem Solving to Limited Problem Solving to Routinized Response Behavior. These are three levels of consumer decision making.","options":["conjunctive decision rules","economic problem solving","affect referral rules","gifting behavior","consumer decision making"]},{"id":"14-6","n":6,"type":"mcq","q":"The first stage in the consumer decision-making process is ________.","answer":2,"exp":"Need recognition is the first stage of the process. Search and evaluation come after it.","options":["information search","evaluation of alternatives","need recognition","product choice","none of the above"]},{"id":"14-7","n":7,"type":"mcq","q":"A consumer is most likely to use ________ when buying an expensive, important, or technically complicated product or service for the first time.","answer":0,"exp":"Expensive, important or complex products bought for the first time need a lot of information and new criteria. That is extensive problem solving.","options":["extensive problem solving","economic problem solving","limited problem solving","passive problem solving","routinized response behavior"]},{"id":"14-8","n":8,"type":"mcq","q":"A consumer is most likely to use ________ when purchasing a new, updated version of something that he or she has purchased before, such as replacing an old laptop with a new one.","answer":2,"exp":"Replacing a laptop you've owned before: the criteria already exist and you only fine-tune with new information. That is LIMITED problem solving.","options":["extensive problem solving","economic problem solving","limited problem solving","passive problem solving","routinized response behavior"]},{"id":"14-9","n":9,"type":"mcq","q":"In cases of ________, the consumer needs a great deal of information to establish a set of criteria on which to judge specific brands and a correspondingly large amount of information concerning each of the brands to be considered.","answer":0,"exp":"Extensive problem solving: a lot of information is needed and you must first establish the criteria for evaluation.","options":["extensive problem solving","economic problem solving","limited problem solving","passive problem solving","routinized response behavior"]},{"id":"14-10","n":10,"type":"mcq","q":"In cases of ________, consumers have already established the basic criteria for evaluating the product category and the various brands in the category, but have not fully established preferences concerning a select group of brands.","answer":2,"exp":"Limited problem solving: the criteria are established, but preferences among brands still need fine-tuning with more information.","options":["extensive problem solving","economic problem solving","limited problem solving","passive problem solving","routinized response behavior"]},{"id":"14-11","n":11,"type":"mcq","q":"Martin needs a new hybrid-electric car that can seat four people comfortably and has a trunk that will hold a set of golf clubs, but isn't sure which brand of hybrid-electric car would be best. Martin's is a case of ________.","answer":2,"exp":"Martin knows his criteria (hybrid, seats four, fits golf clubs) but hasn't chosen a brand. Established criteria plus fine-tuning means LIMITED problem solving.","options":["habitual problem solving","economic problem solving","limited problem solving","passive problem solving","routinized response behavior"]},{"id":"14-12","n":12,"type":"mcq","q":"In cases of ________, consumers have experience with the product category and a wellestablished set of criteria with which to evaluate the brands they are considering.","answer":4,"exp":"Routinized response behavior: consumers have experience and well-established criteria, so they decide based on what they already know.","options":["extensive problem solving","economic problem solving","limited problem solving","passive problem solving","routinized response behavior"]},{"id":"14-13","n":13,"type":"mcq","q":"Maria needs a new pair of jeans. She knows that pants from American Eagle, in size 10, tend to fit her very well in the waist and hips and are long enough, so she goes to American Eagle and picks up a pair. Maria's is a case of ________.","answer":4,"exp":"Maria knows exactly what fits and buys it right away. That is routinized response behavior, deciding from what she already knows.","options":["extensive problem solving","economic problem solving","limited problem solving","passive problem solving","routinized response behavior"]},{"id":"14-14","n":14,"type":"mcq","q":"A firm's marketing activities that attempt to reach, inform, and persuade consumers to buy and use its products are ________.","answer":3,"exp":"The firm's efforts to reach, inform and persuade consumers are marketing inputs, the marketing mix in Chapter 14 (external influences).","options":["policy factors","output factors","process factors","marketing inputs","sociocultural inputs"]},{"id":"14-15","n":15,"type":"mcq","q":"A product's package, guarantees, mass-media advertising for the product, direct marketing for the product, and the selection of distribution channels to move the product from the manufacturer to the consumer are examples of ________.","answer":3,"exp":"Packaging, guarantees, advertising, direct marketing and distribution choices are all parts of the marketing mix, so they are MARKETING inputs.","options":["policy factors","output factors","process factors","marketing inputs","sociocultural inputs"]},{"id":"14-16","n":16,"type":"mcq","q":"Comments of a friend, an editorial in the newspaper, and usage of a product by a family member are examples of ________.","answer":4,"exp":"A friend's comments, an editorial and a family member's usage are non-commercial influences: family, peers and communications. They are SOCIOCULTURAL inputs.","options":["policy factors","output factors","process factors","marketing inputs","sociocultural inputs"]},{"id":"14-17","n":17,"type":"mcq","q":"The ________ component of the consumer decision-making model draws on external influences as sources of information about a particular product that influence a consumer's product-related values, attitudes, and behavior.","answer":3,"exp":"The INPUT stage brings in external influences (the marketing mix and sociocultural influences) that shape the consumer's values, attitudes and behavior.","options":["marketing mix","output","decision","input","emotional"]},{"id":"14-18","n":18,"type":"mcq","q":"Within the context of the model of consumer decision making, the marketing mix activities of organizations and non-marketing sociocultural influences are the ________ factors.","answer":3,"exp":"Marketing mix activities and sociocultural influences are the two kinds of INPUT factors.","options":["cognitive","output","decision","input","emotional"]},{"id":"14-19","n":19,"type":"mcq","q":"Within the context of the model of consumer decision making, a firm's ________ activities are a direct attempt to reach, inform, and persuade consumers to buy and use its products.","answer":0,"exp":"The firm's MARKETING MIX activities are its direct attempt to reach, inform and persuade consumers.","options":["marketing mix","output","decision","input","sociocultural environment"]},{"id":"14-20","n":20,"type":"mcq","q":"The consumer's experience with a product plays a major role in the ________ stage of the model of consumer decision making.","answer":1,"exp":"The consumer's experience is stored in the psychological field and used in the PROCESS stage (Chapter 14: Learning, Knowledge, Experience).","options":["input","process","trial","output","decision"],"book":"Textbook: the psychological field (motivation, perception, learning, personality, attitudes) and past experience both shape the process stage.","depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 14, p. 354","page":354},{"id":"14-21","n":21,"type":"mcq","q":"Within the context of the model of consumer decision making, the ________ include the unwritten codes of conduct that indicate which consumption behavior should be considered \"right\" or \"wrong.\"","answer":4,"exp":"Sociocultural influences such as family, peers, reference groups and culture carry unwritten codes of conduct about what is 'right' or 'wrong' to consume.","options":["marketing mix","output","decision","input","sociocultural influences"],"depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 14, p. 345","page":345},{"id":"14-22","n":22,"type":"mcq","q":"The ________ component of the consumer decision-making model is concerned with how consumers make decisions.","answer":0,"exp":"The PROCESS stage of the model is about how consumers actually make decisions: need recognition, prepurchase search and evaluation.","options":["process","output","decision","input","emotional"]},{"id":"14-23","n":23,"type":"mcq","q":"Within the context of the model of consumer decision making, the ________ represents the internal influences that affect consumers' decision-making processes.","answer":4,"exp":"Inside the Process stage in Chapter 14, the psychological field (motivation, personality, perception, learning, attitudes) is the INTERNAL influence on decisions.","options":["marketing mix","prepurchase search","decision","evaluation of alternatives","psychological field"]},{"id":"14-24","n":24,"type":"mcq","q":"Within the context of the model of consumer decision making, ________ is likely to occur when a consumer is faced with a \"problem.\"","answer":2,"exp":"Need recognition happens when the consumer faces a 'problem', meaning a gap between their current state and the state they want.","options":["marketing mix","prepurchase search","need recognition","evaluation of alternatives","psychological field"]},{"id":"14-25","n":25,"type":"mcq","q":"Chaz is tired of lugging around a digital camera on location site visits as he tries to identify potential retail properties, so he decides to buy a new cell phone with a high quality digital camera. The desire for the new camera was prompted by ________.","answer":2,"exp":"Chaz's frustration with carrying a separate camera creates the desire. That is need recognition.","options":["marketing mix","prepurchase search","need recognition","evaluation of alternatives","psychological field"]},{"id":"14-26","n":26,"type":"mcq","q":"John has decided that he needs to switch cellular service providers because his cell phone doesn't work in his new apartment. Within the context of the model of consumer decision making, this is an example of ________ need recognition.","answer":1,"exp":"John's phone doesn't work in his new apartment, so the product is failing to perform. That is ACTUAL STATE need recognition.","options":["hypothetical state","actual state","desired state","avoidance state","psychological state"],"depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 14, p. 346","page":346},{"id":"14-27","n":27,"type":"mcq","q":"Beth is tired of carrying around a cumbersome CD player and a heavy collection of CDs so she can listen to her music on-the-go, and so has decided to convert her CD music collection to MP3 and buy an iPod. This is an example of ________ need recognition.","answer":2,"exp":"Beth's CD player still works, but she wants something better and newer. That is DESIRED STATE need recognition.","options":["hypothetical state","acquired state","desired state","avoidance state","psychological state"],"depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 14, p. 346","page":346},{"id":"14-28","n":28,"type":"mcq","q":"When a product fails to perform satisfactorily, it triggers ________ need recognition.","answer":1,"exp":"When a product fails to perform satisfactorily, it triggers ACTUAL STATE need recognition.","options":["hypothetical state","actual state","desired state","avoidance state","psychological state"],"depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 14, p. 346","page":346},{"id":"14-29","n":29,"type":"mcq","q":"________ begins when a consumer perceives a need that might be satisfied by the purchase and consumption of a product.","answer":1,"exp":"Pre-purchase search begins once the consumer perceives a need that a purchase could satisfy. The consumer looks first in memory, then at external sources.","options":["Marketing mix","Pre-purchase search","Need recognition","Evaluation of alternatives","Psychological field"]},{"id":"14-30","n":30,"type":"mcq","q":"Past experience is considered a(n) ________ source of information.","answer":3,"exp":"Past experience lives in memory, so it is an INTERNAL source. Chapter 14: memory search versus external information.","options":["irrelevant","risky","external","internal","limited"],"depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 14, p. 348","page":348},{"id":"14-31","n":31,"type":"mcq","q":"Friends, neighbors, coworkers, and salespeople are considered ________ prepurchase information sources.","answer":4,"exp":"Friends, neighbors and salespeople are EXTERNAL information sources (Chapter 14: 'seek opinions of friends').","options":["impersonal","evoked","independent","network","external"],"depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 14, p. 348","page":348},{"id":"14-32","n":32,"type":"mcq","q":"Newspaper or magazine articles, direct-mail brochures, and websites are considered ________ pre-purchase information sources.","answer":0,"exp":"Articles, brochures and websites come from outside the consumer's memory, so they are EXTERNAL sources.","options":["external","evoked","independent","network","personal"],"depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 14, p. 348","page":348},{"id":"14-33","n":33,"type":"mcq","q":"In high risk situations, consumers are likely to engage in complex and extensive ________.","answer":0,"exp":"Factors such as high price, social visibility and unsatisfactory past experience raise risk, and risk increases search. High-risk purchases lead to extensive information search.","options":["information search","need recognition","routinized behavior","psychological field reorganization","self-assessment"],"depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 14, p. 346","page":346},{"id":"14-34","n":34,"type":"mcq","q":"Which of the following is an important form of external information?","answer":2,"exp":"Chapter 14: shopping provides external information. It is an important external source.","options":["memories","past experience","shopping","heuristics","knowledge"]},{"id":"14-35","n":35,"type":"mcq","q":"External search effort associated with purchase is greatest when ________.","answer":3,"exp":"Chapter 14: search is greatest when product category knowledge is LOW.","options":["consumers have high product category knowledge","consumers do not care about the purchase","the purchase situation is low-risk","product category knowledge is low","subjective knowledge is high"]},{"id":"14-38","n":38,"type":"mcq","q":"Which of the following is NOT a factor that increases prepurchase search?","answer":1,"exp":"Chapter 14 says LONG periods between purchases increase search. SHORT periods between purchases do NOT.","options":["high price","short periods of time between successive purchases","no past experience","a socially visible product","high product involvement"]},{"id":"14-39","n":39,"type":"mcq","q":"________ is the number of alternatives and the amount of information available for each alternative.","answer":4,"exp":"Task complexity is the number of alternatives and the amount of information available for each one.","options":["Information organization","Psychological field","External source","Time constraint","Task complexity"]},{"id":"14-40","n":40,"type":"mcq","q":"________ is the presentation, format, and content of available information.","answer":0,"exp":"Information organization is the presentation, format and content of the available information.","options":["Information organization","Psychological field","External source","Time constraint","Task complexity"]},{"id":"14-41","n":41,"type":"mcq","q":"The ________ refers to the specific brands a consumer considers in making a purchase within a particular product category.","answer":4,"exp":"The evoked (consideration) set is the specific brands a consumer considers buying in a category.","options":["inert set","inept set","emotional set","independent set","evoked set"],"depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 14, p. 349","page":349},{"id":"14-42","n":42,"type":"mcq","q":"Tom wants a candy bar and is trying to decide between a Crunch, a Snickers, or a Milky Way. These three candy bars are in Tom's ________.","answer":4,"exp":"Crunch, Snickers and Milky Way are the brands Tom is actually considering, so they are his EVOKED set.","options":["inert set","inept set","emotional set","independent set","evoked set"],"depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 14, p. 349","page":349},{"id":"14-43","n":43,"type":"mcq","q":"The ________ consists of brands the consumer excludes from the purchase consideration because they are felt to be unacceptable.","answer":1,"exp":"The INEPT set is brands the consumer excludes because they are unacceptable, for example poor quality.","options":["inert set","inept set","emotional set","independent set","evoked set"],"depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 14, p. 349","page":349},{"id":"14-44","n":44,"type":"mcq","q":"The ________ consists of brands the consumer is indifferent toward because they are perceived as not having any particular advantages.","answer":0,"exp":"The INERT set is brands the consumer is indifferent toward because they offer no particular advantage.","options":["inert set","inept set","emotional set","independent set","evoked set"],"depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 14, p. 349","page":349},{"id":"14-45","n":45,"type":"mcq","q":"Helen enjoys Jack's Links beef jerky. By contrast, she is indifferent to Oberto beef jerky, and dislikes Pemmican beef jerky. Oberto beef jerky is part of Helen's ________.","answer":0,"exp":"Helen is indifferent to Oberto, so it is in her INERT set. She dislikes Pemmican (inept set), and Jack's Links is in her evoked set.","options":["inert set","inept set","emotional set","independent set","evoked set"],"depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 14, p. 349","page":349},{"id":"14-46","n":46,"type":"mcq","q":"________, which consists of a brand's trustworthiness and expertise, improves the chances that the brand will be included in the consideration set.","answer":3,"exp":"Chapter 14: brand credibility, meaning trustworthiness and expertise, affects perceived quality and risk. It improves a brand's chance of getting into the consideration set.","options":["Brand personality","Brand risk","Brand effort","Brand credibility","Brand attractiveness"]},{"id":"14-47","n":47,"type":"mcq","q":"________ are procedures used by consumers to reduce the burden of making complex decisions by providing guidelines or routines that make the process less taxing.","answer":2,"exp":"Decision rules are shortcuts or procedures that make complex decisions less taxing.","options":["Evoked sets","Inert sets","Decision rules","Inept sets","Independent sets"],"depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 14, p. 351","page":351},{"id":"14-48","n":48,"type":"mcq","q":"Consumers often develop shortcut decision rules to facilitate the decision-making process and to cope with ________.","answer":1,"exp":"Consumers have limited information-processing capacity. Shortcut decision rules help them cope with TOO MUCH information.","options":["lack of information","too much information","contradicting information","negative information","inconsistent information"]},{"id":"14-49","n":49,"type":"mcq","q":"Karen is buying a new laptop. She is looking for a light-weight computer. The laptop she purchases is a little heavier than she had originally hoped, but she was willing to accept the extra weight for a computer with a bigger, clearer screen. Karen made her purchase decision using a(n) ________.","answer":3,"exp":"Karen balanced a weakness (weight) against a strength (screen). Letting one attribute compensate for another is a COMPENSATORY rule.","options":["affective decision rule","noncompensatory decision rule","effective decision rule","compensatory decision rule","cognitive decision rule"]},{"id":"14-50","n":50,"type":"mcq","q":"Bob wants to save electricity and considers energy-efficient light bulbs. He finds that the light they emit is too faint to be acceptable, and so he returns to using less energy-efficient bulbs. Bob made his bulb choice using a(n) ________.","answer":1,"exp":"The light level failed Bob's minimum, so he rejected the bulbs no matter how good their energy savings were. Strengths can't make up for a failed attribute in a NONCOMPENSATORY rule.","options":["affective decision rule","noncompensatory decision rule","effective decision rule","compensatory decision rule","cognitive decision rule"]},{"id":"14-51","n":51,"type":"mcq","q":"Conjunctive, disjunctive, and lexicographic rules are examples of ________.","answer":1,"exp":"Chapter 14 lists conjunctive, lexicographic and disjunctive under NONCOMPENSATORY rules.","options":["affective decision rules","noncompensatory decision rules","effective decision rules","compensatory decision rules","cognitive decision rules"]},{"id":"14-52","n":52,"type":"mcq","q":"In a(n) ________, the consumer establishes a separate, minimally acceptable level as a cutoff point for each brand attribute he or she is considering.","answer":3,"exp":"Conjunctive rule: a minimum cutoff for EACH attribute, as in 'I selected the app that had no bad features'.","options":["lexicographic decision rule","affect referral decision rule","disjunctive decision rule","conjunctive decision rule","cognitive decision rule"]},{"id":"14-53","n":53,"type":"mcq","q":"Hank wants a new digital camera and will accept for consideration any camera with greater than 10 megapixel resolution. Hank is using a(n) ________.","answer":2,"exp":"Hank accepts any camera that passes the cutoff on one attribute (resolution). That matches the disjunctive logic in Chapter 14: an option qualifies if it meets or excels on at least one attribute.","options":["lexicographic decision rule","affect referral decision rule","disjunctive decision rule","conjunctive decision rule","cognitive decision rule"]},{"id":"14-54","n":54,"type":"mcq","q":"In a(n) ________, the consumer ranks a product's attributes in terms of perceived relevance or importance, then compares the various alternatives in terms of the single attribute that is considered most important. When two or more alternatives are comparable for the most important attribute, the consumer rates those alternatives on the basis of the second most important attribute.","answer":0,"exp":"Lexicographic rule: rank the attributes and choose the brand that scores best on the MOST important one. If there is a tie, move to the next attribute.","options":["lexicographic decision rule","affect referral decision rule","disjunctive decision rule","conjunctive decision rule","cognitive decision rule"]},{"id":"14-55","n":55,"type":"mcq","q":"A consumer who selects the product with the highest overall rating when considering all its attributes has used a(n) ________.","answer":1,"exp":"Table 14.1: affect referral means 'I bought the app with the highest overall rating'. It relies on an overall impression without assessing individual attributes.","options":["lexicographic decision rule","affect referral decision rule","disjunctive decision rule","conjunctive decision rule","cognitive decision rule"]},{"id":"14-56","n":56,"type":"mcq","q":"________ are consumers who use coupons or sales to decide among brands and products that fall within their evoked set.","answer":1,"exp":"Opportunistic switchers use coupons and sales to choose among brands that are already in their evoked set.","options":["Practical loyalists","Opportunistic switchers","Bottom-line price shoppers","Deal hunters","Cherry pickers"],"depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 14, p. 352","page":352},{"id":"14-57","n":57,"type":"mcq","q":"Which of the following is NOT one of the ways consumers cope with missing information during the decision-making process?","answer":0,"exp":"Chapter 14 lists four coping strategies: delay the decision, ignore the missing information, change the decision strategy, or 'construct' (infer) the missing information. Arguing about why the information matters is NOT one of them.","options":["develop arguments for why the missing information is important","delay the decision until the missing information is obtained","ignore missing information and continue with current decision rule using available attribute information","change customarily used decision strategy to one that better accommodates missing information","infer the missing information"]},{"id":"14-58","n":58,"type":"mcq","q":"A ________ is the exploratory phase of purchase behavior in which consumers attempt to evaluate a product through direct use.","answer":1,"exp":"A trial is the exploratory first purchase, where the consumer evaluates the product by using it.","options":["gift purchase","trial purchase","loyalty purchase","repeat purchase","long-term commitment purchase"],"depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 14, p. 353","page":353},{"id":"14-59","n":59,"type":"mcq","q":"________ behavior is closely related to the concept of brand loyalty, which most firms try to encourage because it contributes to greater stability in the marketplace.","answer":3,"exp":"Repeat purchase is closely related to brand loyalty and brings stability to the market.","options":["Gifting","Trial purchase","Permission marketing","Repeat purchase","Long-term commitment purchase"],"depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 14, p. 353","page":353},{"id":"14-60","n":60,"type":"mcq","q":"________ occurs when a product's performance is below expectations and the consumer is dissatisfied.","answer":1,"exp":"Negative disconfirmation: performance falls BELOW expectations, so the consumer is dissatisfied.","options":["Positive disconfirmation of expectations","Negative disconfirmation of expectations","Reverse dissonance","Postpurchase analysis","Brand loyalty"],"depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 14, p. 353","page":353},{"id":"14-62","n":62,"type":"mcq","q":"________ can be thought of as the gift exchange that takes place between a giver and a recipient.","answer":2,"exp":"Chapter 14 defines gifting behavior as a gift exchange between a giver and a recipient.","options":["Postpurchase evaluation","Loyalty programs","Gifting behavior","Permission marketing","Long-term commitment purchase"]},{"id":"14-64","n":64,"type":"mcq","q":"The Lambert family (which has five family members) gives their babysitter a high school graduation gift. This is an example of ________.","answer":1,"exp":"Intercategory gifting is between an individual and a group. Here a family (group) gives to the babysitter (individual).","options":["interpersonal gifting","intercategory gifting","intergroup gifting","intrapersonal gifting","intragroup gifting"],"depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 14, p. 355","page":355},{"id":"14-65","n":65,"type":"mcq","q":"Kim finishes a big project for school and treats herself to a coffee and piece of cake at Starbucks. This is an example of ________.","answer":3,"exp":"Intrapersonal gifting is a self-gift: Kim treats herself.","options":["interpersonal gifting","intercategory gifting","intergroup gifting","intrapersonal gifting","intragroup gifting"],"depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 14, p. 355","page":355},{"id":"14-66","n":66,"type":"mcq","q":"Custom Interiors, an interior design firm, sends a holiday basket of Florida citrus fruit to its accounting firm. This is an example of ________.","answer":2,"exp":"Intergroup gifting is one group giving to another: a firm gives to another firm.","options":["interpersonal gifting","intercategory gifting","intergroup gifting","intrapersonal gifting","intragroup gifting"],"depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 14, p. 355","page":355},{"id":"14-67","n":67,"type":"mcq","q":"Marge and Steve treat themselves (a small family unit) to a Caribbean cruise for their 25th wedding anniversary. This is an example of ________.","answer":4,"exp":"Intragroup gifting is a group giving to itself, like a couple or family treating themselves.","options":["interpersonal gifting","intercategory gifting","intergroup gifting","intrapersonal gifting","intragroup gifting"],"depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 14, p. 355","page":355},{"id":"14-68","n":68,"type":"mcq","q":"________ is a macro process concerned with the spread of a new product from its source to the consuming public.","answer":2,"exp":"Diffusion of innovations is the framework for how consumers' acceptance of new products spreads through the social system.","options":["Adoption of innovations","Assumption","Diffusion of innovations","Innovation","Introduction"]},{"id":"14-69","n":69,"type":"mcq","q":"A ________ involves the introduction of a modified product rather than a totally new product, and has the least disruptive influence on established patterns.","answer":4,"exp":"A continuous innovation is a modified product with the least disruption to established behavior.","options":["discontinuous innovation","dynamically discontinuous innovation","dynamically continuous innovation","statically continuous innovation","continuous innovation"],"depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 14, p. 357","page":357},{"id":"14-70","n":70,"type":"mcq","q":"Digital cameras and digital video recorders were somewhat disruptive to consumers, but did not change the basic behavior patterns established with photography and film. These are best described as ________.","answer":2,"exp":"Digital cameras changed the product a lot but not the basic behavior of taking photos. That is a DYNAMICALLY continuous innovation.","options":["discontinuous innovations","dynamically discontinuous innovations","dynamically continuous innovations","statically continuous innovations","continuous innovations"],"depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 14, p. 357","page":357},{"id":"14-71","n":71,"type":"mcq","q":"The degree to which potential customers perceive new products as superior to existing substitutes is its ________.","answer":0,"exp":"Relative advantage is how superior the new product seems compared to existing substitutes.","options":["relative advantage","compatibility","complexity","trialability","observability"],"depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 14, p. 357","page":357},{"id":"14-72","n":72,"type":"mcq","q":"________, the degree to which a new product is difficult to understand or use, is especially important when it comes to innovation in high-tech consumer products.","answer":2,"exp":"Complexity is how hard the product is to understand or use. Technical fear is the most widespread concern.","options":["Relative advantage","Compatibility","Complexity","Trialability","Observability"],"depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 14, p. 357","page":357},{"id":"14-73","n":73,"type":"mcq","q":"Many software companies allow customers to use limited versions of their software free for 30 days. This strategy tries to increase the diffusion of a new product through increasing ________.","answer":3,"exp":"Free 30-day versions let people try before committing. That increases TRIALABILITY.","options":["relative advantage","compatibility","complexity","trialability","observability"],"depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 14, p. 357","page":357},{"id":"14-83","n":83,"type":"tf","q":"Needs are created when the actual state of a customer declines.","answer":true,"exp":"TRUE. Actual state need recognition: a need arises when the consumer's current state declines, for example when a product fails.","depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 14, p. 346","page":346},{"id":"14-84","n":84,"type":"tf","q":"The number of alternatives available when making a purchase decision does not affect the decision-making process.","answer":false,"exp":"FALSE. 'Many alternative brands' is a factor that INCREASES search. The number of alternatives also raises task complexity."},{"id":"14-85","n":85,"type":"tf","q":"All consumer decision-making situations require the same degree of information search.","answer":false,"exp":"FALSE. The decision spectrum shows that the amount of search varies from extensive to limited to routinized."},{"id":"14-86","n":86,"type":"tf","q":"When consumers have no established criteria for evaluating a product category, they engage in extensive problem solving.","answer":true,"exp":"TRUE. With no established criteria, consumers must create them. That is extensive problem solving."},{"id":"14-87","n":87,"type":"tf","q":"Past experience is considered an external source of information. The greater the relevant past experience, the less internal information the consumer is likely to need to reach a decision.","answer":false,"exp":"FALSE. Past experience is an INTERNAL source. The more relevant experience a consumer has, the less EXTERNAL information they need."},{"id":"14-88","n":88,"type":"tf","q":"In high-risk situations, consumers are likely to engage in complex and extensive information search and evaluation.","answer":true,"exp":"TRUE. Higher risk leads to more extensive search and evaluation."},{"id":"14-89","n":89,"type":"tf","q":"In buying a truck, Bob will only consider American-made trucks because he believes foreign truck brands, such as Nissan and Toyota, are of inferior quality. Nissan and Toyota are items in Bob's inept set.","answer":true,"exp":"TRUE. Bob excludes Nissan and Toyota as unacceptable, so they are in his INEPT set.","depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 14, p. 349","page":349},{"id":"14-90","n":90,"type":"tf","q":"The inert set consists of the small number of brands the consumer is familiar with, remembers, and finds acceptable.","answer":false,"exp":"FALSE. That describes the EVOKED set. The inert set holds brands the consumer is indifferent toward.","depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 14, p. 349","page":349},{"id":"14-91","n":91,"type":"tf","q":"It is not essential that a product be part of a consumer's evoked set if it is to be considered at all.","answer":false,"exp":"FALSE. A brand MUST be in the evoked (consideration) set to be considered at all.","depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 14, p. 349","page":349},{"id":"14-92","n":92,"type":"tf","q":"A unique feature of a compensatory decision rule is that it allows a positive evaluation of a brand on one attribute to balance out a negative evaluation on some other attribute.","answer":true,"exp":"TRUE. Compensatory rules weigh each attribute and add up a score, so a strength can balance a weakness."},{"id":"14-93","n":93,"type":"tf","q":"Sue is looking for a new car. She had eliminated any car without comfortable seating for at least four people and that gets less than an average of 35 miles per gallon in fuel efficiency. Sue has used a lexicographic decision rule to narrow down her choices.","answer":false,"exp":"FALSE. Sue set minimum cutoffs on EACH attribute (seats and mpg). That is CONJUNCTIVE, not lexicographic."},{"id":"14-94","n":94,"type":"tf","q":"Task complexity, information organization, and time constraint are the three major contextual factors in decision tasks.","answer":true,"exp":"TRUE. Chapter 14 lists the contextual factors: task complexity, information organization and time constraints."},{"id":"14-95","n":95,"type":"tf","q":"Repeat purchase usually signifies that the product meets with the consumer's approval and that he or she is willing to use it again and in larger quantities.","answer":true,"exp":"TRUE. Repeat purchase signals approval and willingness to use the product again, and it builds brand loyalty."},{"id":"14-96","n":96,"type":"tf","q":"Negative disconfirmation of expectations involves the performance of a product exceeding the consumer's expectations, leading to consumer satisfaction.","answer":false,"exp":"FALSE. Performance ABOVE expectations is POSITIVE disconfirmation. Negative disconfirmation means performance falls short.","depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 14, p. 353","page":353},{"id":"14-97","n":97,"type":"tf","q":"When a single person gives a gift to a family as a thank you for letting him visit in their home for a week, it is an example of intercategory giving.","answer":true,"exp":"TRUE. An individual giving to a group (the family) is intercategory gifting.","depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 14, p. 355","page":355},{"id":"14-99","n":99,"type":"tf","q":"Gifting behavior includes gifts we give because we want to and gifts we give out of obligation.","answer":true,"exp":"TRUE. Chapter 14: gifting includes gifts given voluntarily and gifts given out of obligation."},{"id":"14-100","n":100,"type":"tf","q":"Intrapersonal gifting is a state of mind where the giver and the receiver are the same individual.","answer":true,"exp":"TRUE. Intrapersonal gifting is the self-gift, where the giver and receiver are the same person.","depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 14, p. 355","page":355},{"id":"14-101","n":101,"type":"tf","q":"Diffusion of innovation is the macro process by which the acceptance of an innovation takes place among members of a social system over time.","answer":true,"exp":"TRUE. That matches Chapter 14's definition: how acceptance of new products evolves through the social system over time."},{"id":"14-102","n":102,"type":"tf","q":"The four elements of the diffusion of innovations process include: the innovation, the channels of communication, the social system, and time.","answer":true,"exp":"TRUE. Chapter 14 lists the four elements: the innovation, the channels of communication, the social system and time."},{"id":"14-103","n":103,"type":"tf","q":"Airplanes, radios, TVs, automobiles, fax machines, PCs, videocassette recorders, and the Internet are all examples of dynamically continuous innovations.","answer":false,"exp":"FALSE. Airplanes, TVs, PCs and the Internet created totally new behaviors. They are DISCONTINUOUS innovations.","depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 14, p. 357","page":357},{"id":"14-104","n":104,"type":"tf","q":"Technological fear includes fear of technical complexity, fear of rapid obsolescence, fear of social rejection, and fear of physical harm.","answer":true,"exp":"TRUE. Chapter 14: technical fear (the most widespread), rapid obsolescence, social rejection and physical harm."},{"id":"14-105","n":105,"type":"tf","q":"Products that are used in private are diffused more easily than products that have a high degree of social visibility.","answer":false,"exp":"FALSE. Observability helps diffusion. Socially VISIBLE products spread more easily than privately used ones.","depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 14, p. 358","page":358},{"id":"14-106","n":106,"type":"tf","q":"One of the weaknesses of the innovation adoption process framework is it does not adequately provide for the possibility of evaluation and rejection of a new product or service after each stage.","answer":true,"exp":"TRUE. That is the answer to Chapter 14's question 'What is missing?' The model doesn't show rejection or re-evaluation after each stage.","depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 14, p. 358","page":358},{"id":"14-107","n":107,"type":"tf","q":"Ricky had a positive experience when she tried the new cell phone from a well-known cell phone manufacturer, so she decides to regularly use the new cell phone. She is currently in the adoption stage of the innovation adoption process.","answer":true,"exp":"TRUE. After a positive trial, Ricky decides to use the phone regularly. That is the ADOPTION stage."}],"book":{"first":344,"last":361,"count":18}},{"id":6,"title":"Consumer Attitude Formation and Change","exam":"final","slides":[{"img":"slides/ch6/01.jpg","title":"Chapter 6: Consumer Attitude Formation and Change","text":"Consumer Behavior Twelfth Edition, Global Edition. Chapter 6 Consumer Attitude Formation and Change"},{"img":"slides/ch6/02.jpg","title":"Learning Objectives (1 of 2)","text":"Learning Objectives (1 of 2) 6.1 To understand how consumers' attitudes influence their decision-making. 6.2 To understand the tri-component attitude model. 6.3 To understand how to apply multiattribute models to change consumers' attitudes. 6.4 To understand how to alter consumers' attitudes by making particular needs prominent."},{"img":"slides/ch6/03.jpg","title":"Learning Objectives (2 of 2)","text":"Learning Objectives (2 of 2) 6.5 To understand cognitive elaboration and the two routes to persuasion. 6.6 To understand cognitive dissonance and resolving cognitive conflicts. 6.7 To understand how people assign causality to events."},{"img":"slides/ch6/04.jpg","title":"Learning Objective 6.1","text":"Learning Objective 6.1 To understand how consumers' attitudes influence their decision-making."},{"img":"slides/ch6/05.jpg","title":"Attitude","text":"Attitude Defined: A learned predisposition to behave in a consistently favorable or unfavorable manner with respect to a given object."},{"img":"slides/ch6/06.jpg","title":"Example: Wrangler","text":"Example: Wrangler (Traveler Jeans ad)"},{"img":"slides/ch6/07.jpg","title":"Attitude Formation","text":"Attitude Formation. Consumers learn attitudes. Sources of attitude formation: Experience; Family and friends; Media/Internet/Social Media"},{"img":"slides/ch6/08.jpg","title":"Role of Personality Factors","text":"Role of Personality Factors: Need for cognition; Innovativeness"},{"img":"slides/ch6/09.jpg","title":"Role of Attitudes","text":"Role of Attitudes. Attitudes are consistent with behavior. How do situations affect attitudes? Table 6.1 Situations Affecting Attitudes (Energizer batteries - hurricane coming; Mini Cooper - buying a new car with high gas prices; Cheerios - high cholesterol; Wall Street Journal - extra cash; Delta Airlines - friend's bachelor party; Maxwell House - need to stay awake; Stouffer's - want dinner at home)"},{"img":"slides/ch6/10.jpg","title":"Learning Objective 6.2","text":"Learning Objective 6.2 To understand the tri-component attitude model. Cognitive: knowledge and perception of product or brand features, expressed as beliefs about a brand. Affective: emotions and feelings about a product or brand, expressed as favorable or unfavorable attitude toward a brand. Conative: actions or behavior toward a product or brand, expressed as intention to purchase a brand."},{"img":"slides/ch6/11.jpg","title":"The Cognitive Component","text":"The Cognitive Component. Table 6.2 Beliefs about Two Smart Speakers (Google Home vs Amazon Echo: voice commands, prompt word, works with smart home, customizable appearance, personal assistant, music streaming)"},{"img":"slides/ch6/12.jpg","title":"The Affective Component","text":"The Affective Component. Dapper Dan Aftershave Balm: rate how your skin felt (refreshed, tightened, smoothed, suppled, revived) from Strongly Agree to Strongly Disagree"},{"img":"slides/ch6/13.jpg","title":"Likert Scale","text":"Likert Scale Defined: The most popular form of attitude scale, where consumers are asked to check numbers corresponding to their level of agreement or disagreement with a series of statements about the studied object."},{"img":"slides/ch6/14.jpg","title":"Semantic Differential Scale","text":"Semantic Differential Scale. HI Lipgloss: mark an X between bipolar adjectives (Refreshed - Not refreshed, Supple - Not supple, Revived - Not revived)"},{"img":"slides/ch6/15.jpg","title":"The Conative Component","text":"The Conative Component. Intention-to-buy scales: I definitely will buy it… I definitely will not buy it; How likely are you to buy in the next three months: Very likely… Very unlikely"},{"img":"slides/ch6/16.jpg","title":"Discussion Questions","text":"Discussion Questions: Explain your attitude toward your college/university based on the tricomponent attitude model. Isolate the cognitive, affective, and conative elements."},{"img":"slides/ch6/17.jpg","title":"Altering Consumer Attitudes","text":"Altering Consumer Attitudes: Changing beliefs about products; Changing brand image; Changing beliefs about competing brands (Avocados From Mexico ad: Relax, it's the good fats)"},{"img":"slides/ch6/18.jpg","title":"Learning Objective 6.3","text":"Learning Objective 6.3 To understand how to apply multiattribute models to change consumers' attitudes."},{"img":"slides/ch6/19.jpg","title":"Attitude-Toward-Object Model","text":"Attitude-Toward-Object Model. Used to change attitudes. Ways: Add an attribute; Change perceived importance of an attribute; Develop new products (Kraft Mac & Cheese: It's changed. But it hasn't.)"},{"img":"slides/ch6/20.jpg","title":"Other Multi-Attribute Models","text":"Other Multi-Attribute Models: Theory of Reasoned Action; Theory of trying-to-consume; Attitude-toward-the-ad model. How are the models different from the attitude-toward-object model?"},{"img":"slides/ch6/21.jpg","title":"Multiattribute Models","text":"Multiattribute Models. Attitude toward the object: does a brand have the needed attribute? what is the importance of that attribute? Attitude toward behavior: attitude toward the brand; how do I feel about buying this brand? Theory of Reasoned Action: tri-component attitude model; normative beliefs; motivation to comply with norms. Theory of Trying to Consume: attitude toward the behavior; personal impediments; environmental impediments. Attitude toward the ad: attitudes toward brands are formed based on how consumers feel about the advertisements for these brands. Attitude-toward-social-media posts: attitudes toward brands are formed based on how consumers feel about what they see on social media about the brands."},{"img":"slides/ch6/22.jpg","title":"Learning Objective 6.4","text":"Learning Objective 6.4 To understand how to alter consumers' attitudes by making particular needs prominent."},{"img":"slides/ch6/23.jpg","title":"Functional Approach","text":"Functional Approach: Utilitarian function; Ego-defensive function; Value-expressive function; Knowledge function; Associate brands with worthy causes and events"},{"img":"slides/ch6/24.jpg","title":"Example: Utilitarian Function","text":"Example: Utilitarian Function (Lysol wipes: paper towels spread harmful germs, Lysol kills harmful germs)"},{"img":"slides/ch6/25.jpg","title":"Learning Objective 6.5","text":"Learning Objective 6.5 To understand cognitive elaboration and the two routes to persuasion."},{"img":"slides/ch6/26.jpg","title":"Elaboration Likelihood Model","text":"Elaboration Likelihood Model Defined: The proposition that attitudes can be changed by either one of two different routes to persuasion - a central route or a peripheral route - and that the cognitive elaboration related to the processing of information received via each route is different."},{"img":"slides/ch6/27.jpg","title":"Central Route","text":"Central Route: High involvement; Considered thought and cognitive processing; Learning through attribute-based information, high quality arguments, exertion of effort to learn, comprehend, evaluate; Comparative ads; Objective knowledge"},{"img":"slides/ch6/28.jpg","title":"Peripheral Route","text":"Peripheral Route: Low involvement; Little thought and little information processing; Learning through repetition, passive processing of visual cues, holistic processing; Non-comparative ads; Subjective knowledge"},{"img":"slides/ch6/29.jpg","title":"Applications (1 of 2)","text":"Applications (1 of 2): Comparative ads processed centrally; noncomparative ads processed peripherally. Product knowledge: higher objective knowledge for utilitarian products than hedonic products; higher subjective knowledge for hedonic products than utilitarian products."},{"img":"slides/ch6/30.jpg","title":"Learning Objective 6.6","text":"Learning Objective 6.6 To understand cognitive dissonance and resolving cognitive conflicts."},{"img":"slides/ch6/31.jpg","title":"Dissonance","text":"Dissonance: Cognitive dissonance; Post-purchase dissonance; Ways to reduce post-purchase dissonance: 1. Rationalize decision 2. Seek advertisements that support choices (avoid competitive ads) 3. Sell friends on the positive features of the purchase 4. Seek reassurance from satisfied owners"},{"img":"slides/ch6/32.jpg","title":"Learning Objective 6.7","text":"Learning Objective 6.7 To understand how people assign causality to events."},{"img":"slides/ch6/33.jpg","title":"Attribution Theory","text":"Attribution Theory: Self-perception attribution; Defensive attribution; Foot-in-the-door technique; Door-in-the-face technique"},{"img":"slides/ch6/34.jpg","title":"Defensive Attribution","text":"Defensive Attribution Defined: People generally accept (or take) credit for success (internal attribution), but assign failure to others or outside events (external attribution)."},{"img":"slides/ch6/35.jpg","title":"Applications (2 of 2)","text":"Applications (2 of 2): Why does the foot-in-the-door technique increase the likelihood that the requestee will fulfill a larger request? How is it different from the door-in-the-face technique?"},{"img":"slides/ch6/36.jpg","title":"Analyzing Self-Attributions","text":"Analyzing Self-Attributions. Table 6.4 Reviewing Self-Attributions: Distinctiveness, Consistency over time and varied situations, Consensus (alumnus donating to MBA program; amateur photographer with HP printer)"}],"questions":[{"id":"6-1","n":1,"type":"mcq","q":"In a consumer behavior context, ________ are learned predispositions to behave in a consistently favorable or unfavorable way with respect to a given object.","answer":0,"exp":"Chapter 6 defines an attitude as a learned predisposition to behave in a consistently favorable or unfavorable manner with respect to a given object.","options":["attitudes","beliefs","values","feelings","intentions"]},{"id":"6-2","n":2,"type":"mcq","q":"John is conducting research on American attitudes toward European car brands, particularly Volkswagen, Volvo, Mercedes, and BMW. The car brands he is researching are ________.","answer":2,"exp":"In the definition in Chapter 6, the 'given object' is the attitude object: the thing the attitude is about. Here that is each car brand.","options":["experiences","attitudes","objects","attributions","cognitions"],"depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 6, p. 143","page":143},{"id":"6-3","n":3,"type":"mcq","q":"The shift from no attitude to an attitude is a result of ________.","answer":2,"exp":"Chapter 6: consumers LEARN attitudes. Moving from no attitude to an attitude is the result of learning.","options":["biological pressures","environment","learning","genetic predisposition","none of the above"]},{"id":"6-4","n":4,"type":"mcq","q":"Attitudes stemming from ________ are more confidently held, more enduring, and more resistant to competitors' messages than attitudes originating from promotional messages only.","answer":1,"exp":"Experience is the first source of attitude formation in Chapter 6. Attitudes from actually using a product are held more confidently and resist competitors' messages better than attitudes formed from ads alone.","options":["print advertisements","product usage","websites","television advertisements","endorsers"],"depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 6, p. 144","page":144},{"id":"6-5","n":5,"type":"mcq","q":"Which of the following is true of attitudes and their relationship with behavior?","answer":3,"exp":"Chapter 6: attitudes are consistent with behavior. When consumers are free to act as they wish, their actions should match their attitudes, although the situation can interfere (Table 6.1).","options":["Attitudes are permanent, but the behaviors they reflect change over time.","Consumers always demonstrate consistency between their attitudes and their behaviors.","There is no demonstrable link between attitudes and behavior.","When consumers are free to act as they wish, we anticipate that their actions will be consistent with their attitudes.","Attitude change is always followed by behavior change."]},{"id":"6-6","n":6,"type":"mcq","q":"Marketers that offer coupons and free samples of new products to entice consumers to try them understand the importance of ________ in attitude formation.","answer":4,"exp":"Coupons and free samples get consumers to TRY the product. Direct experience is a key source of attitude formation.","options":["subjective norms","indirect experience","ego defense","attribution","direct experience"],"depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 6, p. 144","page":144},{"id":"6-7","n":7,"type":"mcq","q":"Attitudes that develop through ________ tend to be more confidently held, more enduring, and more resistant to attack than those developed via ________.","answer":0,"exp":"Attitudes formed through direct experience are more confidently held, more enduring and more resistant than those formed through indirect experience such as ads or word of mouth.","options":["direct experience; indirect experience","internal attribution; external attribution","utilitarian function; knowledge function","cognition; emotions","direct marketing; niche marketing"],"depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 6, p. 144","page":144},{"id":"6-8","n":8,"type":"mcq","q":"Consumers who have a high need for cognition are likely to ________.","answer":1,"exp":"Need for cognition is a personality factor in Chapter 6. People high in need for cognition enjoy thinking, so they respond positively to ads that are RICH in product information.","options":["form positive attitudes in response to ads that feature an attractive model or a celebrity","form a positive attitude in response to ads or direct mail that are rich in product-related information","form negative attitudes toward ads that are rich in product information","feel neutral toward ads that feature attractive models or product information","feel neutral toward ads that are rich in product-related information"],"depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 6, p. 144","page":144},{"id":"6-9","n":9,"type":"mcq","q":"According to the ________, attitudes consist of three major components: a cognitive component, an effective component, and a conative component.","answer":1,"exp":"The tri-component attitude model has three parts: cognitive, affective and conative.","options":["dual mediation model","tri-component attitude model","self-perception theory","multiattribute attitude model","functional approach"]},{"id":"6-10","n":10,"type":"mcq","q":"________ include the knowledge and perceptions that are acquired by a combination of direct experience with the attitude object and related information from varied sources.","answer":2,"exp":"The cognitive component is the knowledge and perceptions about the product, expressed as beliefs about the brand.","options":["Affectations","Emotions","Cognitions","Objectives","Conations"]},{"id":"6-11","n":11,"type":"mcq","q":"The ________ component of the tri-component attitude model includes a consumer's emotions or feelings about a particular product or brand.","answer":3,"exp":"The affective component is the consumer's emotions and feelings about the product or brand.","options":["conative","objective","cognitive","affective","situational"]},{"id":"6-12","n":12,"type":"mcq","q":"Roy is looking to buy a new HDTV set. He knows from friends that LCD set screens reflect less light than plasma set screens, but that LCD sets are also more subject to blurring than plasma sets. This is an example of the ________ component of his attitude toward HDTVs.","answer":2,"exp":"Roy's facts about LCD vs plasma screens are beliefs and knowledge about attributes. That is the COGNITIVE component, like the smart speaker beliefs table in Chapter 6.","options":["conative","objective","cognitive","affective","situational"]},{"id":"6-13","n":13,"type":"mcq","q":"Paula is a regular at Gino's Italian Bistro. She likes going there because the staff always recognizes her, greets her by name, and makes her feel welcome at the restaurant, so she feels an emotional connection with the staff and the restaurant. This is an example of the ________ component of her attitude toward Gino's.","answer":4,"exp":"Paula's emotional connection and good feelings about Gino's are the AFFECTIVE component.","options":["cognitive","conative","situational","perspective","affective"]},{"id":"6-14","n":14,"type":"mcq","q":"________ is concerned with the likelihood or tendency than an individual will undertake a specific action or behave in a particular way with regard to the attitude object.","answer":4,"exp":"The conative component is the likelihood that a person will act toward the object, expressed as intention to buy.","options":["Cognition","Affectation","Situation","Perspective","Conation"]},{"id":"6-15","n":15,"type":"mcq","q":"In marketing and consumer research, the conative component of the tri-component model is frequently treated as an expression of the consumer's ________.","answer":2,"exp":"Chapter 6: the conative component is expressed as intention to purchase the brand. Chapter 6 shows how it is measured.","options":["attitude","level of familiarity","intention to buy","attitude toward the object","ego-defensive function"]},{"id":"6-16","n":16,"type":"mcq","q":"________ assess(es) the likelihood of a consumer purchasing a product or behaving in a certain way.","answer":1,"exp":"The scales in Chapter 6 ('I definitely will buy it' to 'I definitely will not buy it', 'Very likely' to 'Very unlikely') are buyer intention scales.","options":["Cognitive dissonance theory","Buyer intention scales","Attitude-change strategies","Emotionally charged states","Self-perception theory"]},{"id":"6-17","n":17,"type":"mcq","q":"________ portray consumers' attitudes with regard to an attitude object as a function of consumers' perceptions and assessment of the key attributes or beliefs of that object.","answer":0,"exp":"Multiattribute models treat an attitude as a function of how consumers perceive and evaluate the object's key attributes or beliefs.","options":["Multiattribute attitude models","Functional models","Dual mediation models","Cognitive dissonance theories","Tri-component attitude models"]},{"id":"6-18","n":18,"type":"mcq","q":"Attitude-toward-object, attitude-toward-behavior, and theory of reasoned action models are examples of ________.","answer":4,"exp":"Chapter 6 groups attitude-toward-object, attitude-toward-behavior and the theory of reasoned action under multiattribute models.","options":["tri-component attitude models","buyer intention scales","attitude-change strategies","self-perception theories","multiattribute attitude models"]},{"id":"6-19","n":19,"type":"mcq","q":"According to the attitude-toward-object model, the consumer's attitude toward a product is a function of ________.","answer":2,"exp":"Attitude-toward-object model: does the brand have the needed attribute, and how important is that attribute?","options":["price","communication messages the consumer receives","the presence or absence of certain product-specific beliefs or attitudes","the consumer's intention to behave in a certain way","consumers' subjective norms"]},{"id":"6-20","n":20,"type":"mcq","q":"Microsoft wants to measure public attitudes toward the default media-playing software included in its Windows operating system. Which of the following types of multiattribute attitude models would be most appropriate for Microsoft to use?","answer":2,"exp":"Microsoft wants attitudes toward the software ITSELF (the object), so the attitude-toward-object model fits.","options":["theory of reasoned action model","trying-to-consume model","attitude-toward-object model","attitude-toward-behavior model","attitude-toward-the-ad model"]},{"id":"6-21","n":21,"type":"mcq","q":"The ________ is designed to capture the individual's attitude toward acting with respect to an object rather than the attitude toward the object itself.","answer":3,"exp":"The attitude-toward-behavior model asks 'How do I feel about BUYING this brand?' It measures the attitude toward acting, not toward the object.","options":["theory of reasoned action model","trying-to-consume model","attitude-toward-object model","attitude-toward-behavior model","attitude-toward-the-ad model"]},{"id":"6-22","n":22,"type":"mcq","q":"Jake feels that shopping in thrift shops shows a lack of class and sophistication. This attitude would be captured by which of the following multiattribute attitude models?","answer":3,"exp":"Jake's attitude is about the act of SHOPPING in thrift shops, not about the shops themselves. That is the attitude-toward-behavior model.","options":["theory of reasoned action model","trying-to-consume model","attitude-toward-object model","attitude-toward-behavior model","attitude-toward-the-ad model"]},{"id":"6-23","n":23,"type":"mcq","q":"According to the ________, to understand consumers' intentions we also need to measure the subjective norms that influence an individual's intention to act.","answer":0,"exp":"The theory of reasoned action adds normative beliefs and motivation to comply with norms, which together form subjective norms.","options":["theory of reasoned action model","trying-to-consume model","attitude-toward-object model","attitude-toward-behavior model","attitude-toward-the-ad model"]},{"id":"6-24","n":24,"type":"mcq","q":"________ involve both the beliefs that the consumer attributes to relevant others, such as friends and parents, and the consumer's motivation to comply with the beliefs held by those relevant others.","answer":1,"exp":"Subjective norms = normative beliefs (what relevant others think) + motivation to comply with those others.","options":["Niche markets","Subjective norms","Direct experiences","External attributions","Foot-in-the-door techniques"]},{"id":"6-25","n":25,"type":"mcq","q":"If an undergraduate student was considering getting a tattoo and stopped to ask herself what her parents would think of such behavior, such a reflection would constitute her ________.","answer":1,"exp":"Asking 'What would my parents think?' reflects normative beliefs and the motivation to comply, so it is her SUBJECTIVE NORM.","options":["internal attribution","subjective norm","direct experience","external attribution","niche"]},{"id":"6-26","n":26,"type":"mcq","q":"Which of the following are considered to be underlying factors that are likely to produce a subjective norm?","answer":1,"exp":"Chapter 6: the theory of reasoned action lists normative beliefs and motivation to comply with norms. Together they produce the subjective norm.","options":["conative predispositions and cognitions","normative beliefs and motivation to comply with relevant others","affective predispositions toward the behavior and motivation to comply with others","intrinsic and extrinsic attributions","intention and normative beliefs"]},{"id":"6-28","n":28,"type":"mcq","q":"The ________ is designed to account for cases in which the action or outcome is desired but not certain, and reflects the consumer's attempts to consume, whether or not they are successful.","answer":1,"exp":"The theory of trying to consume covers cases where the outcome is wanted but uncertain. It reflects attempts to consume, whether or not they succeed.","options":["theory of reasoned action model","trying-to-consume model","attitude-toward-object model","attitude-toward-behavior model","attitude-toward-the-ad model"]},{"id":"6-29","n":29,"type":"mcq","q":"In the theory of trying to consume, the consumer's attempts to consume may be a result of ________ or ________ impediments that prevent the desired action or outcome.","answer":1,"exp":"Chapter 6: in trying to consume, personal impediments and environmental impediments can block the outcome.","options":["personal; social","personal; environmental","social; environmental","physical; economic","economic; environmental"]},{"id":"6-30","n":30,"type":"mcq","q":"According to the attitude-toward-the-ad model, the consumer forms various feelings and judgments as a result of exposure to an ad. If the consumer likes the ad, ________.","answer":0,"exp":"Attitude toward the ad: attitudes toward a brand form from how consumers feel about its ads. If they like the ad, they are more likely to buy.","options":["he is more likely to buy the product","he is less likely to buy the product","he is less likely to encounter environmental impediments to consumption","his like or dislike has no relevance to his likelihood of purchasing the product","he is more likely to attribute satisfaction with the product internally"]},{"id":"6-31","n":31,"type":"mcq","q":"Chris notices the label for a popular brand of yogurt suggests the yogurt has more potassium than a banana. Chris had not previously associated the yogurt brand, or any other yogurt, with potassium. In this example, the marketer ________.","answer":2,"exp":"The brand gave yogurt a NEW attribute (potassium) that consumers had never linked to it. 'Add an attribute' is the first way in Chapter 6 to change attitudes with the attitude-toward-object model.","options":["changed the relative evaluation of an existing attribute","changed the importance of an existing attribute","added a new attribute","developed a favorable attitude toward the ad","used priming"]},{"id":"6-35","n":35,"type":"mcq","q":"Changing attitudes according to their applicable consumer motivations is known as the ________.","answer":1,"exp":"The functional approach changes attitudes by appealing to the motivational FUNCTION the attitude serves: utilitarian, ego-defensive, value-expressive or knowledge.","options":["favorable approach","functional approach","competitive approach","industrial approach","goodwill approach"],"depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 6, p. 156","page":156},{"id":"6-36","n":36,"type":"mcq","q":"Ben has a positive attitude toward Nova Hiking Gear because a pair of Nova hiking boots he owns have proven to be very durable and to provide good support during long hikes. Ben has formed this attitude based on the boots' ________.","answer":1,"exp":"Ben likes the boots because they are durable and supportive. That is usefulness, the UTILITARIAN function.","options":["ego-defensive function","utilitarian function","value-expressive function","knowledge function","intention function"],"depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 6, p. 156","page":156},{"id":"6-37","n":37,"type":"mcq","q":"An ad for dentures that tells consumers that they can feel secure, smile, and eat because their brand of denture cream will not let their dentures fall out is trying to attract customers by emphasizing the brand's ________.","answer":0,"exp":"Denture cream that helps you 'feel secure' and smile protects the self-image from embarrassment. That is the EGO-DEFENSIVE function.","options":["ego-defensive function","utilitarian function","value-expressive function","knowledge function","intention function"],"depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 6, p. 156","page":156},{"id":"6-38","n":38,"type":"mcq","q":"Ads for cosmetics and personal care products acknowledge the fact that people want to protect their self-images from inner feelings of doubt. This is consistent with which basic motivational functions?","answer":0,"exp":"Protecting the self-image from inner doubt is the EGO-DEFENSIVE function.","options":["the ego-defensive function","the knowledge function","the utilitarian function","the value-expressive function","the intention function"],"depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 6, p. 156","page":156},{"id":"6-39","n":39,"type":"mcq","q":"An ad for a new allergy medication includes a bar graph which demonstrates the product's superiority by contrasting its allergy symptom relief abilities with other allergy medications. This is consistent with which basic motivational functions?","answer":1,"exp":"A comparison bar chart gives clear information to people who want to understand and make sense of products. That is the KNOWLEDGE function.","options":["the ego-defensive function","the knowledge function","the utilitarian function","the value-expressive function","the intention function"],"depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 6, p. 156","page":156},{"id":"6-40","n":40,"type":"mcq","q":"If a consumer segment generally holds a positive attitude toward owning the latest designer jeans and values having the latest and greatest in designer jeans, then that segment's attitude toward new brands of designer jeans are likely to reflect that orientation. This is an example of the ________ of attitude.","answer":2,"exp":"The segment's attitude reflects what it values and how it wants to express itself (having the latest designer jeans). That is the VALUE-EXPRESSIVE function.","options":["ego-defensive function","utilitarian function","value-expressive function","knowledge function","intention function"],"depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 6, p. 156","page":156},{"id":"6-41","n":41,"type":"mcq","q":"An example of the ________ function of motivation is for Crest to point out how its new toothbrush is superior to all other toothbrushes in controlling gum disease by removing more plaque.","answer":1,"exp":"Crest gives consumers comparative information (removes more plaque than others). That is the KNOWLEDGE function.","options":["ego-defensive","knowledge","utilitarian","value-expressive","intention"],"depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 6, p. 156","page":156},{"id":"6-42","n":42,"type":"mcq","q":"Tom is looking to lose weight by cutting carbohydrates out of his diet, particularly from regular sodas, but has a negative attitude towards diet. Diet Coke promotes its association with the American Heart Association, which generates goodwill from Tom. What strategy did the company use?","answer":1,"exp":"Diet Coke linked itself to the American Heart Association. Chapter 6 lists this strategy: associating brands with worthy causes and events.","options":["changing the relative evaluation of attributes","associating the product with a special group, event or cause","changing the basic motivational function","changing consumer beliefs about competitor brands","changing consumers' subjective norms"]},{"id":"6-44","n":44,"type":"mcq","q":"The Elaboration Likelihood Model proposes that consumers' attitudes are changed by two distinctly different routes to persuasion: ________ routes and ________ routes.","answer":2,"exp":"The Elaboration Likelihood Model has two routes to persuasion: CENTRAL and PERIPHERAL.","options":["central; logical","main; secondary","central; peripheral","rational; emotional","direct; indirect"]},{"id":"6-45","n":45,"type":"mcq","q":"When consumers are willing to exert the effort to comprehend, learn, or evaluate the available information about the attitude object, learning and attitude change occur via the ________ to persuasion.","answer":0,"exp":"The central route means high involvement: considered thought and the effort to learn, comprehend and evaluate attribute information.","options":["central route","celebrity endorsement","peripheral route","functional approach","dual mediation model"]},{"id":"6-46","n":46,"type":"mcq","q":"When a consumer's motivation or assessment skills are low, learning and attitude tend to occur via the ________ to persuasion.","answer":4,"exp":"When motivation or ability is low (low involvement), persuasion goes through the PERIPHERAL route.","options":["central route","tri-component route","functional route","primary route","peripheral route"]},{"id":"6-48","n":48,"type":"mcq","q":"Central brand claims that are critical to the evaluation are most likely processed via the ________.","answer":0,"exp":"Critical central brand claims are processed through the central route, using attribute-based information and high-quality arguments.","options":["central route","tri-component route","functional route","primary route","peripheral route"]},{"id":"6-49","n":49,"type":"mcq","q":"Attitude change via the peripheral route may be an outcome of ________ like cents-off coupons, free samples, beautiful background scenery, great packaging, or a celebrity endorsement.","answer":3,"exp":"The peripheral route relies on cues rather than arguments: coupons, samples, scenery, packaging and celebrities. These are secondary (peripheral) inducements.","options":["core route","primary inducements","functional route","secondary inducements","passive route"],"book":"Textbook: central route = primary inducements (product claims, arguments). Peripheral route = secondary inducements (cents-off coupons, free samples, beautiful scenery, celebrity endorsers).","depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 6, p. 158","page":158},{"id":"6-50","n":50,"type":"mcq","q":"A popular pop singing sensation is a spokesperson for a cellular phone company. As a nonexpert celebrity endorser, her presence in communications about the cellular phone company's service is likely to be processed via the ________.","answer":4,"exp":"A non-expert celebrity is a peripheral cue processed with little thought, so this goes through the PERIPHERAL route.","options":["central route","tri-component route","functional route","primary route","peripheral route"]},{"id":"6-51","n":51,"type":"mcq","q":"According to ________, discomfort occurs when a consumer holds conflicting thoughts about a belief or an attitude object.","answer":1,"exp":"Cognitive dissonance is the discomfort a consumer feels when holding conflicting thoughts about a belief or an attitude object.","options":["attitude-change strategies","cognitive dissonance theory","attribution theory","self-perception theory","attitude research"]},{"id":"6-52","n":52,"type":"mcq","q":"Matthew recently purchased a new laptop for $1,500. He subsequently saw an advertisement for what appeared to be a similar model being sold for only $1,350. In order to resolve his ________, Matt decided that the cheaper model must not have as many attractive features as the model he purchased.","answer":3,"exp":"Matthew feels doubt AFTER buying and then rationalizes ('the cheaper one must have fewer features'). That is reducing post-purchase dissonance.","options":["indirect experience","attitude formation","subjective norm","post-purchase dissonance","situational influence"]},{"id":"6-53","n":53,"type":"mcq","q":"In order to reduce ________, consumers can rationalize their purchase decision as being wise, seek out advertisements that support their choice, try to \"sell\" friends on the positive features of the brand, or look to known satisfied owners for reassurance.","answer":0,"exp":"Chapter 6 lists the ways to reduce post-purchase dissonance: rationalize, seek supporting ads, 'sell' friends and seek reassurance from satisfied owners.","options":["post-purchase dissonance","emotionally charged states","defensive attribution","subjective norms","attitude formation"]},{"id":"6-54","n":54,"type":"mcq","q":"Jerry bought a new washing machine for his home, but he has some tension over whether he made the best decision. Which of the following is NOT a strategy Jerry might use to reduce postpurchase dissonance?","answer":3,"exp":"Chapter 6 says consumers seek ads that SUPPORT their choice and AVOID competitive ads. So looking for competitors' ads is NOT a dissonance-reducing strategy.","options":["rationalize his decision","\"sell\" friends on the positive features of the washing machine","look for satisfied owners of the washing machine","seek out ads for competitive products he did not choose","seek out ads for the washing machine he chose"]},{"id":"6-55","n":55,"type":"mcq","q":"________ attempts to explain how people assign blame or credit to events on the basis of either their own behavior or the behavior of others.","answer":2,"exp":"Attribution theory explains how people assign causality, meaning blame or credit, to events based on their own or others' behavior.","options":["Self-perception theory","Cognitive dissonance theory","Attribution theory","Buyer intention theory","Attitude theory"],"depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 6, p. 159","page":159},{"id":"6-56","n":56,"type":"mcq","q":"When a person says \"I contributed to the American Red Cross because it really helps people in need\" or \"He tried to persuade me to buy an LED rather than a 3D TV because he'd make a bigger commission,\" they are providing examples of ________ in action.","answer":2,"exp":"Both statements assign a cause to a behavior: 'because it helps people' and 'because he'd make a commission'. That is attribution theory in action.","options":["self-perception theory","cognitive dissonance theory","attribution theory","buyer intention theory","attitude theory"],"depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 6, p. 159","page":159},{"id":"6-57","n":57,"type":"mcq","q":"________ suggests that attitudes develop as consumers look at and make judgments about their own behavior.","answer":0,"exp":"Self-perception attribution: people form attitudes by looking at and judging their own behavior.","options":["Self-perception attributions","Cognitive dissonance theory","Attribution theory","Buyer intention theory","Attitude theory"],"depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 6, p. 160","page":160},{"id":"6-58","n":58,"type":"mcq","q":"If a student observes that she routinely purchases the Des Moines Register on her way to class, she is apt to conclude that she likes the Des Moines Register. This is an example of how the ________ helps make inferences about one's behavior.","answer":2,"exp":"'I buy it routinely, so I must like it' is inferring an attitude from one's own behavior. That is self-perception.","options":["elaboration likelihood model","attribution theory","self-perception theory","defensive attributions theory","formation theory"],"depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 6, p. 160","page":160},{"id":"6-59","n":59,"type":"mcq","q":"When consumers give themselves credit for the outcome of a behavior, they are engaging in ________.","answer":1,"exp":"Taking credit yourself is INTERNAL attribution.","options":["subjective attribution","internal attribution","objective attribution","defensive attribution","external attribution"]},{"id":"6-60","n":60,"type":"mcq","q":"According to the principle of ________, consumers are likely to accept credit personally for success and to attribute failure to others or to outside events.","answer":3,"exp":"Defensive attribution: people take credit for success (internal) and blame others or outside events for failure (external).","options":["subjective attribution","internal attribution","objective attribution","defensive attribution","external attribution"]},{"id":"6-61","n":61,"type":"mcq","q":"Someone who donates $10 to Amnesty International might be persuaded to donate a much larger amount when properly approached because that person may decide that he or she is the kind of person who makes such charitable donations. This demonstrates the basic premise of the ________.","answer":2,"exp":"Getting a small yes ($10) first, which makes the person see themselves as a donor so they agree to a bigger request, is the foot-in-the-door technique.","options":["door-in-the-face technique","consensus technique","foot-in-the-door technique","attribution technique","dual mediation technique"],"depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 6, p. 161","page":161},{"id":"6-62","n":62,"type":"mcq","q":"One of the criteria used by consumers to confirm their initial attributions about objects is ________, which means that the reaction (the prior inference) is perceived in the same way by other consumers.","answer":3,"exp":"Consensus: checking whether other people perceive the same thing ('Would my friends agree?').","options":["distinctiveness","consistency over time","consistency over modality","consensus","self-defense"]},{"id":"6-63","n":63,"type":"mcq","q":"The principle of ________ suggests people accept or take credit for success, but assign failure to others or outside events.","answer":1,"exp":"This is the definition of defensive attribution in Chapter 6.","options":["distinctiveness","defensive attribution","door-in-the-face","consensus","compliance"]},{"id":"6-64","n":64,"type":"mcq","q":"If an MBA alumnus considers donating to the program, but is concerned about whether he will become part of a select group if he donates, he is trying to seek reinforcement for his initial attributions via ________.","answer":0,"exp":"'Will I become part of a select group?' is the DISTINCTIVENESS question in Table 6.4.","options":["distinctiveness","consistency over time","consistency over modality","consensus","self-defense"]},{"id":"6-65","n":65,"type":"mcq","q":"Carl starts reconsidering his brand choice right after he buys a Subaru. This constitutes ________.","answer":4,"exp":"Reconsidering a brand choice right after buying it is post-purchase dissonance.","options":["buying intention","niche marketing","indirect experience","defensive attribution","post-purchase dissonance"]},{"id":"6-66","n":66,"type":"mcq","q":"Subaru portrays its station wagon as a car designed for young people who need extra cargo space for their active lives. This appeals to Carl because that is how he sees himself. Subaru is appealing to the ________ function to change attitudes toward station wagons.","answer":4,"exp":"Subaru shows the wagon as fitting how Carl sees himself (young and active). It appeals to his self-concept and values, which is the VALUE-EXPRESSIVE function.","options":["utilitarian","knowledge","ego-defensive","competitive","value-expressive"]},{"id":"6-76","n":76,"type":"tf","q":"Attitudes can be directly observed.","answer":false,"exp":"FALSE. An attitude is a learned PREDISPOSITION. It is internal and has to be inferred from what people say or do.","depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 6, p. 143","page":143},{"id":"6-77","n":77,"type":"tf","q":"Attitudes are learned from direct experience with the product, word-of-mouth information acquired from others, or exposure to mass-media advertising, the Internet, and various forms of direct marketing.","answer":true,"exp":"TRUE. Chapter 6 lists the sources of attitude formation: experience, family and friends, and media/Internet/social media."},{"id":"6-78","n":78,"type":"tf","q":"The affective component of the tri-component attitude model represents the consumer's emotions and feelings regarding the attitude object.","answer":true,"exp":"TRUE. The affective component is emotions and feelings about the product or brand."},{"id":"6-80","n":80,"type":"tf","q":"In marketing and consumer research, the conative component of the tri-component attitude model is frequently treated as an expression of the consumer's intention to buy.","answer":true,"exp":"TRUE. Chapter 6: the conative component is expressed as intention to purchase."},{"id":"6-82","n":82,"type":"tf","q":"Competitors often try to change the strong and positive attitudes consumers have toward market leaders in an attempt to get consumers to switch brands.","answer":true,"exp":"TRUE. 'Changing beliefs about competing brands' is a strategy challengers use against market leaders."},{"id":"6-83","n":83,"type":"tf","q":"The appeal of the attitude-toward-behavior model is that it allows researchers to understand consumers' subjective norms and the factors that form them.","answer":false,"exp":"FALSE. Subjective norms (normative beliefs and motivation to comply) belong to the THEORY OF REASONED ACTION, not the attitude-toward-behavior model."},{"id":"6-84","n":84,"type":"tf","q":"Consumers' attitudes toward a particular brand's advertising are independent of their beliefs about the brand and do not influence their attitudes toward the brand itself.","answer":false,"exp":"FALSE. The attitude-toward-the-ad model says feelings about a brand's ads DO shape attitudes toward the brand."},{"id":"6-85","n":85,"type":"tf","q":"If consumers like the ad they see, they are more likely to buy the product.","answer":true,"exp":"TRUE. Attitude toward the ad: liking the ad makes a purchase more likely."},{"id":"6-86","n":86,"type":"tf","q":"The purchase and consumption of a product are necessary for the formation of attitudes.","answer":false,"exp":"FALSE. Attitudes can also form from family and friends and from media and social media, without buying or using the product."},{"id":"6-87","n":87,"type":"tf","q":"In general, the more information consumers have about a product or service, the more likely they are to form attitudes about it, either positive or negative.","answer":true,"exp":"TRUE. Attitudes are learned from information sources. The more information consumers have, the more likely they are to form an attitude.","depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 6, p. 143","page":143},{"id":"6-88","n":88,"type":"tf","q":"The ego-defensive function maintains that people form attitudes in order to protect themselves from sensing doubt and to replace uncertainty with feelings of security and confidence.","answer":true,"exp":"TRUE. The ego-defensive function: attitudes protect people from self-doubt and give security and confidence.","depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 6, p. 156","page":156},{"id":"6-89","n":89,"type":"tf","q":"When an ad for Green Works from Clorox states the product cleans effectively and is environmentally friendly, unlike competing brands, it is using the utilitarian approach to attitude change.","answer":true,"exp":"TRUE. Green Works stresses what the product DOES (cleans well, eco-friendly). That is the UTILITARIAN function."},{"id":"6-90","n":90,"type":"tf","q":"The knowledge function of the functional approach to attitude change relies on the fact that most people want to protect their self-images from inner feelings of doubt.","answer":false,"exp":"FALSE. Protecting the self-image from doubt is the EGO-DEFENSIVE function. The knowledge function is the need to understand and organize information.","depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 6, p. 156","page":156},{"id":"6-91","n":91,"type":"tf","q":"Highly involved consumers use attribute-based information to evaluate brands, whereas less involved consumers apply simpler decision rules.","answer":true,"exp":"TRUE. The central route uses attribute-based information under high involvement. Low-involvement consumers take the peripheral route and process little."},{"id":"6-92","n":92,"type":"tf","q":"The elaboration likelihood model (ELM) proposes that attitudes can sometimes be changed by either one of two different routes to persuasion - a central route or a peripheral route.","answer":true,"exp":"TRUE. That is the definition of the ELM in Chapter 6."},{"id":"6-93","n":93,"type":"tf","q":"Central inducements have greater staying power over time than secondary inducements.","answer":true,"exp":"TRUE. Attitudes changed through the central route (arguments) last longer than those changed through peripheral cues.","depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 6, p. 158","page":158},{"id":"6-94","n":94,"type":"tf","q":"Because expensive and important purchases often require compromise when choosing among alternatives, post-purchase dissonance commonly occurs.","answer":true,"exp":"TRUE. Expensive, important choices involve compromise, so post-purchase dissonance is common."},{"id":"6-95","n":95,"type":"tf","q":"Marketers can help consumers relieve their dissonance by including messages in their advertising specifically aimed at reinforcing consumers' decisions by complimenting their wisdom, offering stronger guarantees or warranties, increasing the number and effectiveness of its services, or providing detailed brochures on how to use its products correctly.","answer":true,"exp":"TRUE. Marketers can help with the strategies in Chapter 6 by giving consumers reassurance: compliments on their choice, guarantees, services and usage guides.","depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 6, p. 159","page":159},{"id":"6-96","n":96,"type":"tf","q":"Jill was recently complemented on her piano playing skills. She attributed her skill to the quality and skill of her instructor. This is an example of defensive attribution.","answer":false,"exp":"FALSE. Defensive attribution means taking credit for SUCCESS. Jill gave the credit for her success to someone else, so this is not defensive attribution."},{"id":"6-97","n":97,"type":"tf","q":"Individuals who try a brand without any inducements or individuals who buy a brand repeatedly are more likely to consider that they buy the brand because they like it, rather than because it was free or on sale.","answer":true,"exp":"TRUE. Self-perception attribution: if you choose a brand without inducements, you conclude 'I buy it because I like it'.","depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 6, p. 160","page":160},{"id":"6-98","n":98,"type":"tf","q":"When cognitive dissonance occurs after the purchase it is called post-purchase dissonance.","answer":true,"exp":"TRUE. Chapter 6: dissonance that happens after purchase is post-purchase dissonance."},{"id":"6-99","n":99,"type":"tf","q":"If a first request for a $1,000 contribution to your university's alumni foundation is followed by a second, less costly request for $150, the person soliciting the funds may be using the doorin-the-face technique.","answer":true,"exp":"TRUE. Door-in-the-face: start with a big request that is likely to be refused, then follow with a smaller one.","depth":"book","ref":"Book Ch. 6, p. 162","page":162},{"id":"6-100","n":100,"type":"tf","q":"If an alumnus considering donating money to his or her MBA program asks how distinctive his contribution would be and whether he would become part of an elite group, he is concerned about consensus.","answer":false,"exp":"FALSE. Asking 'How distinctive will my contribution be?' is about DISTINCTIVENESS, not consensus."}],"book":{"first":142,"last":165,"count":24}}]};
